Object.assign(window.IND_FEED_BODY, {"hp-fk.lingo-song-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lingo-song:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · Lingo and the First Song","text":"सुबह होते-होते मैदान के किनारे हिरन आकर खड़े हो गए थे — जो हिरन कभी नहीं करते — और पूरा गाँव सुनने के लिए बाहर निकल आया था, और किसी ने बिना ध्यान दिए पैर थिरकाना शुरू कर दिया था — और बहुत-सी कहानियों में तो नाचने की शुरुआत ऐसे ही हुई थी।","lang":"hi","body":"By morning there were deer standing at the edge of the clearing, which deer do not do, and the whole village had come out to listen, and somebody had started tapping their foot without noticing they were doing it — which is how dancing got started, in a fair number of stories.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lingo-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lingo-song|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Tales from Every Corner"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lingo-song-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lingo-song:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · Lingo and the First Song","text":"\"अब इसे पकड़ो,\" लिंगो ने कहा, और उसे किसी और को थमा दिया। यही सबसे ज़रूरी बात है। उन्होंने उसे अपने पास नहीं रखा।","lang":"hi","body":"\"Now hold this,\" said Lingo, and gave it to somebody else. That is the important part. He did not keep it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lingo-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lingo-song|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Tales from Every Corner"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"उज्जैन के राजा विक्रमादित्य ने एक साधु को एक वचन दिया था, और वे जैसे राजा थे, उन्होंने पहले ज़्यादा सवाल नहीं पूछे थे। वचन यह था: अमावस्या की रात जंगल के छोर पर खड़े पुराने पेड़ के पास जाना, और उस पर लटके वेताल को लेकर लौटना।","lang":"hi","body":"King Vikramaditya of Ujjain had promised something to a monk, and being the sort of king he was, he had not asked enough questions first. The promise was this: go to the old tree at the edge of the wood on a night with no moon, and bring back the vetala that hangs in it.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"वेताल एक ऐसा प्रेत होता है जो किसी चीज़ में घुस जाए तो बाहर निकलने का नाम नहीं लेता, और यह वाला किसी विशाल सिमटे हुए चमगादड़ की तरह डालियों से उल्टा लटका हुआ था, और सालों से वहाँ मजे से रह रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"A vetala is a spirit that gets into things and will not get out of them, and this one hung upside down in the branches like an enormous folded bat, and had been enjoying itself there for years.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"\"तो फिर उतार लीजिए मुझे नीचे,\" बेताल ने चहकते हुए कहा। \"मैं तो खुद आपकी पीठ पर चढ़ जाऊँगा। बस एक नियम है। आप कुछ बोलेंगे नहीं। रास्ते में एक शब्द भी मुँह से निकाला, और मैं फुर्र से उड़कर अपने पेड़ पर वापस जा बैठूँगा, और आपको फिर से शुरुआत करनी पड़ेगी।\" विक्रम, जो कम बोलने वाले इंसान थे, उन्हें लगा कि यह तो बेहद आसान काम है।","lang":"hi","body":"\"Take me down then,\" said the vetala cheerfully. \"I shall even climb on your back. One rule. You may not speak. Say one word on the way and I fly straight back to my tree and you can start again.\" Vikram, who was a man of few words, thought this sounded extremely easy.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"पर यह काम बेहद आसान नहीं था। क्योंकि बेताल पूरे रास्ते बोलता ही रहा, और उसकी बातों में कहानियाँ थीं — और हर एक कहानी के अंत में एक फँसाने वाला सवाल आ खड़ा होता था। गलती किसकी थी? सबसे बहादुर कौन था? इनाम का असली हकदार कौन था?","lang":"hi","body":"It was not extremely easy. Because the vetala talked all the way, and what it talked about were stories — and every single story stopped at the end with a question that had a hook in it. Whose fault was it? Who was the bravest? Who had really earned the reward?","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"और विक्रम को जवाब मालूम होता था। वे राजा होने के साथ-साथ न्याय करने वाले भी थे, और उन्होंने अपनी पूरी ज़िंदगी दो लोगों को एक ही बात दो अलग-अलग तरीकों से कहते हुए सुना था। वे अपने सामने किसी गलत बात को यूँ ही अनसुना नहीं छोड़ सकते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"And Vikram knew. He was a judge as well as a king, and he had spent his whole life listening to two people tell him the same story two different ways, and he could not hear an unfair thing said out loud and let it stand.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"चौबीस बार विक्रम वापस पेड़ तक चलकर गए। चौबीस बार वे उस पर चढ़े, उस बला को नीचे उतारा, उसे अपनी पीठ पर लादा और चल पड़े। उन्होंने एक बार भी नहीं कहा: यह नामुमकिन है। उन्होंने एक बार भी नहीं कहा: अपनी कहानियाँ अपने पास ही रखो।","lang":"hi","body":"Twenty-four times Vikram walked back to the tree. Twenty-four times he climbed it, took the thing down, put it on his back and set off. He never once said: this is impossible. He never once said: keep your stories to yourself.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"पचीसवीं रात को बेताल ने आखिरी कहानी सुनाई, और आखिरी सवाल पूछा — और विक्रम ने अपना मुँह खोला, फिर बंद कर लिया, और आगे चलते रहे। उन्हें सचमुच इसका जवाब नहीं पता था। यही एकमात्र खामोशी थी जो बेताल उनसे हासिल कर पाया था, और वह एकदम सच्ची थी।","lang":"hi","body":"On the twenty-fifth night the vetala told the last story, and asked the last question — and Vikram opened his mouth, and shut it again, and walked on. He genuinely did not know the answer. It was the only silence the vetala had ever got out of him, and it was an honest one.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.vetala-tree-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.vetala-tree:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Thing in the Tree","text":"\"अरे वाह,\" जैसे-जैसे पेड़ छँटने लगे, बेताल ने उनकी पीठ पर आराम से लटकते हुए कहा। \"आप पहले इंसान हैं जिसे मैं न तो थका पाया, न चिढ़ा पाया, और न ही चकमा दे पाया। अब चूँकि हम पहुँचने ही वाले हैं — तो मैं आपको एक और बात बताता हूँ, और यह कोई पहेली नहीं है। यह आपके उस साधु के बारे में है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Well,\" said the vetala, hanging comfortably on his back as the trees thinned out. \"You are the first person I have not been able to bore, annoy or trick. Since we are nearly there — let me tell you one more thing, and this one is not a riddle. It is about that monk of yours.\"","art":"art/story/sm/wt-vetala-tree.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.vetala-tree|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"बनारस के राजा रोज़ सुबह अपने बाग़ में घोड़ों, नगाड़ों और सौ आदमियों के साथ हिरणों का शिकार करते थे। हिरणों के झुंड तब तक भागते जब तक गिर न पड़ते। रसोई की ज़रूरत से कहीं ज़्यादा हिरण तो भागमभाग में घायल हो जाते थे, और वह बाग़ रहने के लिए एक दुखभरी जगह बनता जा रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"The king of Benares hunted deer in his park every single morning, with horses and drums and a hundred men. The herds ran until they dropped. Far more deer were hurt in the running than were ever needed in the kitchen, and the park was becoming a miserable place to live.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"वहाँ दो झुंड थे और दो मुखिया थे। एक था निग्रोध, बरगदी हिरण, जिसका रंग सोने जैसा था। \"यह सब ऐसे नहीं चल सकता,\" उसने कहा। \"चलो राजा के पास चलते हैं और उनके सामने एक सौदा रखते हैं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"There were two herds and two leaders. One was Nigrodha, the Banyan Deer, whose coat was the colour of gold. \"This cannot go on,\" he said. \"Let us go to the king and offer him a bargain.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"सौदा यह था: न कोई शिकार, न कोई भागमभाग, न टाँगों का टूटना। हर सुबह एक हिरण के नाम की पर्ची निकलेगी और वह खुद चलकर रसोई तक जाएगा। राजा को यह इंतज़ाम बहुत समझदारी भरा लगा और वे तुरंत मान गए।","lang":"hi","body":"The bargain was this: no more hunts, no more running, no more broken legs. Each morning one deer would draw the short lot and walk to the kitchen on its own. The king thought it a very sensible arrangement and agreed at once.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"और लंबे समय तक रोज़ एक-एक करके ऐसा ही चलता रहा। फिर एक सुबह दूसरे झुंड की एक ऐसी हिरणी के नाम की पर्ची निकली, जो उसी हफ़्ते बच्चे को जन्म देने वाली थी। वह अपने मुखिया के पास गई और पूछा कि क्या उसकी बारी बच्चे के जन्म के बाद आ सकती है। उसने कहा कि पर्ची तो पर्ची होती है, और अपना मुँह फेर लिया।","lang":"hi","body":"And so it went, one a day, for a long time. Until one morning the lot fell on a doe of the other herd, who was going to have her fawn within the week. She went to her own leader and asked whether her turn might come after the birth. He said the lot was the lot, and turned away.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"इसलिए वह दूसरे झुंड के सुनहरे हिरण के पास गई, उसके सामने जाकर खड़ी हो गई, और उससे कहा कि वह उसकी जगह किसी और को भेज दे।","lang":"hi","body":"So she went and found the golden deer of the other herd, and stood in front of him, and asked him to send somebody in her place.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"रसोइया अपनी छुरी लेकर बाहर आया और उसके हाथ से छुरी छूट गई, और वह राजा को बुलाने के लिए दौड़ा। राजा खुद भी भागता हुआ आया और उसने देखा कि सुनहरा हिरण वध-शिला पर सिर रखे चुपचाप खड़ा था। \"इस बाग में तुम अकेले ऐसे हिरण हो जिसे मैंने कभी न छूने का वचन दिया था। तुम यहाँ क्यों आए हो?\"","lang":"hi","body":"The cook came out with his knife and dropped it, and ran for the king. The king came at a run himself and found the golden deer standing quietly with his head on the block. \"You are the one deer in this park I promised never to touch. Why are you here?\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"\"क्योंकि पर्ची एक माँ के नाम निकली थी,\" निग्रोध ने कहा। \"और मुझे लगा कि मैं उसकी जगह किसी और को जाने का हुक्म नहीं दे सकता। अगर मैं उन्हें भेज दूँ, तो वे मेरे अपने नहीं हैं। वे तो बस सामान हैं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Because a mother drew the lot,\" said Nigrodha. \"And I found that I could not order anybody else to take her place. If I can send them, they are not mine. They are just supplies.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.banyan-deer-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.banyan-deer:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Deer Who Would Not Send Anyone Else","text":"राजा थोड़ी देर चुप रहा। \"उठ जाओ,\" उसने कहा। \"तुम्हारा जीवन तुम्हारा ही है।\" \"और उसका?\" \"उसका भी।\" \"और बाकी झुंड का?\" \"हाँ।\" \"और बाग के बाहर के हिरणों का? और पक्षियों का? और नदी की मछलियों का?\" राजा हँस पड़ा, और मान गया, और मान गया, और मान गया।","lang":"hi","body":"The king was quiet for a while. \"Get up,\" he said. \"Your life is yours.\" \"And hers?\" \"Hers too.\" \"And the rest of the herd?\" \"Yes.\" \"And the deer outside the park? And the birds? And the fish in the river?\" The king laughed, and gave in, and gave in, and gave in.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-banyan-deer.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.banyan-deer|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.hare-moon-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.hare-moon:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Hare in the Moon","text":"एक नदी के किनारे चार दोस्त रहते थे: एक बंदर, एक सियार, एक ऊदबिलाव और एक ख़रगोश। ख़रगोश वह था जो सोच-विचार करता रहता था। एक शाम, जब पूर्णिमा आने वाली थी, उसने कहा: \"कल दान देने का दिन है। अगर कोई भूखा इधर से गुज़रे, तो हम उसे खाना खिलाएँगे। मंज़ूर?\" सब मान गए, उतनी ही आसानी से जितनी आसानी से लोग एक रात पहले किसी भी बात पर राज़ी हो जाते हैं।","lang":"hi","body":"Four friends lived by a river: a monkey, a jackal, an otter, and a hare. The hare was the one who thought about things. One evening, with the full moon coming, he said: \"Tomorrow is a day for giving. If anybody comes past hungry, we feed them. Agreed?\" Everybody agreed, in the easy way you agree to things the night before.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-hare-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.hare-moon|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.hare-moon-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.hare-moon:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Hare in the Moon","text":"सुबह बंदर बाहर गया और आम लेकर लौटा, जो उसके लिए बहुत आसान था। सियार को खेत के किनारे किसी की छोड़ी हुई दही की एक मटकी मिल गई। उदबिलाव को उथले पानी में मछलियाँ मिल गईं। तीनों बड़े खुश होकर बैठ गए।","lang":"hi","body":"In the morning the monkey went out and came back with mangoes, which was easy for him. The jackal found a pot of curds somebody had left at the edge of a field. The otter found fish in the shallows. All three of them sat down feeling extremely pleased.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-hare-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.hare-moon|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.hare-moon-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.hare-moon:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Hare in the Moon","text":"खरगोश खाना ढूँढ़ने निकला और घास लेकर लौटा। वह काफी देर तक घास के अपने छोटे से ढेर को देखता रहा। उसने कहा, \"कभी किसी ने किसी अजनबी के पास जाकर उसे मुँह भर घास तो नहीं दी होगी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The hare went out to gather food and came back with grass. He looked at his little heap of grass for a long time. \"Nobody,\" he said, \"has ever walked up to a stranger and offered them a mouthful of grass.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-hare-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.hare-moon|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.hare-moon-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.hare-moon:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Hare in the Moon","text":"तभी सड़क पर एक बूढ़े यात्री आए, दुबले-पतले और धूल से सने हुए, और एक पत्थर पर बैठ गए। उन्होंने कहा, \"मैंने कुछ नहीं खाया है। क्या तुम्हारे पास कुछ है?\" बंदर अपने आम ले आया। उदबिलाव मछलियाँ ले आया। सियार दही ले आया। और खरगोश अपनी घास लिए वहीं खड़ा रहा।","lang":"hi","body":"And then an old traveller came up the road, thin and dusty, and sat down on a stone. \"I have not eaten,\" he said. \"Have you anything?\" The monkey brought his mangoes. The otter brought the fish. The jackal brought the curds. And the hare stood there with his grass.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-hare-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.hare-moon|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.hare-moon-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.hare-moon:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Hare in the Moon","text":"यात्री ने लकड़ियाँ इकट्ठी कीं और आग सुलगा दी। और खरगोश ने कूदने से पहले खुद को तीन बार झाड़ा — बहुत संभालकर — ताकि उसके रोएँ में छुपा कोई भी छोटा कीड़ा नीचे गिर जाए और उसे कोई चोट न पहुँचे। फिर वह सीधे आग में कूद पड़ा।","lang":"hi","body":"The traveller gathered sticks and lit them. And the hare, before he jumped, shook himself three times — very carefully — so that any small insect hiding in his fur would fall out and not be hurt. Then he jumped straight in.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-hare-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.hare-moon|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.hare-moon-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.hare-moon:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Hare in the Moon","text":"और आग तो ठंडी थी। नदी के पानी जैसी ठंडी। खरगोश लपटों के बीच बिल्कुल आराम से और पूरी तरह हैरान होकर बैठा रहा, और उसने ऊपर देखा — तो वह बूढ़ा यात्री कोई बूढ़ा यात्री था ही नहीं, और मुस्कुरा रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"And the fire was cold. Cold as river water. The hare sat in the middle of the flames feeling perfectly comfortable and extremely confused, and looked up — and the old traveller was not an old traveller at all, and was smiling.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-hare-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.hare-moon|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-jt.hare-moon-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:jt.hare-moon:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Hare in the Moon","text":"उन्होंने कहा, \"मैं यह देखने आया था कि तुम्हारा इरादा सच्चा है या नहीं। तुम्हारा इरादा सच्चा था। अब यह बात सबको पता चलेगी।\" और उन्होंने हाथ बढ़ाकर चाँद के चेहरे पर खरगोश की आकृति बना दी, ताकि वह आज रात भी वहीं दिखाई दे।","lang":"hi","body":"\"I came to find out whether you meant it,\" he said. \"You meant it. Now everybody is going to know.\" And he reached up and drew the shape of the hare on the face of the moon, so that it would still be there tonight.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-hare-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.hare-moon|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Jataka Tales"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"\"पाँचों पांडव भाई किसी भी राज्य से बहुत दूर, जंगल में रह रहे थे। एक बहुत गर्म दिन ढल रहा था और उनका पानी खत्म हो चुका था। नकुल, जो सबसे तेज़ थे, पानी ढूँढने निकले और उन्हें एक ऐसी शांत झील मिली जो बिल्कुल फ़र्श जैसी लग रही थी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The five Pandava brothers were living in the forest, a long way from any kingdom, and it was the end of a very hot day and they had run out of water. Nakula, the quickest, went off to look and found a lake so still it looked like a floor.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"\"जैसे ही वे घुटनों के बल झुके, पानी से एक आवाज़ आई। \"यह झील मेरी है। मेरे सवालों के जवाब दो, फिर पानी पीना।\" नकुल बहुत प्यासे थे और उन्होंने बोलने वाली झील की बात पर कोई ध्यान नहीं दिया। उन्होंने पानी पिया — और किनारे पर ऐसे गिर पड़े जैसे किसी ने उनका स्विच ही बंद कर दिया हो।\"","lang":"hi","body":"As he knelt down a voice came out of the water. \"This lake is mine. Answer my questions, then drink.\" Nakula was extremely thirsty and thought very little of talking lakes. He drank — and fell down beside it as though he had been switched off.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"\"सहदेव उन्हें ढूँढने गए, और उनके साथ भी वही हुआ। फिर दुनिया के सबसे बड़े धनुर्धर अर्जुन गए, जिन्होंने पहले पानी में एक तीर चलाया और फिर भी पानी पी लिया, और वे भी गिर पड़े। फिर भीम गए, जो दुनिया के सबसे ताक़तवर इंसान थे, पर इससे भी कोई मदद नहीं मिली।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Sahadeva went to look for him, and the same. Then Arjuna, the greatest archer alive, who shot an arrow into the water first and then drank anyway, and fell. Then Bhima, who was the strongest man in the world, which turned out not to help either.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"\"युधिष्ठिर सबसे आखिर में आए और उन्होंने अपने चारों भाइयों को बिना किसी चोट के, गिरे हुए पेड़ों की तरह पानी के किनारे पड़ा पाया। वे सीधे खड़े रहे, और उन्होंने पानी नहीं पिया। \"पूछिए,\" उन्होंने कहा।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Yudhishthira came last and found his four brothers lying by the water like fallen trees, with no mark on them. He stood up, and he did not drink. \"Ask,\" he said.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"\"धरती से भी भारी क्या है?\" — \"माँ।\" \"आसमान से भी ऊँचा क्या है?\" — \"पिता।\" \"हवा से भी तेज़ क्या है?\" — \"मन।\" \"और पूरी दुनिया में सबसे अनोखी बात क्या है?\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"What is heavier than the earth?\" — \"A mother.\" \"What is higher than the sky?\" — \"A father.\" \"What is faster than the wind?\" — \"The mind.\" \"And what is the most amazing thing in the whole world?\"","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"\"यही कि हर रोज़,\" युधिष्ठिर ने कहा, \"लोग दूसरों को मरते देखते हैं। और फिर भी हर कोई ऐसे जीता चला जाता है मानो उसके साथ ऐसा कभी होगा ही नहीं। इससे अनोखी बात दुनिया में कहीं नहीं है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"That every day,\" said Yudhishthira, \"people see others die. And every single one of them goes on living as though it will never happen to them. There is nothing stranger than that anywhere.\"","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"वह आवाज़ पल भर के लिए शांत हो गई। \"तुमने हर सवाल का जवाब दे दिया है। तो यह रहा तुम्हारा इनाम: मैं तुम्हारे भाइयों में से किसी एक को जगा दूँगा। सिर्फ़ एक को। तुम चुनो।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The voice was quiet for a moment. \"You have answered everything. So here is your prize: I will wake up one of your brothers. Only one. You choose.\"","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.yaksha-lake-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.yaksha-lake:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Questions at the Lake","text":"\"नकुल,\" युधिष्ठिर ने कहा। \"मेरे पिता की दो पत्नियाँ थीं। मेरी माँ के तीन बेटे यहाँ पड़े हैं। उनकी माँ के दो हैं। अगर सिर्फ़ एक ही को उठना है, तो वह उनकी माँ का बेटा हो, ताकि दोनों में से किसी भी माँ की झोली खाली न रहे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Nakula,\" said Yudhishthira. \"My father had two wives. My mother has three sons lying here. His mother has two. If only one may get up, let it be one of hers, so that neither mother is left with nothing.\"","art":"art/story/sm/ep-yaksha-lake.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.yaksha-lake|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"हिमालय की ऊँचाइयों में, पांडवों के पड़ाव के पास से बहती धारा में तैरता हुआ एक फूल आया — सौगंधिका, बहुत बड़ा और हलके रंग का, और उसकी महक जैसी महक दुनिया में किसी चीज़ की नहीं थी। द्रौपदी ने उसे पानी से निकाला और कहा कि उन्हें ऐसे कुछ और फूल चाहिए। इससे पहले कि कोई कह पाता कि इसकी कोई जल्दी नहीं है, भीम रास्ते पर निकल भी पड़े थे।","lang":"hi","body":"High in the Himalaya, a flower came floating down a stream past the Pandavas' camp — a saugandhika, huge and pale, and it smelled like nothing else in the world. Draupadi picked it out of the water and said she would like some more. Bhima was up the path before anybody could suggest that this was not urgent.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"भीम दुनिया के सबसे ताकतवर इंसान थे और यह बात वह बखूबी जानते थे। वह पहाड़ पर सीधे रास्ते से चढ़ने लगे—जंगल के चक्कर लगाने के बजाय सीधे उसके बीच से, रास्ते में आने वाले पेड़ों को तोड़ते-मरोड़ते और ऐसी दहाड़ मारते हुए कि हिरण और हाथी दूर भाग जाएं। सच कहें तो, एक फूल के लिए यह बहुत ज़्यादा ही शोर-शराबा था।","lang":"hi","body":"Bhima was the strongest man alive and knew it. He went up the mountain in a straight line, through the forest rather than round it, snapping trees out of his way and roaring so that the deer and the elephants got well clear. It was, frankly, a lot of noise for a flower.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"फिर दो चट्टानों के बीच रास्ता संकरा हो गया, और धूप में ठीक रास्ते के आर-पार एक बेहद बूढ़ा बंदर लेटा हुआ था—दुबला-पतला, मुंह के चारों तरफ सफेद बाल, और देखने में ऐसा जैसे सो रहा हो। उसकी पूंछ रास्ते पर किसी गिरी हुई रस्सी की तरह पड़ी थी।","lang":"hi","body":"Then the path narrowed between two rocks, and lying right across it in the sun was an extremely old monkey, thin, grey round the muzzle, apparently asleep. His tail lay across the path like a fallen rope.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"\"रास्ते से हटिए, बाबा,\" भीम ने कहा। \"अपनी पूंछ हटाइए।\" बूढ़े बंदर ने एक आंख खोली। \"हटा तो देता, बेटा, लेकिन मैं बहुत बूढ़ा हूं और यह बहुत भारी है। तुम खुद ही इसे हटा लो। बस उठाकर एक तरफ रख दो—जरा आराम से, ठीक है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Out of the way, grandfather,\" said Bhima. \"Move your tail.\" The old monkey opened one eye. \"I would, son, but I am very old and it is very heavy. Move it yourself. Just lift it aside — gently, mind.\"","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"भीम ने एक हाथ नीचे बढ़ाया—वही हाथ जिससे वह पेड़ों को उठा लिया करते थे—और पूंछ को झटककर रास्ते से हटाने के लिए पकड़ा। वह टस से मस नहीं हुई। उन्होंने दोनों हाथों का इस्तेमाल किया। कुछ नहीं हुआ। उन्होंने चट्टानों पर अपने पैर जमाए और अपनी पूरी जान लगाकर खींचा, यहां तक कि वह कांपने लगे और उनकी ठुड्डी से पसीना बहने लगा—पर पूंछ बिल्कुल वहीं की वहीं पड़ी रही।","lang":"hi","body":"Bhima reached down with one hand, the hand that lifted trees, and took hold of the tail to flick it out of the way. It did not move. He used both hands. Nothing. He braced his feet on the rocks and pulled with everything he had, until he was shaking and the sweat ran off his chin — and the tail lay exactly where it had been lying.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"\"मुझे क्षमा करें,\" भीम ने कहा, और इस बार उनकी आवाज़ वैसी नहीं थी जैसी आमतौर पर हुआ करती थी। \"दुनिया में ऐसा कोई बंदर नहीं जिसकी पूंछ मैं न उठा सकूं। आप कौन हैं?\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Forgive me,\" said Bhima, and he did not say it the way he usually said things. \"No monkey alive has a tail I cannot lift. Who are you?\"","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"\"वह, जिसने कभी एक समुद्र पार किया था,\" बूढ़े बंदर ने उठकर बैठते हुए कहा। \"और जिसे बताया गया है कि वह तुम्हारा भाई है—हम दोनों के पिता एक ही हैं, पवन देव। मैंने तुम्हें कोई चार मील दूर से आते हुए सुन लिया था, और मैंने सोचा कि इससे पहले तुम इस पहाड़ के हर जानवर को डरा दो, तुम्हारी रफ्तार थोड़ी धीमी कर दूं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Somebody who crossed a sea once,\" said the old monkey, sitting up. \"And who is, I am told, your brother — we have the same father, the wind. I heard you coming from about four miles away, and I thought I would slow you down before you frightened every animal on this mountain.\"","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-ep.bhima-hanuman-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:ep.bhima-hanuman:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Old Monkey on the Path","text":"हनुमान जी ने उन्हें बताया कि सौगंधिक फूल कहाँ खिलते हैं, और उस सरोवर के पहरेदारों पर चिल्लाने के बजाय उनसे प्यार से कैसे मांगना है। और फिर उन्होंने कहा: \"जब तुम्हारे परिवार को सच में मेरी ज़रूरत होगी, एक दिन, मैं वहाँ ज़रूर आऊँगा।\" और वे आए भी।","lang":"hi","body":"Hanuman told him where the saugandhika flowers grew, and how to ask the guardians of that pool politely instead of shouting at them. And then he said: when your family really needs me, one day, I shall be there. And he was.","art":"art/story/sm/ep-bhima-hanuman.jpg","route":"#/story/ep.bhima-hanuman|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","More from the Epics"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"यह केरल की सबसे पुरानी कहानियों में से एक है, और एक बार जब आप इसे सुन लेंगे, तो वहाँ जहाँ भी नज़र डालेंगे, आपको यही कहानी दिखाई देगी। इसकी शुरुआत वररुचि नाम के एक विद्वान से होती है, जो सब कुछ जानने के लिए दूर-दूर तक मशहूर थे — और जिन्हें बस अभी-अभी यह शक होना शुरू हुआ था कि वे सब कुछ नहीं जानते।","lang":"hi","body":"This is one of the oldest stories Kerala tells about itself, and once you have heard it you will see it everywhere you look there. It begins with a scholar called Vararuchi, who was famous across the land for knowing everything — and who was just beginning to suspect that he did not.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"एक दिन नदी किनारे उनकी मुलाक़ात एक युवती से हुई, जिसने कपड़े धोना रोके बिना ही एक ऐसी पहेली का जवाब दे दिया जिसे उनका कोई भी जानने वाला विद्वान हल नहीं कर पाया था। वे अगले दिन उससे भी कठिन पहेली लेकर आए। उसने उसका भी जवाब दे दिया, और अपनी तरफ़ से उससे भी बढ़िया एक पहेली पूछ ली। उन्होंने उससे शादी कर ली, जो उनकी ज़िंदगी का पहला पूरी तरह समझदारी भरा काम था।","lang":"hi","body":"By a river one day he met a young woman who answered, without even putting down the washing, a riddle that had beaten every scholar he knew. He came back the next day with a harder one. She answered that too, and added a better one of her own. He married her, which was the first fully sensible thing he had ever done.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"वे दोनों साथ-साथ सफ़र पर निकल पड़े, जैसा उस दौर में घुमक्कड़ विद्वान किया करते थे, और सालों के दौरान उनके बारह बच्चे हुए — हर एक रास्ते की अलग-अलग जगह पर।","lang":"hi","body":"They took to the road together, the way wandering scholars did, and over the years twelve children were born to them — each one in a different place along the way.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"और यहाँ कहानी एक बड़ा कठोर मोड़ लेती है, और वह जानती है कि यह बहुत कठिन है। हर बार जब कोई बच्चा पैदा होता, वररुचि अपनी पत्नी से एक ही सवाल पूछते: \"क्या बच्चे का मुँह है?\" \"हाँ, बिल्कुल,\" वह कहती। \"तो जिसने मुँह दिया है, वही दाना-पानी भी देगा,\" वे कहते। \"बच्चे को धीरे से नीचे लिटा दो, और आगे बढ़ो।\" और टूटते दिल से, ख़ुद से भी बड़ी किसी शक्ति पर भरोसा करते हुए, उसने वैसा ही किया।","lang":"hi","body":"And here the story does a hard thing, and it knows it is hard. Each time a baby came, Vararuchi asked his wife one question: \"Does the child have a mouth?\" \"Of course,\" she said. \"Then the One who gave the mouth will send the food,\" he said. \"Lay the child down gently, and walk on.\" And, trusting something bigger than her own breaking heart, she did.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"केरल की पगडंडी के किनारे कपड़े में लिपटा पड़ा एक नन्हा बच्चा, जहाँ हर कुछ मिनटों में कोई न कोई गुज़रता है। कहानी अच्छी तरह जानती है कि वह क्या कर रही है।","lang":"hi","body":"A baby, lying wrapped by the side of a path in Kerala, where somebody passes every few minutes. The story knows exactly what it is doing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"एक बच्चा बढ़ई के घर पला-बढ़ा और पेरुंथचन बना, इस धरती का सबसे महान शिल्पकार जिसकी चर्चा हमेशा हुई। एक ढोल बजाने वालों के परिवार में बड़ा हुआ और उनकी थाप को आगे ले गया। एक टोकरी बुनने वालों के यहाँ पला और पक्कनार बना, जिसकी सीधी-सादी बातें किसी भी घमंडी इंसान के बढ़ते क़दम रोक सकती थीं। एक विद्वान के घर में बड़ा हुआ और पवित्र अग्नि की सेवा करने लगा।","lang":"hi","body":"One was raised in a carpenter's house and became Perunthachan, the greatest builder the land ever talked about. One grew up with a family of drummers and carried their beat. One was raised by basket weavers and became Pakkanar, whose plain words could stop a proud man mid-stride. One grew up in a scholar's house tending the sacred fire.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"एक, जिसका नाम उप्पुकोट्टन था, नमक के एक व्यापारी के घर पला-बढ़ा, जहाँ परिवार वैसे ही नमाज़ पढ़ता था जैसे मुसलमान पढ़ते हैं, और वह उसी धरम-ईमान में बड़ा हुआ और देश की हर राह पर नमक ले गया। एक ऐसा भी था जो कभी किसी घर में नहीं टिका — तुम उससे अगली कहानी में मिलोगे, पहाड़ी पर एक भारी पत्थर ढकेलते हुए। बारह घर, बारह काम-धंधे, प्रार्थना करने का एक से ज़्यादा तरीका। माँ सिर्फ़ एक।","lang":"hi","body":"One, called Uppukottan, was raised in a salt trader's house where the family prayed as Muslims pray, and he grew up in that faith and carried salt down every road in the country. One never settled in any house at all — you will meet him in the next story, pushing a boulder up a hill. Twelve homes, twelve trades, more than one way of praying. One mother.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"और कहानी कहती है कि साल में एक बार, अपनी माँ के दावत वाले दिन, वे बारहों एक ही छत के नीचे इकट्ठे होते थे — ढोल बजाने वाला और आग की रखवाली करने वाला, बढ़ई और नमक का व्यापारी, हर कोई वही लाता जो उसके अपने घर में बनता था, हर कोई अपने घर के तरीके से खाता था, एक ही समय पर, एक ही आँगन में।","lang":"hi","body":"And once a year, the story says, all twelve came together under one roof for their mother's feast day — the drummer and the fire keeper, the carpenter and the salt trader, each bringing what his own house made, each eating in the way of his own home, at the same time, in the same courtyard.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.panthirukulam-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.panthirukulam:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · Twelve Houses, One Mother","text":"पड़ोसी तो हैरान-परेशान हो गए, ज़ाहिर है। इतने अलग-अलग लोग, एक ही दावत में! और कहानी बस पड़ोसियों को देखती है, और इंतज़ार करती है, जब तक कि वे समझ न जाएँ: माँ तो एक ही है।","lang":"hi","body":"The neighbours were scandalised, of course. Such different people, at one feast! And the story just looks at the neighbours, and waits, until they work it out: same mother.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"तुम्हें एक ही माँ के वे बारह बच्चे याद हैं? यह वही है जो कभी कहीं नहीं टिका। उसका नाम था नारनाथ, और पूरा केरल उसे नारनाथ भ्रांथन कहता था — यानी पागल नारनाथ — और केरल सैकड़ों सालों से इस बात पर बहस कर रहा है कि क्या यह नाम सही था।","lang":"hi","body":"You remember the twelve children of one mother? This is the one who never settled anywhere. His name was Naranath, and all of Kerala called him Naranath Branthan — Naranath the mad one — and Kerala has been arguing for hundreds of years about whether that name was fair.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"उसके दिन कुछ ऐसे गुज़रते थे। सुबह होते ही वह रायिरनेल्लूर की पहाड़ी की तलहटी में एक विशाल पत्थर पर अपना कंधा टिकाता, और धकेलता। पूरी सुबह। पूरी दोपहर। कराहते हुए, पसीने से तरबतर, इंच-दर-इंच, चोटी तक।","lang":"hi","body":"His days went like this. At dawn he put his shoulder to an enormous boulder at the bottom of the hill at Rayiranellur, and he pushed. All morning. All afternoon. Grunting, sweating, inch by inch, all the way up.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"और चोटी पर, दिन की आखिरी रोशनी में, वह ज़रा पीछे हटा, अपने हाथ झाड़े — और उसे छोड़ दिया। वह भारी पत्थर गड़गड़ाता और उछलता हुआ वापस ठीक उसी जगह जा पहुँचा, जहाँ से शुरू हुआ था। और नारनथ ने अपना सिर पीछे किया और इतनी ज़ोर से हँसा कि हँसते-हँसते उसे बैठना ही पड़ गया।","lang":"hi","body":"And at the top, in the last light, he stood back, dusted his hands — and let it go. The boulder went thundering and bouncing all the way back down to exactly where it had started. And Naranath threw back his head and laughed until he had to sit down.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"\"पागल है,\" गाँव वालों ने बड़े आराम से कहा, और अपने-अपने रात के खाने में लग गए। लेकिन बच्चे बड़ों से ज़्यादा बहादुर थे, जैसे बच्चे होते ही हैं, और एक शाम उनमें से कुछ ऊपर चढ़ आए और सीधे उससे पूछ ही लिया: \"जब आपकी पूरे दिन की मेहनत लुढ़क कर नीचे चली जाती है, तो आप हँसते क्यों हैं?\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Mad,\" said the villagers, comfortably, and went back to their dinners. But the children were braver than the grown-ups, the way children are, and one evening a few of them climbed up and asked him straight out: \"Why do you laugh when your whole day's work rolls away?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"\"घाटी में हर कोई दिन भर किसी न किसी चीज़ को पहाड़ पर ऊपर धकेलता रहता है,\" उसने अपनी आँखें पोंछते हुए बच्चों से कहा। \"पैसे को पहाड़ के ऊपर चढ़ाओ, और वह नीचे लुढ़क जाता है। शोहरत को ऊपर चढ़ाओ, और वह नीचे लुढ़क जाती है। वे हर शाम इसी बात पर रोते हैं। सिर्फ़ मैं ही अकेला था जो जानता था कि यह लुढ़केगा — इसलिए सिर्फ़ मैं ही हँस रहा हूँ।\" और उसने उस भारी पत्थर को किसी बूढ़े बैल की तरह थपथपाया।","lang":"hi","body":"\"Everyone in the valley pushes something uphill all day,\" he told the children, wiping his eyes. \"Money up the hill, and it rolls down. Fame up the hill, and it rolls down. They cry about it every evening. I am the only one who knew it would roll — so I am the only one laughing.\" And he patted the boulder like an old bullock.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"कहानी कहती है कि एक रात, वह एक ऐसी जगह सोने के लिए लेट गया जहाँ लोग अँधेरा होने के बाद जाने से बचते थे। आधी रात को, जैसा कि पुरानी कथाओं में होता है, देवी भद्रकाली अपने शोर मचाने वाले साथियों के साथ वहाँ से गुज़रीं, और उन्होंने देखा कि एक आदमी वहाँ बिल्कुल बेपरवाह, चैन से लेटा हुआ है। उन्हें बड़ा अचरज हुआ। आज तक कोई भी इतना बेपरवाह नहीं रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"One night, the story says, he settled down to sleep in a place people avoided after dark. In the middle of the night the great goddess Bhadrakali came through with her noisy companions, as she does in the old tales, and found a man calmly lying there, entirely unbothered. She was intrigued. Nobody was ever unbothered.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"\"मुझसे कुछ माँगो,\" देवी ने कहा, जिन्हें वह काफ़ी पसंद आया था। नारनथ ने ज़रा सोचा। उसने पूछा कि उसका जीवन कब समाप्त होगा, और देवी ने बता दिया, क्योंकि उसने यह बात इतने शांत मन से पूछी थी। \"क्या मुझे एक दिन और मिल सकता है?\" नहीं, उन्होंने कहा — यह नहीं दिया जा सकता। \"तो फिर एक दिन कम?\" वह भी नहीं। \"तो फिर, अम्मा,\" नारनथ ने कहा, \"जो वरदान मुझे सचमुच चाहिए, वह कोई नहीं दे सकता।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Ask me for something,\" said the goddess, who rather liked him. Naranath thought about it. He asked when his life would end, and she told him, because he asked so calmly. \"Could I have one day more?\" No, she said — that is not given. \"One day less, then?\" Not that either. \"Then, Amma,\" said Naranath, \"the boons I actually need, nobody can give.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"देवी ने ज़िद की — वरदान तो वरदान होता है। इसलिए नारनथ ने नीचे अपने पैरों की तरफ़ देखा। बचपन से ही उसका एक पैर सूजा हुआ था, और उसमें दर्द रहता था। \"तो फिर यह सूजन,\" उसने कहा, \"मेरे बाएँ पैर से हटाकर दाएँ पैर में कर दीजिए। ज़रा बदलाव के लिए।\" और उन्होंने वैसा ही कर दिया। और नारनथ उस पत्थर को देखकर जितना हँसा था, उससे भी कहीं ज़्यादा ज़ोर से हँसा — और कहानी कसम खाकर कहती है कि देवी भी उसके साथ हँस पड़ीं।","lang":"hi","body":"The goddess insisted — a boon is a boon. So Naranath looked down at his legs. One had been swollen since he was young, and ached. \"Then move the swelling,\" he said, \"from my left leg to my right. For a change.\" And she did. And Naranath laughed harder than he had ever laughed at the boulder — and the goddess, the story swears, laughed with him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.naranath-boulder-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.naranath-boulder:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","text":"वह ज़िंदगी भर इस धरती पर चलता रहा—न कुछ चाहा, न किसी की देनदारी रखी, और सही मौकों पर हँसता रहा। और जो लोग उसे पागल कहते थे, वे धीरे-धीरे समझ गए कि पूरे केरल में वही अकेला इंसान था जिसे कोई दुखी नहीं कर सकता था।","lang":"hi","body":"He walked on through the land all his life, wanting nothing, owing nothing, laughing at the right moments — and the ones who called him mad slowly noticed that he was the only person in Kerala nobody could make unhappy.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"पुराने त्रावणकोर के बैकवाटर वाले इलाके कायमकुलम में कभी कोचुन्नी नाम का एक लड़का रहता था, जो बेहद गरीब था—इतना गरीब कि मदद का कोई सहारा तक न था। वह खाने के बदले एक दुकानदार की दुकान में झाड़ू लगाता था, बड़ा फुर्तीला था, और हर चीज़ पर नज़र रखता था।","lang":"hi","body":"In Kayamkulam, in the backwater country of old Travancore, there was once a boy called Kochunni who was poor in the way that has no cushion under it at all. He swept a shopkeeper's floor for his food, and he was quick, and he watched everything.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"वह सबसे ज़्यादा कलरी को देखता था—वह अखाड़ा जहाँ लड़के केरल की पुरानी युद्ध-कला कलरीपायट्टु सीखते थे। वह जगह उसके जैसे लड़कों के लिए नहीं थी; वहाँ सीखने का खर्च उसके लिए कोई नहीं उठाने वाला था। इसलिए वह हर शाम बाड़ के पीछे से देखता, और जो देखता, चाँदनी रात में अकेले उसी का अभ्यास करता, जब तक कि वे सारे दाँव-पेंच उसके शरीर में बस नहीं गए।","lang":"hi","body":"What he watched most was the kalari — the training yard where boys learned kalaripayattu, the old exercise art of Kerala. It was not for boys like him; nobody would pay his way in. So he watched through the fence, every evening, and practised what he saw, alone, by moonlight, until the moves lived in his body.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"एक रात गुरुजी ने उसे ऐसा करते देख लिया। वे साये में खड़े रहे और दुकान के उस लड़के को पूरी शाम का सबक अकेले, बिल्कुल सही तरीके से दोहराते देखते रहे। आखिरकार गुरुजी ने कहा, \"अंदर आ जाओ। जो लड़का बाड़ के पीछे से खुद सीख सकता है, उसे मैं बाहर नहीं रखूँगा।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The teacher caught him at it one night. Stood in the shadows and watched a shop boy run through the whole evening's lesson, alone, correctly. \"Come inside,\" said the teacher at last. \"A boy who teaches himself through a fence will not be kept out by me.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"कोचुन्नी बड़ा होकर ताक़तवर और फुर्तीला बना, बिल्ली की तरह दीवार पर चढ़ने और बिना किसी आवाज़ के आँगन पार करने में माहिर। और यहाँ आकर कहानी की मुस्कान एक पल के लिए थम जाती है, क्योंकि उसने इन सब खूबियों से जो किया। उसने चीज़ें लीं। उन लोगों के बंद गोदामों से, जो भूखों को निचोड़कर अमीर बने थे—मगर उसने लिया, और लेना तो लेना ही होता है, और यह कहानी कोई और दिखावा नहीं करने वाली।","lang":"hi","body":"Kochunni grew up strong and quick, able to climb a wall like a cat and cross a courtyard without a sound. And here the story stops smiling for a moment, because of what he did with all that. He took things. From the locked storerooms of men who had grown rich squeezing the hungry — but he took, and taking is taking, and this story is not going to pretend otherwise.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"\"मगर कायमकुलम को असल में क्या याद है, और वह उसे याद ही क्यों करता है, वह बात यह है। उसने कभी भी किसी गरीब के घर को हाथ तक नहीं लगाया। और उन गरीब घरों ने एक बात और देखी: तंगी के महीने के बाद, किसी विधवा की चौखट पर सिक्कों की एक छोटी-सी पोटली मिलती। जिस घर का चूल्हा ठंडा पड़ चुका होता, उसके दरवाज़े के अंदर चावल की एक बोरी रखी मिलती। किसी ने नहीं देखा कि किसने रखी। पर हर कोई जानता था कि कौन था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"But here is what Kayamkulam actually remembers, and why it remembers him at all. He never, ever touched a poor house. And the poor houses noticed something else: after a hard month, a small bag of coins on the widow's doorstep. A sack of rice inside a door where the cooking fire had gone cold. Nobody saw who. Everybody knew who.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"\"और उसकी ज़बान पत्थर की लकीर थी। अगर कोचुन्नी ने कह दिया कि कोई बात होगी, तो वह होकर रहती थी; अगर उसने किसी को हिफ़ाज़त का भरोसा दे दिया, तो वह आदमी पूरी तरह सुरक्षित था। यहाँ तक कि जिन व्यापारियों को उसने लूटा था, वे भी दाँत पीसते हुए यही मानते थे: वह चोर पूरे इलाके का सबसे सच्चा और खरा इंसान था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And his word was iron. If Kochunni said a thing would happen, it happened; if he promised a man safety, that man was safe. Even the merchants he had robbed admitted it through their teeth: the thief was the fairest man in the district.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"\"इसलिए जब सिपाही घर-घर जाकर उसकी तलाशी लेने कायमकुलम पहुँचे, तो उन्हें कुछ ऐसा मिला जिसकी उन्होंने उम्मीद भी नहीं की थी। सन्नाटा। हर गली में, हर चौखट पर, हर मछुआरे, धोबन और धान के किसान की तरफ़ से: किसी ने उसे नहीं देखा था। किसी को कुछ भी नहीं पता था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"So when the soldiers came to Kayamkulam to search for him, house by house, they found something they had not planned for. Silence. In every lane, in every doorway, from every fisherman and washerwoman and rice farmer: nobody had seen him. Nobody knew anything at all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kochunni-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kochunni:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","text":"\"आख़िरकार कानून के हाथ उस तक पहुँच ही गए, जैसा कि हमेशा होता है, और उसके आख़िरी साल सलाखों के पीछे बीते। और तब भी — पुरानी कहानियों में इसी बात को बड़े चाव से याद किया जाता है — लोग उसके लिए खाना ले जाते, ज़ोर-ज़ोर से उसके किस्से सुनाते, और सवालों के जवाब देते हुए उसका नाम छिपाते रहे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The law caught up with him in the end, because it does, and his last years were spent locked away. And even then — this is the part the old tellings linger on — people carried food to him, and told his stories out loud, and went on leaving his name out of their answers.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"\"ईसाई लोग केरल में बहुत-बहुत पुराने ज़माने से रहते आए हैं — ज़्यादातर यूरोप से भी पहले से, और वे यह बात बड़े गर्व से बताते हैं। हरी-भरी नीची पहाड़ियों के बीच कडामट्टम में उनका एक पुराना गिरजाघर खड़ा है, और यह कहानी उसी की है: उसके सबसे मशहूर पादरी की कहानी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Christians have lived in Kerala for a very, very long time — longer than in most of Europe, they will tell you, and proudly. At Kadamattom, among low green hills, stands one of their old churches, and this story belongs to it: the story of its most famous priest.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"शुरुआत में वह पौलोस नाम का एक लड़का था, जो चर्च के मवेशियों की देखभाल करता था। एक दिन, एक भटकी हुई गाय के पीछे-पीछे वह घने ऊंचे जंगल में बहुत दूर निकल गया और रास्ता भटक गया। जिन लोगों को वह मिला, वे पहाड़ी लोग थे, जो किसी भी शहर से बहुत दूर रहते थे। वे जंगल और रात के बारे में ऐसी बातें जानते थे जिन्हें घाटी के लोग कब का भूल चुके थे। उन्होंने उसे सालों तक अपने पास रखा और बहुत कुछ सिखाया।","lang":"hi","body":"He began as a boy called Poulose who looked after the church's cattle, and one day, following a strayed cow, he went too far into the high forest and was lost. The people who found him were hill people who lived far from any town, and who knew things about the forest and the night that the valley folk had long forgotten. They took him in for years, and taught him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"आखिरकार जब वह घर लौटा, तो उसने चर्च की सेवा की और समय के साथ वहां का पादरी बन गया — यानी 'कतनार', जो केरल में पादरी के लिए एक पुराना शब्द है। कदमत्तथु कतनार: यानी कदमत्तम् का पादरी। और धीरे-धीरे यह बात चारों तरफ फैल गई कि इस पादरी को अंधेरे से बिल्कुल डर नहीं लगता था। ज़रा भी नहीं।","lang":"hi","body":"When he came home at last, he served his church and in time became its priest — its kathanar, which is simply the old Kerala word for a priest. Kadamattathu Kathanar: the priest of Kadamattom. And word slowly went round that this particular priest was not afraid of the dark. At all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"क्योंकि उसने पहाड़ों में एक बात सीखी थी, और वह सीखने लायक बात है: अंधेरा ज़्यादातर अकेली चीज़ों से भरा होता है, बुरी चीज़ों से नहीं।","lang":"hi","body":"Because here is what he had learned in the hills, and it is worth learning: the dark is mostly full of lonely things, not wicked ones.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"एक बार एक परिवार उनके पास आया, बिल्कुल थका-हारा और उतरा हुआ चेहरा लिए। उनके घर में कुछ था। बर्तन अपने आप खिसकने लगते थे। बिना हवा वाली रातों में भी छत खड़खड़ाती थी। हफ़्तों से कोई सोया नहीं था। कतनार ने अपनी लालटेन उठाई और उनके साथ उनके घर चल दिए। वे सबसे ज़्यादा आवाज़ वाले कमरे के बीचो-बीच बैठ गए और घुप अंधेरा होने का इंतज़ार करने लगे।","lang":"hi","body":"A family came to him once, worn out and grey-faced. Something was in their house. Pots shifted by themselves. The roof rattled on windless nights. Nobody had slept in weeks. The Kathanar picked up his lamp and walked home with them, and sat down in the middle of the noisiest room, and waited for full dark.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"और फिर उन्होंने वह किया जो करने की कभी कोई सोचता भी नहीं। उन्होंने उस खाली कमरे से बात की, बड़े आदर से, जैसे किसी मेहमान से बात की जाती है। \"हाँ,\" उन्होंने कहा, \"अब सबका ध्यान तुम्हारी तरफ है। तुम क्या चाहते हो?\"","lang":"hi","body":"And then he did the thing nobody ever thinks to do. He spoke to the empty room, politely, the way you speak to a guest. \"Well,\" he said. \"You have everyone's attention now. What is it you want?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"तो उन्होंने उसे वही दिया जो वह चाहता था। उसकी अपनी एक जगह — ज़मीन के कोने पर खड़ा एक बड़ा पुराना पेड़ — और उससे भी बढ़िया, एक ज़िम्मेदारी: पहरेदारी करने की। \"आत्माएं शादी में आए बच्चों की तरह होती हैं,\" कतनार कहा करते थे। \"अगर उन्हें अनदेखा करो, तो आफत बन जाती हैं। और अगर कोई काम सौंप दो, तो कमाल कर दिखाती हैं।\" बर्तन फिर कभी नहीं हिले, और परिवार चैन से सोया।","lang":"hi","body":"So he gave it what it wanted. A place of its own — a great old tree at the edge of the land — and, even better, a job: keeping watch. \"Spirits are like children at a wedding,\" the Kathanar used to say. \"Dreadful when ignored. Magnificent when given something to do.\" The pots never moved again, and the family slept.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"एक कहानी बताती है कि एक साया रात के रास्ते पर मीलों तक उनके पीछे लगा रहा, पूरे रास्ते शरारत की तरकीबें सोचता रहा — और कदमट्टम पहुँचते-पहुँचते, न जाने कैसे, उनका दोस्त बन गया और वहीं बस गया। उनके साथ यही दस्तूर था। जो कोई भी कथनार को डराने निकला, वह अंत में उन्हीं के काम में लग गया।","lang":"hi","body":"One story tells of a spirit who followed him for miles along a night road, planning mischief the whole way — and who arrived at Kadamattom, somehow, as his friend, and stayed. That was the pattern with him. Whatever set out to frighten the Kathanar ended up working for him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kathanar-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kathanar:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","text":"और हर धर्म के लोग उन्हें बुलावा भेजते थे — हिंदू परिवार, मुस्लिम परिवार, ईसाई परिवार — क्योंकि अंधेरे में जलता दीया दरवाज़े पर लिखा नाम नहीं देखता। वे अपने पादरी वाले चोगे में, अपना दीया लिए, बिना किसी फ़र्क के सबके पास जाते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"And people of every faith sent for him — Hindu households, Muslim households, Christian households — because a lamp in the dark does not check the name on the door. He went to all of them alike, in his priest's robes, with his lamp.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"केरल के सुदूर उत्तर में — कन्नूर, कासरगोड, जिसे लोग मालाबार का इलाक़ा कहते हैं — वहाँ देवता इस इंतज़ार में नहीं बैठते कि कोई उनसे मिलने आए। साल में एक बार, ठंड के मौसम में, वे खुद गाँव आते हैं, और नाचते हुए आते हैं। इसे थैय्यम कहते हैं, और दुनिया में कहीं भी इसके जैसा कुछ और नहीं है।","lang":"hi","body":"In the far north of Kerala — Kannur, Kasaragod, the country they call Malabar — the gods do not wait to be visited. Once a year, in the cool season, they come to the village themselves, and they come dancing. It is called theyyam, and there is nothing else quite like it anywhere.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"देवू नाम की एक लड़की एक रात यह देखने के लिए जागी रही कि उसके अपने दादाजी के साथ यह कैसे होता है। वे मंदिर के आँगन में चटाई पर बिल्कुल शांत बैठे रहे, जबकि कलाकार दीयों की रोशनी में घंटों उन पर काम करते रहे — नारंगी, लाल और काला रंग, नारियल की सींक से उनके चेहरे पर खींची जाती बारीक रेखाएँ, और पास ही तैयार होता एक मुकुट जो दरवाज़े से भी ऊँचा था।","lang":"hi","body":"A girl called Devu sat up one night to watch it happen to her own grandfather. He sat perfectly still on a mat in the temple yard while artists worked on him by lamplight, hour after hour — orange and red and black, fine lines drawn on his face with a slip of coconut leaf, a headdress being built nearby that stood taller than a door.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"उसका परिवार पीढ़ियों से इस थैय्यम को संजोता आ रहा था — रंग-रूप, गीत, नाच के कदम, जो ज़मीन की तरह पीढ़ी-दर-पीढ़ी सौंपे गए थे। और यह बात सचमुच ठहरकर सोचने वाली है। मालाबार में देवताओं का रूप धरने वाले ज़्यादातर परिवार वे हैं, जिन्हें दूसरों ने हमेशा सबसे पीछे रखा — उनके काम की वजह से, और जिन घरों में वे पैदा हुए उस वजह से। थैय्यम की रात, यह सब कुछ पूरी तरह पलट जाता है।","lang":"hi","body":"Her family had carried this theyyam for generations — the painting, the songs, the steps, handed down like land. And here is the part to sit with. The families who carry the gods in Malabar are, most of them, families whom others too often put last — for the work they did, and the houses they were born into. On theyyam night, that turns upside down.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"क्योंकि जब भोर से पहले ढोल बजने लगे, तो हर कोई आया, हर कोई खड़ा रहा, और सबने उसके दादाजी की प्रतीक्षा की। वह ज़मींदार जिसके खेतों में वे काम करते थे। दुकानदार। मास्टरजी। इस रात, इस आँगन में, जिस इंसान के पास से वे साल भर यूँ ही निकल जाते थे, आज उसी के आगे सिर झुकाने वे सब आए थे।","lang":"hi","body":"Because when the drums began before dawn, everyone came, and everyone stood, and everyone waited on her grandfather. The landowner whose fields he worked. The shopkeeper. The teacher. On this night, in this yard, the man they walked past all year was the one they had all come to bow to.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"उनके सिर पर मुकुट सजाया गया। ढोल की थाप तेज़ होती गई। और फिर वह पल आया जिसकी तैयारी में पूरी रात बीती थी: उनके रंगे हुए चेहरे के सामने एक छोटा-सा आईना लाया गया। उन्होंने उसमें देखा। और कहानियों में सब यही मानते हैं — उस आईने में से जो वापस देखता है, वह अब कोई कलाकार नहीं होता। देवू के दादाजी ने आईने में देखा, और उसमें से देवी बाहर देख रही थीं।","lang":"hi","body":"The headdress was lifted onto him. The drums climbed. And then came the moment the whole night had been building to: they held up a small mirror in front of his painted face. He looked into it. And the tellings all agree — the person who looks back out of that mirror is not the performer anymore. Devu's grandfather looked into the glass, and the goddess looked out.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"उस बची हुई पूरी रात, देवी उसी आँगन में रहीं — मशालों की रोशनी में घूमती हुईं, दमकती हुईं, विशाल रूप में — और गाँव वाले वह कर सके जो वे आम तौर पर कभी नहीं कर पाते: अपनी देवी के पास जाना और उनसे बातें करना।","lang":"hi","body":"For the rest of that night, the goddess was in the yard — turning, blazing, enormous under the torches — and the village could do what a village can never usually do: walk up to its goddess and talk to her.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"देवी ने नाम ले-लेकर हर किसी को आशीर्वाद दिया — वे सबको जानती थीं। जहाँ ज़रूरत थी, वहाँ उन्होंने हलके से डाँटा भी। उन्होंने उन दो घरों का झगड़ा सुलझा दिया जो उस शादी के बाद से एक-दूसरे से बोले तक नहीं थे। और ढोल किसी ज़मीन की तरह इस सब को साधे रहे, जब तक कि आसमान धूसर नहीं हो गया।","lang":"hi","body":"The goddess blessed each person by name — she knew them all. She scolded gently where scolding was needed. She settled the quarrel between the two houses that had not spoken since the wedding. And the drums held it all up like a floor, till the sky went grey.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"सुबह होते ही मुकुट उतार लिया गया, चेहरे का रंग पोंछ दिया गया, और वहाँ चटाई पर देवू के दादाजी बैठे थे — फिर से बिल्कुल सामान्य, बेहद थके हुए, काँपते हाथों से गरम चाय पीते हुए। देवू दबे पाँव उनके पास गई और उनसे पूछा: \"देवी कहाँ चली गईं?\"","lang":"hi","body":"At dawn the headdress was lifted off, and the paint was wiped away, and there on the mat sat Devu's grandfather — small again, tired to the bone, drinking hot tea with both hands wobbling. Devu crept up and asked him: \"Where did the goddess go?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.theyyam-mirror-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.theyyam-mirror:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","text":"\"अपने घर,\" सुबह की ओर देखते हुए दादाजी बोले। \"अगले साल तक के लिए।\" उन्होंने चाय की एक और घूँट भरी, और उसकी तरफ़ देखकर मुस्कुराए। \"चिंता मत करो। अब उन्हें रास्ता पता है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"Home,\" said her grandfather, looking out at the morning. \"Until next year.\" He took another sip of tea, and smiled at her. \"Don't worry. Now she knows the way.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"\"पंपा नदी के किनारे आरनमुला का मंदिर है, जहाँ कृष्ण की पूजा पार्थसारथी के रूप में होती है — यानी अर्जुन के सारथी, वो सखा जो रथ हाँकते हैं। याद रखना कि यहाँ वही राह दिखाने वाले सखा बसते हैं। कहानी के अंत के लिए यह बात बहुत मायने रखती है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"On the banks of the river Pampa stands the temple of Aranmula, where Krishna is worshipped as Parthasarathy — Arjuna's charioteer, the friend who steers. Remember that it is the steering-friend who lives here. It matters to how this story ends.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"\"पुरानी कथा है कि नदी के ऊपरी छोर पर रहने वाले एक परिवार को एक बड़ा सम्मान मिला था: हर साल ओणम के समय, उनकी नाव पंपा नदी के रास्ते मंदिर के महाभोज की रसद ले जाती थी — चावल और नारियल, केले और गुड़, हर आने वाले इंसान का पेट भरने के लिए ज़रूरी हर एक चीज़, ताकि कोई भी भूखा न लौटे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Long ago, the legend says, one household upriver was given a great honour: every year at Onam time, their boat would carry the provisions for the temple feast down the Pampa — rice and coconuts, plantains and jaggery, everything needed to feed every single person who came, with nobody turned away.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"\"और एक साल, नदी के एक वीरान और अंधेरे हिस्से में, किनारों की परछाइयों से चुपके से कुछ नावें बाहर निकलीं। वे ऐसे लोग थे जो महाभोज की सामग्री खुद हड़पना चाहते थे। रसद वाली नाव धीमी, भारी और अकेली थी, नदी बहुत लंबी थी, और रात बेहद अंधेरी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And one year, on a lonely dark stretch of the river, boats slid out from the shadows of the banks. Men who wanted the feast for themselves. The provision boat was slow and heavy and alone, and the river was long, and the night was very dark.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"\"लेकिन नदी का किनारा कभी उतना सूना नहीं होता जितना दिखता है। ख़बर पानी के ऊपर किसी भी नाव से भी तेज़ दौड़ी — एक पुकार, एक दिया, एक शंख — और एक के बाद एक गाँव ने अपनी नावें पानी में उतार दीं। और उन गाँवों के पास थीं चुंडन वल्लम: यानी सर्प नौकाएँ। दस घरों जितनी लंबी, पीछे से फन उठाए नाग की तरह मुड़ी हुई, और एक-एक नाव में सौ-सौ मल्लाह।\"","lang":"hi","body":"But a river bank is never as empty as it looks. Word ran along the water faster than any boat — a shout, a lamp, a conch — and village after village pushed out what it had. And what those villages had were chundan vallams: snake boats. Long as ten houses, curled high at the stern like a cobra's raised hood, a hundred rowers to a boat.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"\"अब, चालीस तेज़ नावें एक धीमी नाव की पहरेदारी कर रही थीं। और ऐसा करने का एक सही तरीका होता है, और एक ग़लत तरीका।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Now, forty fast boats guarding one slow one. There is a right way and a wrong way to do that.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"दावत का सामान पहुँच गया। मंदिर ने सबको भोजन कराया, और किसी को भी वापस नहीं लौटाया गया। और कहानी मुस्कुराकर कहती है कि पार्थसारथी — जिन्होंने महायुद्ध में ख़ुद लड़ने के बजाय सिर्फ़ रथ हाँका, उस एक इंसान के ठीक साथ रहकर जिसे उनकी ज़रूरत थी — उस साल बहुत प्रसन्न हुए, और सिर्फ़ खाने की वजह से नहीं।","lang":"hi","body":"The feast arrived. The temple fed everyone, with nobody turned away. And the story smiles and says that Parthasarathy — who spent the great war not fighting but steering, right beside one man who needed him — was pleased that year, and not mainly by the food.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"तो यह एक नियम बन गया, और फिर एक त्योहार। हर साल ओणम के मौसम में, पंपा नदी के गाँवों की सर्प नौकाएँ लौट आती हैं और दावत वाली नाव के साथ-साथ नदी में आगे बढ़ती हैं — ताल में चमकते हुए चप्पू, ताल मिलाते वंचिपाट्टु नाम के नाविक गीत, और किनारों पर गाते हज़ारों लोग।","lang":"hi","body":"So it became the rule, and then the festival. Every year in the Onam season, the snake boats of the Pampa villages come back and go down the river with the feast boat — oars flashing in time, the boat songs called vanchipattu keeping the stroke, thousands singing on the banks.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.aranmula-boat-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.aranmula-boat:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Race That Nobody Must Win","text":"और आरनमुला में, बड़े-बुज़ुर्ग आपको बताएंगे कि यह दिखने में भले ही कैसी भी लगे, पर असल में यह कोई दौड़ नहीं है। सारी नावें एक साथ पहुँचती हैं, क्योंकि वे वही कर रही होती हैं जो उनके परदादाओं की नावें करती थीं: उस दावत की नाव का साथ निभाना, उसकी गति से चलना जिसे उनकी ज़रूरत है। दिन तो सिर्फ़ तभी बिगड़ता है जब कोई अकेला पहुँच जाए।","lang":"hi","body":"And at Aranmula, the elders will tell you, it is not truly a race at all, whatever it looks like. The boats arrive together, because they are doing what their great-great-grandfathers' boats did: escorting the feast, at the pace of the one who needs them. The day is only ruined if somebody arrives alone.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"यह वह कहानी है जो केरल के मुसलमान सुनाते हैं कि कैसे उनका धर्म पहली बार भारत पहुँचा, और यह उन्हीं की कहानी है, और यहाँ इसे उसी तरह सुनाया गया है जैसे वे सुनाते हैं — प्यार के साथ। यह कहानी कोडुंगल्लूर से शुरू होती है, जो मसालों के तट का एक बड़ा बंदरगाह था, जहाँ अरब, चीन और हर जगह से जहाज़ आते थे, और जहाँ चेरामन पेरुमल नाम के राजा राज करते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"This is the story Kerala's Muslims tell about how their faith first came home to India, and it is theirs, and it is told here the way they tell it — with love. It begins in Kodungallur, the great port of the spice coast, where ships came in from Arabia and China and everywhere between, and a king called Cheraman Perumal ruled.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"एक रात राजा ने एक सपना देखा। उन्होंने देखा कि समंदर के ऊपर पूरा चाँद साफ़-साफ़ दो टुकड़ों में बँट गया — और फिर आपस में जुड़कर दोबारा पूरा हो गया। वे धड़कते दिल के साथ जागे, और यह सपना आम सपनों की तरह धुंधला नहीं पड़ा। यह उनके सीने में जूते में अटके किसी कंकड़ की तरह चुभता रहा।","lang":"hi","body":"One night the king dreamed. He saw the full moon over the sea split cleanly into two halves — and then draw together and become whole again. He woke with his heart pounding, and the dream would not fade the way dreams do. It sat in his chest like a stone in a shoe.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"उनके अपने विद्वानों ने इस सपने को हर तरह से समझने की कोशिश की, पर वे इसका अर्थ न खोल पाए। तभी अरब से एक जहाज़ आया, और जब उस पर सवार व्यापारियों से उनके देश का हालचाल आदर से पूछा गया, तो उन्होंने ऐसी बात कही कि राजा अपने सिंहासन पर धीरे से आगे की ओर झुक आए। दूर मक्का नाम के शहर में, लोग एक मार्गदर्शक — पैगंबर मुहम्मद की चर्चा कर रहे थे, जिनका नाम केरल के मुसलमान उनके लिए दुआ भेजे बिना कभी नहीं लेते — और उनसे जुड़ी एक निशानी की चर्चा कर रहे थे: आसमान में दो टुकड़ों में बँटा हुआ चाँद।","lang":"hi","body":"His own wise men turned the dream over and could not open it. Then a ship came in from Arabia, and the merchants aboard, asked politely about news from their country, said something that made the king sit slowly forward on his throne. Far away, in a city called Mecca, people spoke of a teacher — the Prophet Muhammad, whose name Kerala's Muslims never say without adding a blessing after it — and of a sign connected with him: the moon, seen parted in the sky.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"राजा बिल्कुल शांत हो गए। उनका सपना, समुद्र के उस पार से, दूसरों की ज़ुबान पर चला आ रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"The king went very quiet. His dream, walking around in other men's mouths, from the other side of the sea.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"तो उन्होंने वही किया जो किसी कहानी में शायद ही कोई राजा करता है: उन्होंने अपने राज्य को इस तरह व्यवस्थित किया कि वह उनके बिना भी चल सके। उन्होंने अपने सूबेदारों में राज्य के काम बाँट दिए, सारे हिसाब-किताब निपटाए, जो ज़रूरी बातें कहनी थीं वे कहीं — और फिर अपने बंदरगाह की ओर चल पड़े और पश्चिम की ओर जाने वाले जहाज़ पर सवार हो गए, अपने पीछे हरे-भरे तट को धीरे-धीरे छूटते हुए देखते हुए।","lang":"hi","body":"So he did what almost no king in any story does: he set his kingdom in order to run without him. He divided the duties of the land among his governors, settled every account, said what needed saying — and then walked down to his own harbour and boarded a ship west, watching his green coast slide away behind him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"और परंपरा बताती है कि वे अरब पहुँचे, पैगंबर से मिले, और आखिरकार आमने-सामने बैठकर अपना सवाल पूछा। और उनका दिल, जो उस सपने वाली रात से बेचैन था, एकदम शांत और निश्चिंत हो गया — ठीक वैसे जैसे कोई नाव आख़िरकार बंदरगाह पहुँचकर ठहर जाती है। उन्होंने वहाँ दोनों हाथों से नए धर्म को अपना लिया।","lang":"hi","body":"And the tradition tells that he reached Arabia, and met the Prophet, and asked his question at last, face to face. And that his heart, which had been restless since the night of the dream, went still and certain — the way a boat goes still when it finally reaches harbour. He chose the new faith there, with both hands.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"वे अपने लोगों को यह बताने की चाह लिए घर लौटने को निकले कि उन्होंने क्या पाया था। कहानी कहती है कि रास्ते में ही, अरब के तट पर बसे एक बंदरगाह वाले शहर में, उनकी यात्रा ख़ामोशी से समाप्त हो गई। पर उनके पत्र उनके बिना ही आगे बढ़ते रहे, जिन्हें उनके साथी लेकर चले — पूरब की ओर, समुद्र पार, उस तट की तरफ जिसे वे आँखें मूँदते ही देख सकते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"He set out for home, longing to tell his own people what he had found. The story says his journey ended quietly on the way, in a harbour town on the Arabian shore. But his letters travelled on without him, carried by his companions — east, across the sea, to the coast he could see when he shut his eyes.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.cheraman-moon-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.cheraman-moon:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Followed the Moon","text":"और कोडुंगल्लूर में, जहाँ वे राजा थे, उनके दोस्तों का स्वागत किया गया — यह बात ध्यान देने लायक है; उनका स्वागत किया गया — और एक मस्जिद बनाई गई। किसी दूर देश का कोई भव्य गुंबद नहीं: केरल की एक छोटी सी इमारत, जिसकी आस-पास के मंदिरों और घरों जैसी ढलवां खपरैल वाली छतें थीं, और अंदर एक पुराना पीतल का दीया जल रहा था। परंपरा इसे चेरामन जुमा मस्जिद कहती है, भारत की पहली मस्जिद, जिसका नाम उस राजा के नाम पर रखा गया जिसने अपने सवाल का पीछा किया था।","lang":"hi","body":"And at Kodungallur, where he had been king, his friends were welcomed — that is worth noticing; they were welcomed — and a mosque was built. Not a grand dome from a far country: a small Kerala building, with sloping tiled roofs like the temples and houses around it, and an old brass lamp burning inside. Tradition calls it the Cheraman Juma Masjid, the first mosque in India, named for the king who followed his question.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cheraman-moon.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-moon|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"अव्वैयार के बारे में ज़्यादातर कहानियाँ शुरू होने से पहले ही वह बूढ़ी हो चुकी थीं — सफ़ेद बालों वाली एक कवयित्री, जो अपने पैरों पर चलकर पूरे तमिल देश में घूमीं, और जिनके पास अपनी कविताओं के सिवा कुछ नहीं था। जब वह अंदर आतीं, तो राजा तक खड़े हो जाते थे। तमिल बच्चे आज भी कुछ और सीखने से पहले उनकी कही बातें सीखते हैं। सब मानते थे कि वह उस पूरे देश की सबसे समझदार इंसान थीं — और अब तक, मन ही मन, वह खुद भी यही मानने लगी थीं।","lang":"hi","body":"Avvaiyar was old before most stories about her even begin — a white-haired poet who walked the whole Tamil country on her own two feet, owning nothing but her verses. Kings stood up when she came in. Tamil children still learn her sayings before they learn almost anything else. She was, everyone agreed, the wisest person in the land — and by now, quietly, she agreed too.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"एक तपती दोपहर, एक लंबी सड़क पर थककर और प्यास से बेहाल, वह नावल के पेड़ की छाँव में बैठ गईं — वही जामुन, जिसका गहरा जामुनी फल जीभ को स्याही की तरह रंग देता है। डालियों पर ऊपर बैठा एक छोटा ग्वाला अपनी टाँगें झुला रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"One blazing afternoon, tired and thirsty on a long road, she sat down in the shade of a naaval tree — the jamun, whose dark purple fruit stains your tongue like ink. High in the branches, a small cowherd boy sat swinging his legs.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"\"पाट्टी,\" लड़का ऊपर से पुकारा — यानी दादी — \"क्या आपको कुछ फल चाहिए?\" \"हाँ, बहुत मन है,\" अव्वैयार ने कहा। और फिर लड़के ने अपना सवाल पूछा। \"मैं आपको भुना हुआ फल दूँ, या बिना भुना फल?\" उसकी तमिल में यह किसी गीत जैसा लगा: सुट्टा पज़्हम, सुदाद पज़्हम?","lang":"hi","body":"\"Paatti,\" the boy called down — grandmother — \"would you like some fruit?\" \"Very much,\" said Avvaiyar. And then the boy asked his question. \"Shall I give you roasted fruit, or unroasted fruit?\" In his Tamil it sang: sutta pazham, sudatha pazham?","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"अव्वैयार ने बच्चों के इस बचपने पर एक ठंडी सांस भरी। पेड़ों पर भी कहीं फल भुना करते हैं! लेकिन उन्हें गर्मी लग रही थी, वह थकी हुई थीं और फल भी बहुत बढ़िया दिख रहे थे, इसलिए वह भी उसकी बात में शामिल हो गईं। \"तो फिर भुना हुआ ही सही,\" उन्होंने कहा। और लड़के ने अपनी डाल हिला दी।","lang":"hi","body":"Avvaiyar sighed at the silliness of children. Fruit does not roast on trees. But she was hot and tired and the fruit looked wonderful, so she played along. \"Roasted, then,\" she said. And the boy shook his branch.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"पके-पके जामुन उनके पैरों के पास रेत में झमाझम गिर पड़े। उन्होंने उन्हें बटोरा — और बिना सोचे-समझे, खाने से पहले रेत उड़ाने के लिए हर एक पर फूँक मारी, फूँ, फूँ।","lang":"hi","body":"The ripe jamuns rained down into the sand at her feet. She gathered them up — and, without thinking, blew on each one, phoo, phoo, to puff the sand off before eating it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"डालियों से नीचे एक नन्हीं सी आवाज़ तैरती हुई आई, सुबह जैसी मासूम। \"संभल कर, पाट्टी। क्या वे बहुत गरम हैं? मैं देख रहा हूँ कि आप उन्हें ठंडा करने के लिए फूँक मार रही हैं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"A small voice floated down from the branches, innocent as morning. \"Careful, paatti. Are they too hot? I see you are blowing on them to cool them.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"जामुन मुँह तक ले जाते-जाते अव्वैयार ठिठक गईं। पूरे तमिल देश की सबसे ज्ञानी स्त्री। रेत में खड़ी, भुने हुए फल पर फूँक मारती हुई। मात खा गईं — पूरी तरह से, बड़ी ही साफ़ और मीठी मात — पेड़ पर बैठे एक चरवाहे बच्चे से।","lang":"hi","body":"Avvaiyar stopped with a jamun halfway to her mouth. The wisest woman in the Tamil country. Standing in the sand, blowing on a roasted fruit. Beaten — completely, sweetly, fairly beaten — by a cowherd child in a tree.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"और जब उन्होंने उस बालक को आदर से नमन करने के लिए ऊपर देखा, तो डालियाँ रोशनी से जगमगा उठी थीं। तमिल देश कहता है कि वह बालक स्वयं मुरुगन थे — इन पहाड़ियों के प्यारे देवता, जिन्हें तमिल भाषा से गहरा प्रेम है और एक अच्छे खेल से तो और भी ज़्यादा — जो समय रहते अपनी बूढ़ी कवयित्री को एक आख़िरी बात याद दिलाने नीचे आए थे।","lang":"hi","body":"And when she looked up to salute the child properly, the branches were full of light. The Tamil country tells that the boy was Murugan himself — the beloved god of these hills, who loves the Tamil language and loves a good game even more — come down to remind his old poet of one last thing while there was time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.avvaiyar-naaval-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.avvaiyar-naaval:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fruit That Needed Blowing On","text":"अव्वैयार ने पेड़ के आगे सिर झुकाया, और उस दिन से जुड़ी उनकी यह बात आज भी तमिल लोगों की ज़बान पर है: \"कत्रदु कै मण्णलवु; कल्लाददु उलगलवु।\" जो मैंने सीखा है, वह मुट्ठी भर रेत है। जो मैंने नहीं सीखा, वह यह पूरी विशाल दुनिया है।","lang":"hi","body":"Avvaiyar bowed to the tree, and the saying tradition gives her from that day is still on Tamil tongues: \"Katrathu kai mann alavu; kallathathu ulagalavu.\" What I have learned is a handful of sand. What I have not learned is the whole wide world.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-avvaiyar-naaval.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.avvaiyar-naaval|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"पुहार के महान बंदरगाह शहर में, जहाँ कावेरी पूर्वी समुद्र से मिलती है, एक युवा जोड़ा रहता था: कण्णगी, जो दीये की लौ जैसी धीर और अडिग थी, और उसका पति कोवलन, जो मनमोहक और उदार तो था, पर बहुत लापरवाह था।","lang":"hi","body":"In the great port city of Puhar, where the Kaveri meets the eastern sea, there lived a young couple: Kannagi, patient and steady as a lamp flame, and her husband Kovalan, who was charming, and generous, and not very careful.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"सारा पैसा खत्म हो गया। इसमें कुछ हाथ कोवलन का अपना भी था, वह यह बात जानता था और इसका भारी बोझ उसके मन पर था। आखिरकार उनके पास बस एक ही खज़ाना बचा था: कण्णगी की सोने की पायलों की एक जोड़ी—जिन्हें तमिल में 'सिलंबु' कहते हैं—खोखले सोने की पायलें, जिनमें माणिक भरे थे जो उसके चलते वक्त बारिश की फुहार की तरह छनछनाते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"The money ran out. Some of that was Kovalan's own doing, and he knew it, and it sat heavily on him. What was left to them, in the end, was one treasure: Kannagi's pair of golden anklets — silambu, the Tamil calls them — hollow gold, each one filled with rubies that whispered like rain when she walked.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"\"हम फिर से शुरुआत करेंगे,\" कण्णगी ने कहा, और उसका यही पक्का इरादा था। वे लंबा रास्ता तय करके पांड्य राजाओं के शहर मदुरै पहुँचे, और कोवलन उसकी एक पायल बेचने बाज़ार ले गया, ताकि वे एक छोटी सी दुकान खोल सकें और अपनी ज़िंदगी नए सिरे से शुरू कर सकें।","lang":"hi","body":"\"We will start again,\" said Kannagi, and meant it. They walked all the long way to Madurai, the city of the Pandyan kings, and Kovalan took one of her anklets to the market to sell, so they could open a small shop and begin their life over.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"लेकिन उस शहर में, हाल ही में रानी की अपनी पायल गायब हो गई थी—जिसे खुद शाही सुनार ने ही चुराया था, और अब उसे तुरंत किसी ऐसे की तलाश थी जिस पर वह सारा इल्ज़ाम मढ़ सके। और तभी धूल में सना यह परदेसी हाथ में सोने की पायल लिए वहाँ आ पहुँचा। सुनार राजा के पास गया। राजा व्यस्त थे और चोरी की बात से गुस्से में थे, और उन्होंने वह एक सवाल भी नहीं पूछा जो एक राजा को हमेशा पूछना चाहिए। उन्होंने कोई पड़ताल नहीं की। और कोवलन कभी घर नहीं लौटा।","lang":"hi","body":"But in that city, the queen's own anklet had lately gone missing — taken by the royal goldsmith himself, who now needed, urgently, somebody to blame. And here came a dusty stranger, holding out a golden anklet. The goldsmith went to the king. The king was busy, and angry about the theft, and he did not ask the one question a king must always ask. He did not check. And Kovalan did not come home.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"जब यह खबर कण्णगी तक पहुँची, तो वह रो पड़ी—एक बार, फूट-फूट कर, जैसे कोई बादल फट पड़ा हो। और फिर वह थम गई। वह उठ खड़ी हुई, उसने दूसरी पायल उठाई, और उसे अपनी मुट्ठी में ऊँचा थामे मदुरै की गलियों से होती हुई महल के दरवाज़ों की ओर बढ़ चली, और पूरा शहर अपने घरों की चौखटों से बाहर निकलकर उसके पीछे-पीछे चल पड़ा।","lang":"hi","body":"When the word reached Kannagi, she wept — once, hard, like a monsoon breaking. And then she stopped. She stood up, and picked up the second anklet, and walked through the streets of Madurai to the palace gates with it held high in her fist, and the whole city came out of its doorways and followed her.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"वह पांड्य राजा के सिंहासन के सामने जा खड़ी हुई—सफ़र की साड़ी पहने एक अकेली, साधारण सी औरत, और उसकी आवाज़ ज़रा भी नहीं काँपी। \"आपके सुनार का कहना है कि मेरे पति ने रानी की पायल चुराई है। यह रही उसकी दूसरी जोड़ी, जो मेरे अपने पाँव की है। मुझे बताइए, महाराज—रानी की पायलों के अंदर क्या है?\"","lang":"hi","body":"She stood before the throne of the Pandyan king, one small woman in a travelling sari, and her voice did not shake. \"Your goldsmith says my husband stole the queen's anklet. Here is its twin, from my own ankle. Tell me, King — what is inside the queen's anklets?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"\"मोती,\" राजा ने कहा। \"रानी की पायलों में मोती भरे हैं।\" कण्णगी ने अपना हाथ उठाया और सिंहासन के आगे लगे पत्थरों पर पटक कर अपनी पायल तोड़ दी—और लाल बारिश की तरह माणिक पूरे फर्श पर बिखर गए, उछलते और लुढ़कते हुए दरबार के हर दरबारी के कदमों तक जा पहुँचे। इसके बाद जो सन्नाटा पसरा, वह मदुरै में गूँजने वाली अब तक की सबसे बुलंद आवाज़ थी।","lang":"hi","body":"\"Pearls,\" said the king. \"The queen's anklets hold pearls.\" Kannagi raised her arm and broke her anklet open on the stones before the throne — and rubies burst across the floor like red rain, bouncing and rolling to the feet of every courtier in the hall. The silence after them was the loudest sound Madurai ever heard.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"एक ही पल में राजा सब कुछ समझ गए — सुनार का झूठ, अपनी जल्दबाज़ी, वह बेकसूर आदमी, और वह सवाल जो उन्होंने नहीं पूछा था। प्राचीन महाकाव्य कहता है कि उनका दिल यह बोझ सह नहीं पाया और वहीं सिंहासन पर ही रुक गया; और उनके पीछे-पीछे रानी की साँसें भी थम गईं। महाकाव्य यह याद दिलाना नहीं भूलता कि एक राजा की गलती ने राजा से भी उसका सब कुछ छीन लिया।","lang":"hi","body":"The king understood everything in a single heartbeat — the goldsmith's lie, his own haste, the innocent man, the question he had not asked. The old epic says his heart could not carry it, and stopped, there on the throne; and the queen's followed his. A king's mistake, the epic is careful to say, cost the king everything too.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"और महाकाव्य बताता है कि कण्णगि का दुख इतनी तेज़ आग बनकर दहका कि वह प्राचीन शहर उसे कभी भूल नहीं पाया। कहानी का वह हिस्सा तब के लिए है जब तुम थोड़े और बड़े हो जाओगे — जब तैयार हो, तब पूछना कि मदुरै उस रात को कैसे याद रखता है। यहाँ जो बात मायने रखती है वह यह है कि इसके बाद क्या हुआ: तमिल धरती ने उन्हें कभी खोने नहीं दिया।","lang":"hi","body":"And Kannagi's grief, the epic tells, blazed so hot that the old city itself never forgot it. That part of the story is for when you are older — ask, when you are ready, how Madurai remembers that night. What matters here is what came after: the Tamil land did not let her go.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kannagi-anklet-9":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kannagi-anklet:9","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Anklet Full of Rubies","text":"लोगों ने उन्हें देवी बना दिया। पत्तिनी, पतिव्रता देवी — जिनकी पूजा तमिलनाडु में हुई, केरल में हुई, जहाँ पुरानी परंपरा उन्हें कोडुंगल्लूर के भव्य मंदिर से जोड़ती है, और समुद्र पार श्रीलंका में भी। एक ऐसी नारी, जो अपनी मुट्ठी में सच लेकर महल में दाखिल हुईं, और जीत गईं।","lang":"hi","body":"They made her a goddess. Pattini, the faithful one — honoured in Tamil Nadu, honoured in Kerala, where old tradition links her to the great temple at Kodungallur, honoured across the sea in Sri Lanka. A woman who walked into a palace with the truth in her fist, and won.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kannagi-anklet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kannagi-anklet|h9","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"बहुत, बहुत पुरानी बात है, चोल देश में — इतनी पुरानी कि जब बाकी पुरानी कहानियाँ नई थीं, तब भी यह पुरानी हो चुकी थी — एक राजा की कहानी तब खत्म हो गई जब उनका बेटा अभी छोटा बच्चा ही था। और जो लोग खुद गद्दी हथियाना चाहते थे, उन्होंने उस बच्चे को देखा, उसे दूर भिजवा दिया, एक घर में कैद कर दिया, और उसे हमेशा-हमेशा वहीं बंद रखने की सोची।","lang":"hi","body":"Long, long ago in the Chola country — so long ago that this was already an old story when most old stories were new — a king's story ended while his son was still a small boy. And the men who wanted the throne for themselves looked at the boy, and had him taken far away, and kept shut in a house, and meant to keep him there forever.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"एक रात, उस घर में आग लग गई। धुआँ, लपटों की दहाड़, गिरती हुई कड़ियाँ — ऐसी रात जो किसी बच्चे को कभी न देखनी पड़े। लेकिन इस बच्चे ने होश नहीं खोया। उसने एक कपड़ा भिगोया, जैसा उसे सिखाया गया था वैसे चेहरे पर लगाया, लपटों के बीच से निकलने का इकलौता रास्ता ढूँढा, और उस जलते हुए घर से ज़िंदा बाहर निकल आया। आग उसे सिर्फ एक बार छू पाई: एक पैर पर, एक ऐसा निशान छोड़ते हुए जो जीवन भर उसके साथ रहा।","lang":"hi","body":"One night, fire broke out in that house. Smoke, roaring, falling beams — a night no child should ever have. But this child kept his head. He wet a cloth, held it to his face the way he had been taught, found the one gap in the flames, and walked out of that burning house alive. The fire touched him only once: along one leg, leaving a mark he would carry all his life.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"और वही निशान उनका नाम बन गया। करि-काल: तमिल में, \"झुलसे हुए पैर वाला।\" अब — कई राजकुमार ऐसा निशान अच्छे कपड़ों के नीचे छिपा लेते। लेकिन करिकाल ने इस नाम को ज़िंदगी भर एक तमगे की तरह पहना। क्योंकि इसका मतलब \"वह लड़का जो जल गया था\" नहीं था। इसका मतलब था: \"वह लड़का जो बचकर बाहर निकल आया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And that mark became his name. Kari-kala: in Tamil, \"the one with the charred leg.\" Now — plenty of princes would have hidden a mark like that under fine cloth. Karikala wore his name like a medal, his whole life. Because it did not say \"the boy who was burned.\" It said: \"the boy who walked out.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"समय के साथ, राज्य के बुज़ुर्ग असली वारिस को खोजते हुए आए, और उन्हें ढूँढ़ निकाला, पर वे झिझक गए — क्योंकि वे तब भी बस एक बालक ही थे। वे आपस में फुसफुसाए, \"चोल देश पर कोई बच्चा राज नहीं कर सकता।\" एक पुरानी कहानी कहती है कि करिकाल ने बिना कोई बहस किए इसका जवाब दिया था।","lang":"hi","body":"In time the elders of the land came looking for the true heir, and found him, and hesitated — for he was still barely more than a child. \"The Chola country cannot be ruled by a boy,\" they murmured. One old telling says Karikala answered that without a single argument.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"दरबार में एक पेचीदा झगड़ा लाया गया — दो ज़िद्दी पक्ष, दावों का उलझा हुआ जाल, ऐसा मामला जो बड़े-बड़े अक्लमंदों के सिर में दर्द कर दे। और एक बूढ़े न्यायाधीश ने, जिन्हें कोई पहचान नहीं पा रहा था, जिनके बाल सफ़ेद थे और चाल धीमी, बड़े धैर्य से पूरी बात सुनी और उसे इतनी सफ़ाई से सुलझाया कि दोनों पक्ष ख़ुश होकर घर लौटे। फिर वह न्यायाधीश सीधे तनकर खड़े हुए, और सफ़ेद बाल उतार दिए — और मुस्कुराते हुए वही लड़का सामने था। बुज़ुर्गों ने सिर झुका लिया। सुनाते-सुनाते यह कहानी भले ही थोड़ी बढ़ गई हो, पर तमिल देश इसे हज़ार सालों से प्यार करता आया है, इसलिए यह आज भी ज़िंदा है।","lang":"hi","body":"A difficult dispute was brought to the court — two stubborn parties, a tangle of claims, the kind of case that makes wise heads ache. And an aged judge nobody quite recognised, grey-haired and slow-moving, heard it all patiently and untangled it so cleanly that both sides went home satisfied. Then the judge straightened up, and pulled off the grey — and it was the boy, smiling. The elders bowed. That telling may have grown in the telling, but Tamil country has loved it for a thousand years, so it stays.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"तो करिकाल राजा बन गए। और उनके राज्य का सबसे बड़ा मसला कोई दुश्मन या ख़ज़ाना नहीं था। वह तो एक नदी थी। कावेरी — माँ भी और आफ़त भी। वह राज्य के हर खेत को सींचती थी; और फिर, कुछ सालों में, ऐसी बाढ़ लाती कि खेत, घर और फ़सल सब एक साथ बहा ले जाती।","lang":"hi","body":"So Karikala was king. And the greatest matter in his kingdom was not an enemy or a treasury. It was the river. The Kaveri — mother and monster both. She watered every field in the land; and then, some years, she rose in flood and took the fields, the houses and the harvest all at once.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"उन्होंने पूरे देश को नदी के किनारे-किनारे बड़े-बड़े तटबंध बनाने के काम में लगा दिया, ताकि बाढ़ का पानी किनारे लग सके। और पुरानी मान्यताएँ उन्हें सबसे साहसी काम का श्रेय देती हैं: कल्लाणै — कावेरी के आर-पार तराशे हुए पत्थरों से बना एक लंबा, नीचा बाँध, जो नदी को रोकता नहीं बल्कि उसे धीरे से बाँट देता है, और उसका पानी नहरों के ज़रिए एक के बाद एक, खेत-दर-खेत फैला देता है।","lang":"hi","body":"He set the whole country to work raising great embankments along her length, so the flood had somewhere to be. And tradition credits him with the boldest stroke of all: the Kallanai — a long, low weir of fitted stone laid right across the Kaveri, which does not stop the river but gently divides her, sending her water spreading out through channels to field after field after field.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.karikala-kaveri-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.karikala-kaveri:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy With the Fire-Marked Leg","text":"आज भी उस जगह कावेरी के आर-पार पत्थरों का एक बाँध खड़ा है — इतना पुराना कि सदियों का हिसाब लगाना मुश्किल है, जिसकी बार-बार मरम्मत हुई, और वह आज भी अपना काम कर रहा है: डेल्टा के धान का पूरा इलाक़ा हर मौसम में इसी से पानी पाता है। पुरानी परंपराएँ उसकी ओर इशारा करके बस इतना कहती हैं: आग के निशान वाले पैर के उस लड़के ने इसे बनाया था।","lang":"hi","body":"A stone barrage stands across the Kaveri at that spot today — ancient beyond easy counting, repaired and repaired again, and still doing its work: the rice country of the delta drinks through it every single season. Tradition points at it and says, simply: the boy with the fire-marked leg built that.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karikala-kaveri.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karikala-kaveri|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"चित्रदुर्ग पत्थर की एक लहर जैसा किला है — कर्नाटक के सूखे दिल में, ग्रेनाइट की विशाल गोल पहाड़ियों पर चढ़ती दीवारों के सात घेरे। जिस ज़माने की यह कहानी है, दुश्मनों की एक फ़ौज महीनों से बाहर डेरा डाले पड़ी थी, एक के बाद एक दरवाज़ा तोड़ने की कोशिश कर रही थी, और हाथ कुछ नहीं आ रहा था। चित्रदुर्ग जैसे किले यूँ नहीं खुला करते।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Chitradurga is a fort like a stone wave — seven rings of wall climbing over enormous round granite hills, in the dry heart of Karnataka. In the days this story happened, an enemy army had camped outside it for months, trying gate after gate, and getting nowhere. Forts like Chitradurga do not open.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"दीवारों पर दिन-रात पहरेदार खड़े रहते थे, और उनमें से एक कहले मुद्द हनुमा नाम का आदमी था, जिसकी चौकी एक शांत कोने की रखवाली करती थी जहाँ से पत्थरों के बीच होकर एक छोटा-सा झरना बाहर निकलता था। दोपहर को उसकी पत्नी ओबव्वा ने कहला भेजा कि खाना तैयार है, और उसने अपनी पहरेदारी उस शांत कोने के ही भरोसे छोड़ दी — भला दोपहर के खाने के समय क्या हो सकता है? — और वह खाना खाने घर चला गया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"On the walls stood watchmen, day and night, and one of them was a man named Kahale Mudda Hanuma, whose post guarded a quiet corner where a stream slipped out through the rocks. At midday his wife Obavva sent word that his meal was ready, and he handed his watch to the quiet corner itself — what could happen at lunchtime? — and went home to eat.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"जब वह खाना खा रहा था, ओबव्वा पानी लाने के लिए दीवारों के पास वाले तालाब पर गई। और जैसे ही वह अपने घड़े के साथ झुकी, उसे पक्षियों के चहकने के बीच एक ऐसी आवाज़ सुनाई दी जो वहाँ की नहीं थी: एक खनक। एक फुसफुसाहट। जो पत्थरों के अंदर से आ रही थी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Obavva walked down to the pond by the walls to fetch water while he ate. And as she bent with her pot, she heard something under the birdsong that did not belong: a clink. A whisper. Coming from inside the rocks.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"वह दबे पाँव और करीब पहुँची। वहाँ, पानी की नाली के पीछे, एक किंडी थी — बड़े-बड़े पत्थरों के बीच एक दरार, बस इतनी चौड़ी कि एक आदमी रेंगकर निकल सके — और उसके अंधेरे में कुछ हिल-डुल रहा था। दरवाज़ों पर महीनों के हमलों से जो रास्ता बाहर की फ़ौज को नहीं मिला था, वह अब मिल चुका था: अंदर आने का रास्ता। और वे एक-एक करके उसी रास्ते से अंदर आ रहे थे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"She crept closer. There, behind the water channel, was a kindi — a crevice, a crack in the great stones, just wide enough for one crawling man — and in its darkness, things were moving. The army outside had found what months of attacking the gates had not: a way in. And they were coming through it, one man at a time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"उसके अगले कुछ पलों के बारे में सोचिए, क्योंकि जो कुछ भी होना था उन्हीं पलों में समाया था। अपने पति को बुलाने दौड़े — तो चंद मिनट बर्बाद, दरार बिना पहरे के छूट जाएगी, दर्जनों आदमी अंदर आ जाएँगे और दरवाज़ा भीतर से खुल जाएगा। चिल्लाए — तो दरार के अंदर वाले झपट पड़ेंगे, और बाहर वाले हमला बोल देंगे। अपने दोनों हाथों में उसने बस वही एक चीज़ थाम रखी थी जो वह अपनी रसोई से लेकर निकली थी: ओनाके — वह लंबा, भारी लकड़ी का मूसल, जिससे कन्नड़ रसोइयों में धान कूटा जाता है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Think about her next few seconds, because everything lived inside them. Run for her husband — minutes lost, the crack unwatched, a dozen men through and the gate opened from inside. Scream — and the men in the crack rush, and the men outside charge. In her two hands she held the only thing she had carried from her kitchen: the onake — the long, heavy wooden pestle that Kannada kitchens pound rice with.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"वह उस दरार के पास गर्म पत्थर से सटकर खड़ी हो गईं और पहाड़ी की तरह बिलकुल स्थिर हो गईं। अँधेरे में से धीरे-धीरे एक लोहे का टोप बाहर निकला। ओणके एक बार घूमा। वह आदमी बिना किसी आवाज़ के गिर पड़ा, और उन्होंने चुपचाप उसे एक तरफ खींच लिया — ताकि अगला आदमी, कुछ भी न सुनते हुए, आगे बढ़ता रहे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"She pressed herself against the warm stone beside the opening and went as still as the hill itself. A helmet came slowly out of the dark. The onake swung once. The man dropped without a sound, and she drew him quietly aside — so that the next man, hearing nothing, kept coming.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"और फिर अगला। और फिर अगला। ख़ामोशी ही उनका इकलौता हथियार थी। दीवार के बाहर, फ़ौज एक-एक करके सिपाहियों को दरार में भेजती रही और बाहर सिर्फ़ सन्नाटा सुनती रही, जिसे उन्होंने अपनी कामयाबी समझ लिया। अंदर, तालाब के पास, हाथ में मूसल लिए धूप में खड़ी एक अकेली औरत पूरे क़िले की पहरेदारी करती रहीं, न एक बार चीखीं, और न एक कदम पीछे हटीं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And the next. And the next. Her whole weapon was stillness. Outside the walls, the army kept feeding men into the crack and hearing only silence, which they took for success. Inside, by the pond, one woman stood in the sun with a rice pestle, guarding an entire fort alone, and did not once cry out, and did not once step back.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"जब उनके पति खाना खाकर लौटे, तो उन्होंने देखा कि उनका मोर्चा सँभाला हुआ था — हाथ में मूसल लिए, तेज़ साँसें लेती ओबव्वा द्वारा, और उनके पीछे दीवार के सहारे दुश्मन सिपाहियों की एक कतार चुपचाप पड़ी थी। उनके शंख की गूँज ने पूरे क़िले को जगा दिया। सिपाही दौड़ते हुए आए, पत्थरों से दरार को बंद कर दिया गया, और उस दिन चित्रदुर्ग हारने से बच गया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"When her husband came back from his meal he found his post held — by Obavva, pestle in hand, breathing hard, with a line of enemy soldiers lying quiet along the wall behind her. His conch blast raised the whole fort. Soldiers came running, the crack was sealed with stone, and Chitradurga did not fall that day.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.obavva-onake-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.obavva-onake:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · Obavva and the Crack in the Rocks","text":"\"पुरानी कहानियों में कहा जाता है कि ओबव्वा की अपनी कहानी उसी शाम चुपचाप पूरी हो गई, मानो उस दिन ने उनसे वह सब कुछ माँग लिया था जो उनके पास था और उन्होंने अपना सब कुछ दे दिया था। कर्नाटक ने उनका नाम लेना कभी नहीं छोड़ा।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The old tellings say Obavva's own story ended that same evening, quietly, as if the day had asked everything she had and she had given all of it. Karnataka has never stopped saying her name.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-obavva-onake.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.obavva-onake|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"\"कर्नाटक के श्रवणबेलगोला में, ग्रेनाइट की एक सूनी पहाड़ी के ऊपर, दुनिया के अजूबों में से एक खड़ा है: बाहुबली — गोमटेश्वर — एक ही पत्थर से तराशे गए, इतने ऊँचे कि लगता है बादल उनके कंधों को छू रहे हों, इतने अडिग खड़े कि मूर्तिकारों ने उनके पैरों पर लिपटी हुई बेलें भी तराश दीं। आपने शायद उनकी कहानी पहले ही सुन रखी होगी: वे वही हैं जिनकी उठी हुई मुट्ठी रुक गई थी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"At Shravanabelagola in Karnataka, on top of a bare granite hill, stands one of the wonders of the world: Bahubali — Gommateshwara — carved from a single stone, so tall that clouds seem to brush his shoulders, standing so perfectly still that the sculptors carved vines twining up his legs. You may have heard his story already: he is the one whose raised fist stopped.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"\"यह मूर्ति एक महान मंत्री चावुंडराय के आदेश पर बनाई गई थी। इसे बनने में कई साल लग गए थे, और यह इतनी भव्य थी कि चावुंडराय को भी अपनी इस कृति का पूरा एहसास था। अब पहले महामस्तकाभिषेक का दिन आ पहुँचा था — जब ऊँचे मचान से मूर्ति के सिर पर दूध डाला जाना था, ताकि वह चमकता हुआ सिर से लेकर पाँव तक पूरी विशाल मूरत पर बह निकले।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The statue was made at the order of Chavundaraya, a great minister, and it had taken years, and it was magnificent, and he knew it. Now came the day of the first anointing — when milk would be poured over the statue's head from scaffolding high above, to run shining down the whole great figure from crown to feet.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"\"बड़े-बड़े मटके ऊपर ले जाए गए — सैकड़ों मटके, जिनमें एक पूरी नदी जितना दूध था। दूध पत्थर के सिर से नीचे गिरा, कंधों के पार बहा — और रुक गया। बीच में ही। वह आगे बहने का नाम ही नहीं ले रहा था, मानो खुद पत्थर ने ही कोई दरवाज़ा बंद कर लिया हो। उन्होंने फिर से दूध डाला। वह फिर उसी जगह आकर रुक गया और अगल-बगल से रिसकर बेकार बह गया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Up went the pots — huge ones, hundreds of them, milk enough for a river. Down came the milk over the stone head, down over the shoulders — and stopped. Halfway. It simply would not flow further, as if the stone itself had closed a door. They poured again. It stopped again, at the same line, and trickled away sideways into nothing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"\"चावुंडराय ने हर उस बात की जाँच की जो एक बारीक़ी से सोचने वाला इंसान करता है। और ज़्यादा दूध। और भी शुद्ध दूध। और ऊँचे स्वर में मंत्रोच्चार, और भी पहुँचे हुए पंडित, और भी कीमती बर्तन। पहाड़ी की चोटी पर खड़ी भीड़ चुपचाप देखती रही, और दूध हर बार बीच में ही रुक जाता था, और कोई नहीं बता पा रहा था कि ऐसा क्यों हो रहा था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Chavundaraya checked everything a thorough man checks. More milk. Purer milk. Louder chanting, holier priests, costlier pots. The crowd stood hushed on the hilltop and the milk stopped halfway every single time, and nobody could say why.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"\"भीड़ के एक कोने में एक छोटी-सी बूढ़ी औरत खड़ी थीं, जिन्हें पहरेदारों ने दो बार पीछे हटा दिया था। एक अज्जि — यानी कन्नड़ में दादी माँ — जो इतनी गरीब और सीधी-सादी थीं कि किसी ने उनका नाम तक नहीं पूछा था। उन्होंने अपने दोनों हाथों में अपनी भेंट थाम रखी थी: दूध, जिसे वे एक गुल्लाकाई में पूरी लंबी पहाड़ी पर चढ़ाकर लाई थीं। गुल्लाकाई, यानी सूखी लौकी को खोखला करके बनाई गई एक छोटी-सी कटोरी। उसमें बस इतना ही दूध था, जितना शायद किसी बिल्ली के बच्चे के लिए काफी हो।\"","lang":"hi","body":"At the edge of the crowd stood a small old woman the guards had twice waved back. An ajji — a grandmother, in Kannada — so poor and so ordinary that nobody had asked her name. In her two hands she held her offering: milk, carried up the whole long hill in a gullakayi, a little cup made from a hollowed gourd shell. Enough milk, perhaps, for a kitten.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"\"वे ऊपर पहुँचीं, अपनी साँस संभाली, और अपनी छोटी-सी लौकी की कटोरी को मूर्ति के सिर पर उलट दिया — और दूध बह निकला। कंधों के ऊपर से। बाहों से नीचे, तराशी गई लताओं को पार करता हुआ, पाँवों के ऊपर से, जब तक कि वह पूरी विशालकाय मूरत सिर से लेकर पाँव तक चमकने न लगी। आखिरकार अभिषेक पूरा हुआ — वो भी सिर्फ करीब आधी कटोरी दूध से।\"","lang":"hi","body":"She reached the top, caught her breath, tipped her little gourd cup over the statue's head — and the milk flowed. Over the shoulders. Down the arms, down past the carved vines, over the feet, until the whole colossal figure stood gleaming from crown to toe, anointed at last — by about half a cup of milk.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"\"चावुंडराय भीड़ में खड़े थे और एक ही पल में समझ गए कि वह पत्थर उनसे क्या कह रहा था। जिस महापुरुष ने अपना सब कुछ त्याग दिया था, उनकी मूरत एक अमीर आदमी के घमंड की बस एक और चीज़ बनने जा रही थी। उनके दूध में अहंकार भरा था। जबकि अज्जि के दूध में सिर्फ और सिर्फ प्यार — और प्यार, सचमुच, सबसे आगे तक बहता है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Chavundaraya stood in the crowd and understood, all at once, what the stone had been telling him. The statue of the man who gave up everything had been about to become one more thing for a rich man to be proud of. His milk was full of pride. Hers was full of nothing but love — and love, it turns out, flows further.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gullakayajji-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gullakayajji:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Grandmother's Little Cup of Milk","text":"महामंत्री उस छोटी-सी दादी अम्मा के पास चलकर गए और सबके सामने पूरी तरह झुककर उन्हें प्रणाम किया। यह उनके जीवन का दूसरा सबसे समझदारी भरा काम था। और सबसे बड़ी समझदारी तो इसके बाद हुई: उन्होंने यह पक्का किया कि कोई उन्हें कभी न भूले।","lang":"hi","body":"The great minister walked over to the small grandmother and bowed to her, all the way down, in front of everyone. It was the second wisest thing he ever did. The wisest was what came next: he made sure nobody ever forgot her.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gullakayajji.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gullakayajji|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"कोडगु की धुंध से घिरी ऊँची पहाड़ियों में — यानी कर्नाटक के कॉफ़ी वाले इलाके कूर्ग में — पहाड़ की ढलान पर एक छोटा-सा जलस्रोत है जिसे तलकावेरी कहते हैं: \"कावेरी का सिर।\" इतनी बड़ी पूरी नदी वहीं से शुरू होती है, एक ऐसे छोटे-से कुंड से जिसे आप बस एक कदम में पार कर सकते हैं। और यह कहानी है कि वह वहाँ से बाहर कैसे निकलीं।","lang":"hi","body":"High in the misty hills of Kodagu — Coorg, the coffee country of Karnataka — there is a small spring on a mountainside called Talakaveri: \"the head of the Kaveri.\" A whole great river starts there, in a pool you could step across. And this is the story of how she got out.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"ऋषि अगस्त्य — दक्षिण के वे छोटे-से मगर पराक्रमी ऋषि, जिनसे आप इन कहानियों में फिर मिलेंगे — अपने साथ कमंडल रखते थे, वही छोटा-सा जलपात्र जो घूमते-फिरते ऋषि अपने पास रखते हैं। और कथा कहती है कि उस पात्र में कोई साधारण जल नहीं था। वह खुद कावेरी थीं: एक ऐसी देवी जिन्होंने अपने मन से नदी बनना चुना था, ताकि दक्षिण कभी भूखा न रहे।","lang":"hi","body":"The sage Agastya — the small, mighty sage of the south, whom you will meet again in these stories — carried a kamandalu, the little water-pot that wandering sages carry. And in that pot, the legend says, was no ordinary water. It was Kaveri herself: a goddess who had chosen, of her own free heart, to become a river, so that the south would never go hungry.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"मगर नदी तो बस एक ही बार बहाई जा सकती है, इसलिए यह काम बिल्कुल ठीक होना चाहिए था — और अगस्त्य इसे एकदम सही ढंग से करना चाहते थे। बिल्कुल सही पहाड़ी। बिल्कुल सही घड़ी। वे हाथ में कमंडल लिए पहाड़ियों में घूमते रहे — कभी कुछ चुनते और कभी छोड़ देते, फिर सोचते और फिर मना कर देते — जबकि कमंडल के अंदर, बहने के इंतज़ार में देवी बस राह देखती रहीं, देखती रहीं, और देखती रहीं।","lang":"hi","body":"But a river can only be poured out once, so it must be done right — and Agastya meant to do it perfectly. The perfect hillside. The perfect hour. He walked the hills with the pot in his hand, considering and rejecting, considering and rejecting, while inside the pot a goddess waited, and waited, and waited to run.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"और नीचे ज़मीन में दरारें पड़ रही थीं। बारिश बार-बार दगा दे रही थी। किसान उन धूल भरे मैदानों में खड़े आसमान को ताक रहे थे, जो कभी खेत हुआ करते थे। दक्षिण अब प्यासा था — और सब कुछ एकदम सही होने में तो बरसों बीत रहे थे।","lang":"hi","body":"And down below, the land was cracking. The rains had failed and failed again. Farmers stood in dust that had been fields, looking at the sky. The south was thirsty now — and perfect was taking years.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"\"गणेश यह सब देख रहे थे — वह कमंडल, इंतज़ार करती देवी, और सूखी फटी ज़मीन — और गणेश, जिन्हें टेढ़े-मेढ़े तरीक़े से काम बनाना पसंद है, उन्होंने तय किया कि इंतज़ार अब बहुत हो चुका। उन्होंने खुद को छोटा, पंखों वाला और काला बना लिया: एक नन्हा-सा कौआ।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Ganesha had been watching all of this — the pot, the waiting goddess, the cracked fields — and Ganesha, who loves solving things sideways, decided the waiting had gone on exactly long enough. He made himself small, and feathered, and black: a little crow.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"\"जब ऋषि प्रार्थना में मग्न खड़े थे, कौआ फड़फड़ाता हुआ नीचे उतरा, एक डाल पर फुदका, कमंडल के किनारे पर आराम से बैठा — और उसे उलट ही दिया। पानी छलककर पहाड़ी पर बह निकला।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The crow flapped down while the sage stood in prayer, hopped along a branch, perched neatly on the rim of the kamandalu — and tipped it right over. The water leapt out onto the hillside.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"\"उस मनहूस पक्षी को उड़ाने के लिए अगस्त्य हाथ उठाए तेज़ी से घूमे — तभी वह कौआ एक गोल-मटोल, मुस्कुराता हुआ बालक बन गया, और ऋषि का हाथ हवा में ही थम गया। गणेश! वे लगभग गणेश पर ही हाथ चला बैठे थे। शर्मिंदा होकर, ऋषि ने माफ़ी माँगते हुए अपनी ही उँगलियों के पोरों से अपनी कनपटी पर धीरे-धीरे ठोका — और गणेश खिलखिलाकर हँस पड़े। आज भी लोग गणेश जी के आगे सिर पर अपनी उँगलियों से धीरे से ठक-ठक करते हैं, और यह कहानी कहती है कि इसकी शुरुआत यहीं से हुई थी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Agastya spun round with his hand raised to shoo the wretched bird — and the crow became a round, grinning boy, and the sage froze mid-swing. Ganesha. He had very nearly swatted Ganesha. Mortified, the sage rapped his own knuckles against his own temples in apology — and Ganesha laughed with delight. To this day, people knock their knuckles gently on their heads before Ganesha, and this story claims that is where it began.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kaveri-pot-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kaveri-pot:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River in the Sage's Pot","text":"\"और वह छलका हुआ पानी? वह घास से ऊपर उठा, हँसा, एक कन्या बना, एक धारा बना, और नाचता हुआ पहाड़ी से नीचे उतर गया — कावेरी, आखिरकार आज़ाद, समंदर की ओर दौड़ती हुई। कोडगु की पहाड़ियों से होते हुए, कर्नाटक के फैले हुए खेतों के पार, आगे तमिल देश में — वही नदी, जिस पर आग से झुलसे पैर वाला एक बालक राजा एक दिन पत्थर की दीवार बाँधेगा, ताकि उसका पानी सबको बाँट सके।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And the spilled water? It rose from the grass, and laughed, and became a girl, and became a stream, and went dancing away downhill — Kaveri, free at last, running for the sea. Through the hills of Kodagu, out across the wide fields of Karnataka, on into Tamil country — the very river a boy king with a fire-marked leg would one day lay a stone wall across, to share her out.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kaveri-pot.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kaveri-pot|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"\"कन्नड़ देश के एक गाँव में एक औरत रहती थी जिसे बस एक ही कहानी और एक ही गीत याद था — उसकी अपनी नानी ने कान में फुसफुसाकर उसे ये ऐसे सौंपे थे, जैसे कोई गहने सौंपता है। और जाने कैसे, उसने कभी किसी को वे नहीं सुनाए। कभी खाना पकाना था। कभी भैंस को देखना था। और कल का दिन तो हमेशा ही था। वह कहानी और वह गीत बरसों तक उसके भीतर ही बंद रहे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"In a village in the Kannada country there lived a woman who knew exactly one story and one song — her own grandmother had given them to her, mouth to ear, the way you hand over jewellery. And somehow, she had never once told them. There was cooking. There was the buffalo. There was always tomorrow. The story and the song stayed shut inside her for years.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"अब सुनो, यह एक ऐसी बात है जो हर कोई नहीं जानता। कहानियों और गानों में जान होती है, और अनकही कहानी का तो दम घुटने लगता है। उसकी कहानी और गाना उसके अंदर ऐसे बैठे थे जैसे किसी बहुत छोटे पिंजरे में दो चिड़ियाँ — पंख समेटे, छटपटाते हुए, सालों-साल — जब तक कि एक रात उनका सब्र बिल्कुल टूट नहीं गया।","lang":"hi","body":"Now, here is a thing not everybody knows. Stories and songs are alive, and an untold story gets cramped. Hers sat inside her like two birds in a cage gone too small — wings folded, fidgeting, year after year — until one night they had simply had enough.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"उस रात वह मुँह खोलकर सोई। और वे दोनों साँस की तरह चुपके से, एक के बाद एक, बाहर फिसल आए। मगर दुनिया में बाहर निकली कहानी को कुछ न कुछ तो बनना ही था — सो वह कहानी दरवाज़े के पास खूँटी पर टंग गई और किसी आदमी का कोट बन गई। और गाना नीचे कूदकर एक जोड़ी जूते बन गया।","lang":"hi","body":"That night she slept with her mouth open. And out they slipped, one after the other, quiet as breath. But a story that is out in the world must be something — so the story hung itself up on the peg by the door and became a man's coat. And the song hopped down below it and became a pair of shoes.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"उसका पति खेतों से देर से घर लौटा, उसने दीया उठाया — और ठिठक कर खड़ा रह गया। \"यह किसका कोट है?\" उसकी खूँटी पर एक अजनबी कोट! उसके नीचे अजनबी जूते! इतनी रात गए कोई मेहमान, और वह था कहाँ? \"ये सब आख़िर हैं किसके?\" और उसकी पत्नी ने, आँखें मलते हुए जागकर, सबसे बुरा सच कह दिया: \"मुझे ज़रा भी नहीं पता।\" और आप भी मानेंगे कि इससे बात सुलझने के बजाय और उलझ गई।","lang":"hi","body":"Her husband came home late from the fields, lifted the lamp — and stopped dead. \"Whose coat is this?\" A strange coat on his peg. Strange shoes beneath it. A visitor, at this hour, and where was he? \"Whose ARE these?\" And his wife, blinking awake, gave the worst possible true answer: \"I have no idea.\" Which, you will agree, did not help.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"वह इतना आहत था कि चिल्ला भी न सका। उसने अपना कंबल उठाया और अंधेरे में पैर पटकते हुए गली के नुक्कड़ पर बने हनुमान जी के मंदिर में सोने चला गया — उस गाँव के मर्द जब सचमुच नाराज़ होते थे, तो ऐसे ही रूठते थे। उसकी पत्नी रात भर जागी रही, उस कोट को एकटक देखती रही जो उसने पहले कभी नहीं देखा था, उस घर में जो अचानक बिल्कुल ठंडा पड़ गया था।","lang":"hi","body":"He was too hurt to shout. He took his blanket and stalked off through the dark to sleep in the Monkey God's temple at the end of the street, the way men in that village sulked when sulking was serious. His wife lay awake, staring at a coat she had never seen, in a house gone suddenly cold.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"मगर गली के नुक्कड़ वाला मंदिर रात में खाली नहीं था। अरे नहीं! उसी मंदिर में तो सारी लौ इकट्ठी होती थीं। गाँव का हर दीया जब रात को बुझाया जाता है, तो उसकी लौ मरती नहीं है — वह चुपके से मंदिर चली जाती है ताकि दूसरी लौ के साथ देर रात तक बैठकर अपने-अपने घरों की गपशप कर सके। और अब वे एक के बाद एक नन्हीं लौ बनकर अंदर आने लगीं, और गौरैयों की तरह कतारों में बैठ गईं।","lang":"hi","body":"But the temple at the end of the street was not empty at night. Oh no. That temple was where the flames went. When every lamp in the village is blown out for the night, its flame does not die — it slips off to the temple to sit up late with the other flames, gossiping about their households. And in they came now, flame after little flame, settling in rows like sparrows.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"उसके अपने घर की लौ सबसे आख़िर में आई, और बाक़ी सब उस पर टूट पड़ीं। \"फिर से देर कर दी! कहाँ रह गई थी?\" \"हमारे यहाँ तो आज रात क्या ही गज़ब हो गया,\" नन्हीं लौ ने ठंडी आह भरते हुए कहा। \"तुम तो जानती ही हो कि मेरी मालकिन ने एक कहानी और एक गाने को अंदर बंद कर रखा था और कभी किसी को नहीं सुनाती थी? आज रात जब वह सो रही थी, तो वे दोनों भाग निकले — एक खूँटी पर टंगकर कोट बन गया है, दूसरा जूते बन गया है — और अब मालिक को लग रहा है कि कोई अजनबी आया है, और वे यहीं इसी मंदिर में आकर रूठे बैठे हैं, और उन दो भगोड़ों की वजह से सब दुखी हैं।\" सारी लौ चाचियों-मौसियों की तरह 'च-च-च' करने लगीं।","lang":"hi","body":"His own house's flame came in last, and the others pounced. \"Late again! What kept you?\" \"What a night at ours,\" sighed the little flame. \"You know my mistress keeps a story and a song locked up and never tells them? Tonight they escaped while she slept — one is hanging on the peg being a coat, the other is being shoes — and now the master thinks a stranger has come, and he is sulking right here in this temple, and everyone is miserable over two runaways.\" The flames all clucked like aunties.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"और एक खंभे के पीछे, ठोड़ी तक कंबल ताने पूरी तरह जागा हुआ एक पति बिल्कुल चुपचाप लेटा था, और अपने ही दीये को अपनी शादी की बातें करते सुन रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"And behind a pillar, one wide-awake husband lay very still with his blanket to his chin, listening to his own lamp discuss his marriage.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.story-song-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.story-song:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Story That Wanted Out","text":"सुबह उसने पूछा, और वह शुरू हुई — पहले धीरे-धीरे, बात टटोलते हुए, फिर तेज़ी से, और फिर चेहरे पर चमक लिए — और जब उसने कहानी सुनाई और आख़िरकार वह गीत गाया, तो उसे याद आया कि उसकी दादी की आवाज़ ठीक एक ही जगह पर हमेशा धीमी क्यों पड़ जाती थी। जब उसने बात ख़त्म की, तो दरवाज़े के पास वाली खूंटी खाली थी। कोट और जूते दोनों ग़ायब — घर, उसी कहानी में, जहाँ वे रहते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"In the morning he asked, and she began — slowly at first, finding it, then faster, then glowing — and as she told the story and sang the song at last, she remembered why her grandmother's voice had always gone soft in exactly one place. When she finished, the peg by the door was empty. Coat and shoes both gone — home, into the telling, where they lived.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-story-song.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.story-song|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"आंध्र के हरे-भरे डेल्टा वाले इलाके के तेनाली शहर में, राम नाम का एक लड़का रहता था — गरीब, बिना पिता का, और ज़बान इतनी तेज़ कि दिन में कम से कम एक बार तो उसकी माँ उससे तंग आ ही जाती थी। आगे चलकर दुनिया उसे दक्षिण भारत के सबसे मज़ाकिया इंसान, तेनाली रामन के नाम से जानने वाली थी। लेकिन उससे पहले, यह होना बाकी था।","lang":"hi","body":"In the town of Tenali, in the green delta country of Andhra, there lived a boy called Rama — poor, fatherless, and with a tongue so quick that his mother despaired of him at least once a day. The world would come to know him as Tenali Raman, the funniest man in the south. But first, this had to happen.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"लड़के की हाज़िरजवाबी से खुश होकर एक घूमते-फिरते साधु ने उसे एक पावन मंत्र सिखाया, और उससे कहा: आधी रात को अकेले महान देवी काली के मंदिर जाना, और इसका जाप करना, और चाहे कुछ भी हो जाए, रुकना मत। ज़्यादातर लोग तो इसकी कोशिश भी नहीं करते। पर वह लड़का उसी रात चला गया।","lang":"hi","body":"A wandering holy man, amused by the boy's wit, taught him a sacred chant, and told him: go alone to the temple of the great goddess Kali at midnight, and recite it, and do not stop, whatever happens. Most people would not even try. The boy went that same night.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"अंधेरे मंदिर में अकेला, थोड़ा डरा हुआ — और सच तो यह है कि उस घड़ी डर लगना लाज़िमी ही था — वह बैठ गया और मंत्र जपने लगा, और रुका नहीं। दीपक फड़फड़ाए। अंधेरा और गहरा हो गया। वह जपता रहा। और ठीक आधी रात को, पूरा मंदिर भर गया।","lang":"hi","body":"Alone in the dark temple, a little afraid — which is the only honest way to be — he sat down and chanted, and did not stop. The lamps guttered. The dark got thick. He chanted. And at midnight, the temple filled.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"साक्षात माँ प्रकट हुईं — विशाल और तेजोमय, उनके इतने सारे चेहरे थे कि मंदिर के हर दीये को रोशन करने के लिए एक चेहरा मिल गया था। यह ऐसा नज़ारा था जिसे देखकर बड़े-बड़े शूरवीर ज़मीन पर लोट-पोट हो जाएँ। लड़के ने ऊपर उनकी ओर देखा, उन सारे भव्य चेहरों को देखा — और खिलखिला पड़ा। फिर उसने झट से अपने मुँह पर हाथ रख लिया। और फिर से हँस पड़ा। वह अपनी हँसी रोक ही नहीं पा रहा था।","lang":"hi","body":"The great Mother came — vast and shining, with so many faces that every lamp in the temple found one to light. It is the kind of sight that flattens grown heroes to the floor. The boy looked up at her, all those magnificent faces — and giggled. And clapped his hand over his mouth. And giggled again. He could not stop.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"देवी सचमुच हैरान रह गईं। ऋषि-मुनि उनके आगे कांपते हैं। राजा बेहोश हो जाते हैं। कोई भी — युगों-युगों में कभी कोई भी — यूँ नहीं हँसता। \"बच्चे,\" वे बोलीं, और उनकी आवाज़ में जैसे पूरा मंदिर गूँज उठा, \"आखिर ऐसी कौन-सी हँसी की बात है?\"","lang":"hi","body":"The goddess was genuinely astonished. Sages tremble before her. Kings faint. Nobody — nobody in all the ages — giggles. \"Child,\" she said, and her voice was the temple itself, \"what, exactly, is funny?\"","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"लड़के ने जैसे-तैसे खुद को संभाला। \"अम्मा,\" उसने कहा, \"मुझे माफ़ कर दीजिए। लेकिन जब हम लोगों को ज़ुकाम होता है, तो संभालने के लिए सिर्फ एक नाक होती है, और वही हमें रात भर जगाए रखती है। और मैं आपके इन सारे सुंदर चेहरों को देखकर सोच रहा था — अम्मा, जब आपको ज़ुकाम होता होगा, तो आप कैसे संभालती होंगी?\" वहाँ एक गहरा सन्नाटा छा गया। और फिर पूरा मंदिर किसी घंटी की तरह गूँज उठा, क्योंकि देवी हँस रही थीं।","lang":"hi","body":"The boy pulled himself together, mostly. \"Amma,\" he said, \"forgive me. But when we people catch a cold, we have just one nose to manage, and it keeps us up all night. And I was looking at all your beautiful faces and thinking — Amma, when YOU catch a cold, however do you cope?\" There was a terrible silence. And then the temple rang like a bell, because the goddess was laughing.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"अपनी आँखें पोंछते हुए उन्होंने दो कटोरे आगे बढ़ाए। \"तुमने एक उपहार कमाया है। यह ज्ञान का दूध है — इसे पियो और सबसे बड़े विद्वान बन जाओ। यह दौलत का दही है — इसे पियो और तुम्हें कभी किसी चीज़ की कमी नहीं होगी। एक को चुनो, बच्चे। सिर्फ एक को।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Wiping her eyes, she held out two bowls. \"You have earned a gift. This is the milk of learning — drink it and be the wisest of scholars. This is the curd of riches — drink it and want for nothing. Choose one, child. Only one.\"","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"वह नाराज़ हो सकती थीं। कोई और होता तो शायद हो भी जाता। लेकिन उन्होंने इस अनोखे बच्चे को देखा — जो एक कटोरे के हिसाब से कुछ ज़्यादा ही होशियार था, और इतना सच्चा था कि दिखावा भी न कर सका — और उसे इसके बदले एक निराला और कहीं बेहतर वरदान दिया। \"तुम न तो किसी विद्वान जितने गंभीर हो पाओगे, न किसी अमीर जितने लालची। तुम इन दोनों से भी कुछ अलग बनोगे: एक विकटकवि। एक विदूषक-कवि।\"","lang":"hi","body":"She could have been angry. Anyone else, she might have been. But she looked at this impossible child — too clever for one bowl, too honest to pretend he wasn't — and gave him instead a stranger and better blessing. \"You will never be solemn enough for a scholar nor greedy enough for a rich man. You will be rarer than either: a vikatakavi. A jester-poet.\"","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-vikatakavi-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-vikatakavi:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Boy Who Made the Goddess Laugh","text":"\"और इस शब्द पर ध्यान देना, बच्चे,\" दीये की रोशनी में ओझल होते हुए उन्होंने कहा। \"वि-क-ट-क-वि। इसे आगे से पढ़ो या पीछे से, यह एक जैसा ही पढ़ा जाता है — बिल्कुल तुम्हारी तरह। किसी को कभी ठीक-ठीक पता नहीं चलेगा कि तुम्हारा रुख़ किस तरफ़ है।\" और आगे चलकर वह बिल्कुल वही बना।","lang":"hi","body":"\"And note the word, child,\" she said, already fading into the lamplight. \"Vi-ka-ta-ka-vi. It reads the same forwards and backwards — like you. Nobody will ever be quite sure which way you are facing.\" And that is precisely what he became.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-vikatakavi.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-vikatakavi|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"कृष्णदेवराय के महान दरबार में एक बाहरी विद्वान आया, जिसके पीछे नौकरों और प्रमाणपत्रों का ताँता लगा था। उसने ऐलान किया कि उसने पहाड़ों से लेकर समुद्र तक, हर राज्य के सबसे बड़े बुद्धिमानों को हराया है, और वह वहाँ मौजूद किसी भी इंसान से आज तक लिखी गई किसी भी किताब पर शास्त्रार्थ करने को तैयार है। किसी भी किताब पर। उसने पूरे दरबार पर एक मुस्कान बिखेरी और इंतज़ार करने लगा।","lang":"hi","body":"A visiting scholar arrived at the great court of Krishnadevaraya, trailing servants and certificates. He had, he announced, defeated the best minds of every kingdom from the mountains to the sea, and he would debate anyone present on any book ever written. Any book at all. He smiled around the hall and waited.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"दरबार के अपने विद्वान ज़मीन की तरफ़ देखने लगे। हर कोई कुछ किताबों का तो उस्ताद था — पर सारी किताबों का ज्ञाता कोई नहीं होता, और इस आदमी के सामने एक छोटी-सी चूक का मतलब था ज़िंदगी भर के लिए हँसी का पात्र बन जाना। सन्नाटा खिंचता चला गया। राजा के कान धीरे-धीरे लाल पड़ने लगे। और तभी एक जानी-पहचानी आवाज़ बड़े इत्मीनान से बोली, \"अरे, मैं करूँगा इनसे शास्त्रार्थ।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The court's own scholars looked at their feet. Each was master of some books — nobody is master of every book, and one slip against this man meant being laughed out of the profession. The silence stretched. The king's ears slowly went red. And then a familiar voice said, comfortably: \"Oh, I'll debate him.\"","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"तेनाली रामन ने पूरी विनम्रता के साथ शर्तें रखीं: कल सुबह-सवेरे, भरे दरबार के सामने, किसी एक महान ग्रंथ पर चर्चा होगी — और उस किताब का नाम वे शास्त्रार्थ के समय ही बताएँगे। उस विद्वान ने, जिसने सब कुछ पढ़ रखा था, ज़रा झुककर और चेहरे पर एक घमंडी मुस्कान लाकर चुनौती स्वीकार कर ली।","lang":"hi","body":"Tenali Raman proposed the terms, all courtesy: tomorrow at dawn, before the full court, on a single great classic — a book he would name at the debate itself. The scholar, who had read everything, accepted with a small bow and a large smirk.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"उस रात तेनाली घर गए और बड़े जतन से तैयारी की। पढ़ाई करके नहीं। उन्होंने तिल के सूखे डंठलों का एक गट्ठर लिया और भैंस बाँधने वाली रस्सी का एक टुकड़ा, फिर उस पूरे पुलिंदे को बड़ी शान से कढ़ाईदार रेशम में लपेटा, और अपने आप से बेहद खुश होकर जल्दी सो गए।","lang":"hi","body":"That night Tenali went home and prepared with enormous care. Not by reading. He took a bundle of dried sesame stalks and a length of the rope used for tying up buffaloes, wrapped the whole thing grandly in embroidered silk, and went to bed early, extremely pleased with himself.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"सुबह होते ही वे रेशम की पोटली को बड़े आदर के साथ थामे दरबार में पहुँचे और उसे शास्त्रार्थ की मेज़ पर रख दिया। \"आज के शास्त्रार्थ का ग्रंथ,\" उन्होंने ऐलान किया, \"वह महान कालजयी रचना — तिलकाष्ठ महिष बंधन।\" विद्वान के चेहरे की मुस्कान तो वैसी ही रही, मगर उनकी आँखें एकदम ठहर गईं। उन्होंने यह नाम कभी सुना ही नहीं था। और वे यह बात कह भी नहीं सकते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"At dawn he swept into court bearing the silk bundle with tremendous reverence and laid it on the debating table. \"The text for today's debate,\" he announced, \"that towering classic — Tilakashta Mahisha Bandhana.\" The scholar's smile stayed put while his eyes went completely still. He had never heard of it. And he could not say so.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"ज़रा देखिए उन्हें सोचते हुए, क्योंकि उनकी पूरी ज़िंदगी उन्हें इसी कोने में ला पटकी थी। इतनी डींगें हाँकने के बाद, सबके सामने अपनी अज्ञानता स्वीकार करें? सोचा भी नहीं जा सकता था। और जिस किताब को कभी देखा तक न हो, उस पर बहस करें? नामुमकिन।","lang":"hi","body":"Watch him think, because his whole life had led him into this corner. Admit ignorance, in public, after that boast? Unthinkable. Debate a book he had never seen? Impossible.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"पूरी रात वह विद्वान अपनी किताबों के संदूक और अपनी याददाश्त का कोना-कोना खंगालता रहा। कुछ नहीं। साहित्य की पूरी दुनिया में कहीं कोई तिलकाष्ठ महिष बंधन नहीं था। और जब सुबह दरबार लगा, तो उस महापंडित का कमरा खाली था और उनका घोड़ा गायब — वे पहली किरण फूटने से पहले ही अपनी सनदें समेटकर रफ़ूचक्कर हो चुके थे।","lang":"hi","body":"All night the scholar ransacked his trunks of books and his palace of a memory. Nothing. No Tilakashta Mahisha Bandhana anywhere in the world of letters. And when the court gathered at dawn, the great man's rooms were empty and his horse was gone — he had ridden out before first light, certificates and all.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"राजा ने कहा कि कम से कम उन्हें यह मशहूर किताब देखनी तो है। तेनाली ने बड़े ठाठ से रेशमी कपड़ा खोला: तिल के डंठल और भैंस बाँधने वाली रस्सी। \"महाराज — सीधे-सादे शब्दों में: तिल यानी तिल के डंठल। काष्ठ यानी सूखी लकड़ी। महिष बंधन यानी भैंस बाँधने की रस्सी। यह नाम तो बस सबको यह बता रहा था कि इस पोटली में क्या है। किसी ने पूछने की सोची ही नहीं।\" पूरा दरबार हफ़्ते भर ठहाके लगाता रहा।","lang":"hi","body":"The king demanded to at least see this legendary book. Tenali untied the silk with great ceremony: sesame stalks and a buffalo rope. \"Maharaja — in plain words: tila, sesame stalks. Kashta, dry wood. Mahisha bandhana, a rope for tying a buffalo. The title was simply telling everyone what was in the parcel. Nobody thought to ask.\" The court roared for a week.","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-wt.tenali-book-8":{"src":"passage:hi:story:wt.tenali-book:8","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Book That Did Not Exist","text":"\"लेकिन अगर उसने इसे खोल लिया होता तो?\" राजा ने अपनी आँखों से आँसू पोंछते हुए पूछा। \"महाराज,\" तेनाली बोले, \"जो इंसान यह नहीं कह सकता कि 'मुझे नहीं पता', वह कभी कुछ नहीं खोलेगा। मेरी बहस तो इसी बात पर थी। और मैं जीत गया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"\"But what if he had opened it?\" asked the king, wiping his eyes. \"Maharaja,\" said Tenali, \"a man who cannot say I don't know will never open anything. That is what I was debating. I won.\"","art":"art/story/sm/wt-tenali-book.jpg","route":"#/story/wt.tenali-book|h8","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Clever Ones"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"तेलुगु देश में कभी परमानंदय्या नाम के एक गुरु रहते थे, बड़े ही असीम धैर्य वाले इंसान — और यह अच्छा ही था, क्योंकि उनके दस शिष्य बड़े आज्ञाकारी, मेहनती और गज़ब के बेवकूफ़ थे। तेलुगु दादियाँ सैकड़ों सालों से उन पर हँसती आ रही हैं, और अभी तक उनकी हँसी थमी नहीं है।","lang":"hi","body":"In the Telugu country there once lived a guru called Paramanandayya, a man of truly bottomless patience — which was fortunate, because his ten disciples were devoted, hardworking, and magnificently, dazzlingly foolish. Telugu grandmothers have been laughing about them for hundreds of years, and are not finished yet.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"एक दिन गुरुजी ने उन्हें नदी पार अगले गाँव किसी काम से भेजा। \"तुम दसों एक साथ जाना,\" उन्होंने धीरे-धीरे और साफ़ शब्दों में कहा, \"और तुम दसों एक साथ ही वापस आना। खुद को गिन लेना। दस।\" शिष्य नदी तक पूरे रास्ते यही दोहराते रहे: दस, दस, दस।","lang":"hi","body":"One day the guru sent them on an errand to the next village, across the river. \"All ten of you go together,\" he said slowly and clearly, \"and all ten of you come back together. Count yourselves. Ten.\" The disciples repeated it all the way to the water: ten, ten, ten.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"वे एक-दूसरे का हाथ थामे, किलकारियाँ मारते हुए पानी में से होकर नदी पार कर गए, और भीगते-टपकते उस पार किनारे पर पहुँचे। और सबसे बड़े शिष्य ने, ज़िम्मेदार होने के नाते, सबको एक कतार में खड़ा किया और सिर गिने: एक, दो, तीन, चार, पाँच, छह, सात, आठ — नौ। उसके हाथ-पाँव ठंडे पड़ गए। उसने फिर गिना। नौ!","lang":"hi","body":"They waded across the river, holding hands and squealing, and climbed out dripping on the far bank. And the eldest, being responsible, lined everyone up and counted heads: one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight — nine. He went cold. He counted again. Nine!","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"बारी-बारी से हर चेले ने गिनती की, और बड़े ध्यान से हर किसी का सिर गिना—सिवाय अपने खुद के सिर के, जिस पर उसकी आँखें थीं। नौ। नौ। दसों बार गिनती नौ ही आई। अब इसका एक ही मतलब हो सकता था: नदी उनके दसवें भाई को बहा ले गई थी। और वे दसों के दस किनारे पर एक कतार में बैठकर उसके लिए रोने लगे—जिसके लिए, ध्यान दीजिए, पूरे दस लोगों की ज़रूरत पड़ी।","lang":"hi","body":"Each disciple took a turn counting, and each, with perfect care, counted every head except the one his own eyes lived in. Nine. Nine. Nine, ten times over. There was only one possible explanation: the river had taken their tenth brother. And all ten of them sat down in a row on the bank and wept for him — which, you will notice, took ten people.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"रास्ते से गुज़रते एक मुसाफ़िर ने देखा कि दस हट्टे-कट्टे आदमी कतार में बैठकर रो रहे हैं। \"कौन चल बसा?\" \"हमारा भाई! हम दस थे, और अब नौ ही बचे हैं—आप खुद गिन लीजिए!\" मुसाफ़िर ने धीरे-धीरे पूरी कतार पर नज़र घुमाई, उसके होंठ हिले, और उसके मुँह के कोने में हल्की-सी मुस्कुराहट थिरकने लगी। \"मान लो,\" उसने गंभीर होकर कहा, \"अगर मैं तुम्हारा दसवाँ आदमी ढूँढ़ दूँ, तो तुम मुझे क्या दोगे?\" \"हमारी पोटलियों में जो कुछ भी है, सब कुछ!\"","lang":"hi","body":"A traveller came down the road and found ten grown men weeping in a row. \"Who has died?\" \"Our brother! We were ten, and now we are nine — count for yourself!\" The traveller looked slowly along the line, and his lips moved, and something began to twitch at the corner of his mouth. \"Suppose,\" he said gravely, \"I find your tenth man. What will you give me?\" \"Everything in our bundles!\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"मेरी जानी-पहचानी कहानी में, उसने हर आदमी से नदी की गीली मिट्टी में अपने अँगूठे का निशान लगाने को कहा, और फिर पीछे हट गया। \"अब इन अँगूठों के निशानों को गिनो।\" एक, दो, तीन... आठ, नौ—दस। दस निशान! चेले खुशी से चिल्लाते हुए उछल पड़े, मुसाफ़िर के गले लगे, एक-दूसरे को गले लगाया, और नदी से अपने भाई को वापस लौटाने के लिए भगवान का शुक्रिया अदा किया।","lang":"hi","body":"In the telling I know best, he had each man press one thumbprint into the wet river clay, then stood back. \"Now count the thumbprints.\" One, two, three... eight, nine — TEN. Ten thumbprints! The disciples leapt up whooping, embraced the traveller, embraced each other, and thanked the heavens for returning their brother from the river.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"उन्होंने अपनी पोटलियाँ मुसाफ़िर को थमानी चाहीं, लेकिन उसने सिर्फ घूँट भर पानी पिया और आगे चल पड़ा, और बाकी पूरा दिन अपनी हँसी दबाते हुए हल्के-हल्के हिलता रहा। उधर चेले अपने गुरुजी को इस हादसे और चमत्कार की बात बताने के लिए जल्दी-जल्दी घर की तरफ दौड़े।","lang":"hi","body":"They pressed their bundles on the traveller, who took only a drink of water and walked on, shaking gently for the rest of the day. And the disciples hurried home to tell their guru of the tragedy and the miracle.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.paramananda-count-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.paramananda-count:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Disciple Who Was Always Missing","text":"परमानंदय्या ने आँखें मूँदकर सारी बात सुनी—डूबने की, रोने-धोने की, उस भले अजनबी की, और उन दस अँगूठों के निशानों की। उन्होंने एक लंबी, धीमी साँस ली, वैसी साँस जो हँसी को अंदर ही दबा देती है ताकि वह छूट न जाए। \"और वह दसवाँ आदमी,\" उन्होंने पूछा, \"था कौन?\" \"यही तो चमत्कार है, गुरुजी! वह कभी सामने ही नहीं आया—और फिर भी मिट्टी में उसके अँगूठे का निशान था!\"","lang":"hi","body":"Paramanandayya heard it all with his eyes closed — the drowning, the weeping, the wonderful stranger, the ten thumbprints. He took one long, slow breath, the kind that holds a laugh down where it cannot escape. \"And who,\" he asked, \"was the tenth man?\" \"That is the miracle, guruji! He never appeared — and yet his thumbprint was in the clay!\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-paramananda-count.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.paramananda-count|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"बहुत पुरानी बात है, शहर बसने से भी बहुत पहले, आंध्र की नदी स्वर्णमुखी—\\\"सुनहरे मुख वाली नदी\\\"—के किनारे जंगल में, पेड़ों के नीचे एक शिवलिंग था। न कोई पुजारी, न कोई घंटी, और न कोई छत। बस पत्थर, और एक गहरा सन्नाटा। और जंगल के तीन जीव, तीनों बिल्कुल अकेले, उससे सच्चा प्रेम करने लगे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Long before there was a town, in the forest by the river Swarnamukhi — \"the golden-faced river\" of Andhra — there stood a shivalinga under the trees, with no priest, no bell, and no roof. Just stone, and quiet. And three creatures of the forest, each entirely alone, fell in love with it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"सबसे पहले मकड़ी आई। और एक मकड़ी के पास देने को क्या होता है? रेशम। तो उसने वही दिया। शिवलिंग के ठीक ऊपर, उसने धागे पर धागा बुनकर एक शामियाना तैयार किया—चांदी की जाली जैसी एक छत, ताकि गिरते पत्तों और तपती धूप से बचाव हो सके। हर रोज़ कोई न कोई उसे फाड़ देता। और हर रोज़ वह उसे फिर से पूरा बुन देती।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The spider came first. And what does a spider have to give? Silk. So she gave it. High over the linga she wove a canopy, thread by thread by thread — a ceiling of silver lace to keep off the falling leaves and the beating sun. Every day something tore it. Every day she wove it whole again.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"भोर होते ही नदी की ओर से हाथी आया, अपनी सूंड में साफ़ पानी भरकर। उसने शिवलिंग को बड़े लाड़ से ऊपर से नीचे तक नहलाया—और अनजाने में ही, चांदी की जाली वाली उस छत को बहा दिया—फिर उस साफ़, भीगे पत्थर पर जंगली पत्ते और फूल सजाए, कुछ देर वहीं खड़ा रहा, और फिर चला गया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The elephant came at dawn, up from the river, with his trunk full of clean water. He washed the linga tenderly from top to bottom — sweeping away, though he never once noticed, a certain silver lace canopy — and then laid wild leaves and flowers on the clean wet stone, and stood a while, and went.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"और रात के अंधेरे में सांप सरकते हुए आया, अपने पास का इकलौता खज़ाना लिए: एक चमकती हुई मणि। उसने उसे शिवलिंग के चरणों में रख दिया, ताकि वह अंधेरे में एक छोटे से चांद की तरह चमकती रहे। सुबह हाथी के नहलाने से वह मणि बहकर सूखे पत्तों में जा गिरी। उस रात सांप ने देखा कि उसकी भेंट एक तरफ फेंकी पड़ी है—और ऊपर मकड़ी ने देखा कि उसका शामियाना फिर से फटा हुआ है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And in the night came the snake, gliding, carrying the one treasure a serpent owns: a shining jewel, which he laid at the foot of the linga so it would gleam there in the dark like a small moon. In the morning the elephant's washing swept the jewel into the leaves. That night the snake found his gift flung aside — and the spider, above, found her canopy torn again.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"दिन-ब-दिन यही चलता रहा। तीनों में से हर कोई जब भी आता, अपनी भेंट को उजड़ा हुआ पाता, और तीनों के दिल में वही एक दुख भरी बात कौंधती: कोई मेरी पूजा बिगाड़ रहा है। जानबूझकर। मकड़ी ने गुस्से में और तेज़ी से जाला बुना। सांप अपनी मणि के चारों ओर और कसकर कुंडली मारकर बैठ गया। हाथी ने शिवलिंग को और भी ज़ोर-शोर से नहलाया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Day after day it went on. Each of the three arrived to find their offering ruined, and each thought the same hurt thought: somebody is spoiling my worship. On purpose. The spider spun grimly. The snake coiled tighter round his jewel. The elephant washed harder.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"और फिर एक धुंधली सुबह, तीनों एक साथ आ पहुंचे—हाथी अपने पानी के साथ, सांप अपनी मणि के पास कुंडली मारे, और मकड़ी अपने धागे पर लटकी हुई—और आख़िरकार इतने दिनों की ग़लतफ़हमी फुफकारती और चिंघाड़ती हुई अपने पैरों पर आ खड़ी हुई। पुरानी कथाओं में इसके बाद जो हुआ, वह सचमुच बहुत बुरा था। पर यह कहानी उसे सही वक़्त पर रोक लेती है—और आपको यह बात साफ़-साफ़ बता भी देती है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And so one grey dawn, all three arrived at once — the elephant with his water, the snake still coiled by his jewel, the spider on her thread — and the long misunderstanding stood up on its feet at last, hissing and trumpeting. The oldest tellings let what happened next go very badly indeed. This telling stops it in time — and tells you so.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"क्योंकि अब खुद उस पत्थर से भी और नहीं रहा गया। एक शांत, दिव्य रोशनी के साथ शिवजी शिवलिंग में से प्रकट हुए और उनके बीच आ खड़े हुए — वही भगवान, जिन्हें वे तीनों अकेले-अकेले, इतने समय से पूजते आ रहे थे। और उन्होंने तीनों को समझाया: वह रेशम उनके लिए छांव था। वह जल उनके स्नान के लिए था। वह मणि उनके लिए रोशनी थी। तीन भेंट। एक ही प्रेम। झगड़ा वहीं का वहीं ढेर हो गया, और उन तीनों ने पहली बार एक-दूसरे को ठीक से देखा।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Because the stone itself had had enough. Shiva rose out of the linga in a blaze of gentle light and stood between them — the god all three had been loving, all along, alone. And he showed them, each to each: the silk was shade for him. The water was washing for him. The jewel was light for him. Three gifts. One love. The quarrel fell down dead where it stood, and the three of them looked at one another properly for the first time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.srikalahasti-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.srikalahasti:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Spider, the Snake and the Elephant","text":"\"और ताकि कोई भी, कभी भी उन तीनों को न भूले, उस जगह का नाम ही उनके नाम पर पड़ गया। श्री, यानी मकड़ी। काल, यानी सांप। हस्ती, यानी हाथी। इन्हें एक साथ बोलकर देखो — श्री-काल-हस्ती।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And so that no one, ever, would forget the three of them, the place itself took their names. Sri, the spider. Kala, the snake. Hasti, the elephant. Say them together — Sri-kala-hasti.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-srikalahasti.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.srikalahasti|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"\"रामायण का वह दुखद मोड़ तो आप जानते ही हैं: सीता जी को आकाश के रास्ते दक्षिण की ओर हर कर ले जाया जा रहा था। पर शायद आप यह न जानते हों कि एक जीव ने इसे रोकने की कोशिश की थी। जटायु — पक्षियों के महान राजा, विशाल और वृद्ध, जो कभी बहुत बलवान थे और राम जी के पिता के मित्र थे। उन्होंने ऊपर आकाश से सीता जी की पुकार सुनी, अपने बूढ़े पंखों पर उड़ान भरी, और वे लड़े। वे बूढ़े थे, और हार गए।\"","lang":"hi","body":"You know the terrible turn of the Ramayana: Sita, carried away south through the sky. What you may not know is that one creature tried to stop it. Jatayu — the great eagle-king, huge and old, who had been mighty once and had been Rama's father's friend. He heard Sita cry out overhead, and he rose on his old wings, and he fought. He was old, and he lost.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"\"अब महाकाव्य से निकलकर ज़रा आंध्र की सूखी, पथरीली पहाड़ियों में आइए — शहद के रंग जैसी विशाल, बिखरी हुई चट्टानों की उस धरती पर। क्योंकि वहाँ के गाँव आपको बताएंगे कि यहीं, इन्हीं चट्टानों के बीच, वह महान पक्षी आकर गिरा था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Now come down out of the epic for a moment, into the dry, boulder-strewn hills of Andhra — the country of huge tumbled rocks the colour of honey. Because the villages there will tell you it was here, among these very boulders, that the great bird came down.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"\"राम और लक्ष्मण खोजते हुए दक्षिण की ओर आए, उस वीराने में सीता जी का नाम पुकारते हुए। और एक गर्म चट्टान पर उन्हें वे मिले — वह विशाल, वृद्ध पक्षी, जिसके पर दूर-दूर तक बिखरे पड़े थे, एक पंख कट चुका था, और जिनकी कहानी बस ख़त्म होने को थी। लेकिन उनकी आँखों में अब भी चमक थी, और वे अपनी साँसें थामे हुए थे। उन्हें कोई संदेश जो पहुँचाना था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Rama and Lakshmana came searching south, calling Sita's name into the empty country. And spread on a warm rock they found him — the enormous old bird, feathers scattered wide, one wing gone, his story nearly over. But his eyes were still bright, and he had been holding on. He had something to deliver.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"जितनी बची-खुची ताक़त थी, उससे जटायु ने उन्हें सब कुछ बता दिया: सीता को कौन ले गया था, और उसका रथ किस तरफ़ गया था—दक्षिण की ओर, समुद्र की तरफ़। उस भयानक खोजबीन में यह पहली बार था जब किसी ने उन्हें सीता का कोई पक्का सुराग दिया था—उम्मीद की पहली किरण। वह बूढ़ा पक्षी अपनी लड़ाई हार चुका था, फिर भी उसने अपना संदेश पहुँचा ही दिया।","lang":"hi","body":"With the strength he had saved, Jatayu told them everything: who it was that had taken her, and the way the chariot had gone, south, toward the sea. It was the first real news of Sita anyone had given them — the first thread of hope in all that terrible searching. The old bird had lost his fight, and delivered his message anyway.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"राम चट्टान पर घुटनों के बल बैठ गए और उस बड़े, ज़ख़्मी सिर को अपनी गोद में रख लिया, और उनके लिए वैसे ही शोक मनाया जैसे एक बेटा मनाता है—क्योंकि जटायु राम के परिवार के लिए अपनों की तरह लड़े थे। और यहीं पर तेलुगु की धरती इस महाकाव्य में अपने दो शब्द जोड़ती है। राम ने नीचे गिरे पक्षी को देखा और कहा: \"ले, पक्षी।\" उठो, पक्षी।","lang":"hi","body":"Rama knelt on the rock and took the great scarred head into his lap, and grieved for him as a son grieves — for Jatayu had fought for Rama's family as family. And here the Telugu country adds its own two words to the epic. Rama looked at the fallen bird and said: \"Le, pakshi.\" Rise, bird.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"और महाकाव्य बताता है कि राम ने उस बूढ़े पक्षी को वही दिया जो एक बेटा अपने पिता को देता है—सम्मान, धन्यवाद और एक शांत विदाई। और जटायु ने अपनी लंबी ज़िंदगी का सफ़र वहीं शांति से, स्वयं ईश्वर की गोद में पूरा किया, अपनी सबसे बड़ी वीरता दिखाकर। फिर दोनों भाई उठे और दक्षिण की ओर बढ़ चले, उनके संदेश को युद्ध के अंत तक अपने साथ लिए हुए।","lang":"hi","body":"And the epic tells that Rama gave the old bird what a son gives a father — honour, and thanks, and a gentle letting-go — and that Jatayu finished his long story there in peace, in the god's own lap, having done the bravest thing in it. Then the brothers rose and walked on south, carrying his message all the way to the end of the war.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"लेकिन वे दो तेलुगु शब्द वहीं रह गए, उसी जगह पर जहाँ वे बोले गए थे। ले, पक्षी। इन्हें जल्दी से बोलिए—लेपाक्षी। आज तक उस गाँव का यही नाम है।","lang":"hi","body":"But the two Telugu words stayed behind, on the spot where they were spoken. Le, pakshi. Say them quickly — Lepakshi. That is the village's name to this day.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.lepakshi-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.lepakshi:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · Rise, Bird","text":"सदियों बाद, बनाने वालों ने लेपाक्षी में एक मंदिर खड़ा किया, मानो वह जगह शुरू से ही पवित्र रही हो—एक ही चट्टान से तराशा हुआ इतना विशाल नंदी बैल, जिसे आप सड़क से ही देख सकते हैं, और अंदर एक मशहूर खंभा जिसे लोग बड़े चाव से आपको दिखाएँगे कि वह ज़मीन को छूता ही नहीं है। लेकिन वहाँ किसी से भी इस नाम का मतलब पूछिए, तो हर बार आपको उसी बूढ़े पक्षी की बात सुनने को मिलेगी।","lang":"hi","body":"Centuries later, builders raised a temple at Lepakshi, as if the place had been holy all along — with a Nandi bull carved from a single rock so enormous you can see it from the road, and a famous pillar inside that people will delightedly show you does not quite rest on the ground. But ask anyone there what the name means, and you get the old bird, every time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepakshi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepakshi|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"\"दक्षिण के लंबे पूर्वी तट को कोरोमंडल कहा जाता है, और पुदुच्चेरी का समंदर किनारे बसा हिस्सा—कराईकल—इस तट का अपना एक कोना सँभाले हुए है: मछुआरों की नावें, झाऊ के पेड़, और समंदर की लहरों की ऐसी गूँज जो बिस्तर में लेटे-लेटे भी सुनाई दे जाए। इस तट की मछुआरी दादियाँ उन लहरों के उस पार की एक कहानी सुनाती हैं, और वह कहानी शुरू होती है इस तट पर बसे अब तक के सबसे भव्य शहर से।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The long east coast of the south is called the Coromandel, and Karaikal — the sea-side piece of Puducherry — keeps its own stretch of it: fishing boats, casuarina trees, surf you can hear from your bed. The fisher grandmothers of this coast tell a story about what lies out past the breakers, and it begins with the grandest city the coast ever had.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"\"लोग उसे पुहार कहते थे—कावेरीपूमपट्टिनम, यानी कावेरी का फूल—जो वहाँ बसा था जहाँ यह महान नदी समंदर से मिलती थी। पुरानी तमिल कविताएँ उसकी जगमगाहट को याद करती हैं: ऐसे-ऐसे देशों से आए जहाज़ जिनके नाम भी कोई ठीक से न बोल पाए, रेशम, काली मिर्च और मोतियों से भरे गोदाम, सुनारों की गलियाँ, रात भर जलने वाला प्रकाश-स्तंभ, और खिड़कियों से छनकर आती संगीत की तानें।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Puhar, they called it — Kaveripoompattinam, the flower of the Kaveri — built where the great river met the sea. The old Tamil poems remember it glittering: ships from countries nobody could pronounce, warehouses of silk and pepper and pearls, streets of goldsmiths, a lighthouse burning all night, and music coming out of the windows.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"\"और कहानी कहती है कि उस शहर और समंदर के बीच एक समझौता था। हर साल पुहार में शुक्रिया अदा करने का एक बड़ा त्यौहार मनाया जाता था—झंडे लहराए जाते, लहरों पर दीये तैराए जाते, आकाश के देवता इंद्र के लिए गीत गाए जाते, और पूरा शहर किनारे पर उतरकर ऊँचे सुर में याद करता कि वह इस पानी का कितना कर्ज़दार है। और जब तक यह त्यौहार मनाया जाता रहा, समंदर भी एक अच्छे पड़ोसी की तरह अपनी हद में रहा।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And the city had, the story says, an understanding with the sea. Every year Puhar kept a great festival of thanks — flags up, lamps floated on the waves, songs to the sky god Indra, the whole city down on the shore remembering out loud what it owed the water. And as long as the festival was kept, the sea kept its distance, like a good neighbour.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"\"फिर एक ऐसा साल आया जब राजा अपने ही दुखों में डूबे हुए थे, किसी की उन्हें याद दिलाने की हिम्मत न हुई, और त्यौहार का दिन चुपचाप बीत गया। न कोई झंडे। न कोई दीये। न कोई गीत। किसी की भी याद में यह पहला ऐसा साल था जब पुहार शुक्रिया कहना भूल गया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Then came a year when the king's heart was buried in his own sorrows, and nobody dared remind him, and the calendar turned quietly past the festival day. No flags. No lamps. No songs. The first year anyone could remember that Puhar forgot to say thank you.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"\"पानी बड़ी नर्मी से ऊपर चढ़ा, इस कहानी में यही तो एक अजीब सी दया है—हर दिन थोड़ा-सा और ऊँचा, चौखटों को छूता हुआ, आँगनों में आ बैठता हुआ, शहर को संभलने की मोहलत देता हुआ। और लोग समझ गए, उन्होंने अपने बच्चों, अपने बर्तनों और पिंजरों में अपनी चिड़ियों को समेटा, और ज़मीन के अंदरूनी हिस्से की ओर चल पड़े, जबकि पानी बड़े धीरज से उनके पीछे-पीछे चल रहा था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"It rose gently, that is the strange mercy in the telling — a little higher each day, lapping at doorsteps, sitting down in courtyards, giving the city time. And the people understood, and gathered their children and their pots and their birds in cages, and walked inland with the water walking patiently behind them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"\"और फिर समंदर उस खाली शहर में आकर पसर गया, सुनारों की गलियों, गोदामों और प्रकाश-स्तंभ की सीढ़ियों के ऊपर, और वहीं ठहर गया। इस तट का सबसे बड़ा बंदरगाह पानी के नीचे एक गहरी ख़ामोशी बनकर रह गया। पुराने तमिल महाकाव्य—वही जो कण्णगि और मणिमेकलै की गाथा सुनाते हैं—आज भी उस दिन को याद करते हैं जब यह हुआ था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And then the sea lay down in the empty city, over the goldsmith streets and the warehouses and the lighthouse stair, and stayed. The greatest port of the coast became a quietness under the water. The old Tamil epics — the very ones that tell of Kannagi and of Manimekalai — remember the day it happened.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"लेकिन मछुआरों के परिवार आज भी यह कहानी क्यों सुनाते हैं, उसकी वजह यह है। वे कहते हैं कि वह शहर कभी टूटा ही नहीं था — बस समूचा का समूचा, वापस ले लिया गया था। वह सब आज भी वहीं नीचे है, गलियों समेत, धुंधला, हरा और शांत। और बेहद शांत रातों में, झागदार लहरों के पार, कभी-कभी एक पुरानी घंटी को अपना काम याद आ जाता है।","lang":"hi","body":"But here is why the fisher families tell it still. The city, they say, was never wrecked — only taken back, whole. It is all still down there, streets and all, dim and green and quiet. And on very still nights, out past the surf, an old bell sometimes remembers its job.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.poompuhar-sea-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.poompuhar-sea:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The City Under the Sea","text":"और यह बात शायद तुम्हें चौंककर सीधा बिठा दे: गोताखोरी के सामान और अनोखे औज़ारों के साथ बड़े लोग सचमुच वहाँ खोज करने गए हैं, उस छोटे से कस्बे के पास जिसे आज पूमपुहार कहा जाता है — और उन्हें पानी के बहुत नीचे दूर पुराने तराशे हुए पत्थर मिले हैं, ठीक वहीं जहाँ कहानी हमेशा से शहर का होना बताती थी। समुद्र थोड़ा-सा दिखाता है, और बाकी सब अपने पास रख लेता है।","lang":"hi","body":"And this may make you sit up: grown-ups with diving gear and clever instruments have actually gone looking, off the little town that carries the name Poompuhar today — and have found old worked stones far out under the water, where the story always said the city was. The sea shows a little, and keeps the rest.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-poompuhar-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.poompuhar-sea|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.amudha-surabhi-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.amudha-surabhi:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Bowl That Was Never Empty","text":"इससे पहले कि समुद्र पुहार को वापस ले लेता — वह कहानी तो तुमने सुनी ही है — उस शहर के पास तट को देने के लिए एक और कहानी थी, और वह यह है। यह मणिमेकलै नाम की एक लड़की के बारे में है, जो एक मशहूर नृत्यांगना की बेटी थी, और जिसे बड़े होकर खुद भी एक मशहूर नृत्यांगना बनना था। पूरे शहर ने उसके लिए उसकी ज़िंदगी तय कर रखी थी। पर मणिमेकलै की सोच कुछ और ही थी।","lang":"hi","body":"Before the sea took Puhar back — you have heard that story — the city had one more tale to give the coast, and it is this one. It is about a girl called Manimekalai, a famous dancer's daughter, who was supposed to grow up to be a famous dancer herself. The whole city had planned her life for her. Manimekalai had other ideas.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-amudha-surabhi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.amudha-surabhi|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.amudha-surabhi-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.amudha-surabhi:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Bowl That Was Never Empty","text":"शहर के बाग-बगीचों में उसने बौद्ध आचार्यों को सुना था — क्योंकि उन दिनों इस तमिल तट पर बुद्ध का मार्ग खूब फल-फूल रहा था, उनके भिक्षु और उनके उपवन मंदिरों और बाज़ारों के बीच ही मौजूद थे। वहाँ उसने जो कुछ सुना, वह उसके दिल में उतर गया और वहीं बस गया: कला दिखाने में बिताई ज़िंदगी, दूसरों को देने में बिताई ज़िंदगी के आगे बहुत छोटी लगने लगी।","lang":"hi","body":"In the city's garden groves she had listened to the Buddhist teachers — for in those days the Buddha's way flourished all along this Tamil coast, its monks and its gardens right there among the temples and the markets. What she heard there sat down in her heart and stayed: a life spent performing seemed a small thing next to a life spent giving.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-amudha-surabhi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.amudha-surabhi|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.amudha-surabhi-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.amudha-surabhi:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Bowl That Was Never Empty","text":"जिस महाकाव्य का नाम उसके नाम पर है, वह बताता है कि एक द्वीप पर, एक झील के किनारे, त्योहार के दिन, उसके हाथों में एक चमत्कार आया: एक भिक्षापात्र जिसका नाम अमुद सुरभि था — \"वह अमृत जो कभी ख़त्म नहीं होता।\" यह बिल्कुल वैसा ही कटोरा दिखता था जैसा कोई घुमक्कड़ भिक्षुणी लेकर चलती है। पर वह वैसा बिल्कुल नहीं था।","lang":"hi","body":"The epic that carries her name tells that on an island, beside a lake, on a festival day, a wonder came into her hands: a begging bowl called Amudha Surabhi — \"the nectar that never fails.\" It looked like any bowl a wandering nun might carry. It was not.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-amudha-surabhi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.amudha-surabhi|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.amudha-surabhi-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.amudha-surabhi:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Bowl That Was Never Empty","text":"\"सच्चे दिल से दिए गए एक मुट्ठी चावल से एक बार भर जाने के बाद, वह कटोरा देता ही जाता, देता ही जाता और कभी खाली नहीं होता था। लेकिन इस्तेमाल करते-करते उसका एक नियम समझ आया: वह तभी भर-भरकर देता था जब सबसे भूखे इंसान को पहले परोसा जाए। अगर कतार के गलत छोर से शुरुआत कर दी, तो वह बस एक मामूली कटोरा बनकर रह जाता था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Filled once, with a handful of rice given by a good heart, the bowl would pour and pour and not run dry. But it had one rule, learned by using it: it poured only when the hungriest were served first. Start at the wrong end of the queue, and it was just a bowl.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-amudha-surabhi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.amudha-surabhi|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.amudha-surabhi-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.amudha-surabhi:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Bowl That Was Never Empty","text":"\"तो वह उसे लेकर दिन-ब-दिन शहर में घूमती रही — और जहाँ भूख सबसे ज़्यादा होती, कटोरा भी वहीं सबसे गहरा हो जाता। जब तक सामने फैला हुआ आखिरी हाथ न भर जाता, कटोरा कभी खाली नहीं हुआ। अमीर लोगों ने उसे खरीदने की पेशकश की। पर वह उस तरह का कटोरा नहीं था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"So she walked the city with it, day after day — and wherever the hunger was worst, the bowl was deepest. It never once ran dry before the last outstretched hand. Rich men offered to buy it. It was not that kind of bowl.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-amudha-surabhi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.amudha-surabhi|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.amudha-surabhi-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.amudha-surabhi:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Bowl That Was Never Empty","text":"\"फिर उसने कुछ ऐसा माँगा जिससे पूरा शहर हैरान रह गया: उसे जेल के अंदर जाने दिया जाए, ताकि वह कैदियों को खाना खिला सके — वे भूले-बिसरे लोग, जो सबसे भूखे थे, जिन्हें कभी कोई पहले खाना नहीं खिलाता था। और राजा, उस लड़की को उन लोगों को चावल परोसते देख जिन्हें बाकी सब भूल चुके थे, इतने भावुक हुए कि महाकाव्य कहता है, उन्होंने जेल का रूप ही बदल दिया: सज़ा की जगह से बदलकर उसे देखभाल का घर बना दिया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Then she asked for something that startled the whole city: to be let into the prison, to feed the prisoners — the forgotten ones, the hungriest of all, whom nobody fed first. And the king, watching this girl serve rice to the people everyone else had crossed off, was so moved that the epic says he changed what the prison was: from a place of punishment into a house of care.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-amudha-surabhi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.amudha-surabhi|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.amudha-surabhi-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.amudha-surabhi:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Bowl That Was Never Empty","text":"\"मणिमेकलै की कहानी तमिल भाषा के पाँच महान महाकाव्यों में से एक बन गई — एक बौद्ध महाकाव्य, जो इसी तट पर रचा गया था जब यहाँ बुद्ध का मार्ग फल-फूल रहा था, और जो एक ऐसी लड़की को याद करता है जिसने पूरे शहर से बढ़कर दान दिया था। जिस विश्वास ने इसे रचा, उसे यहाँ उसी तरह बताया गया है जैसे वह खुद को बताता है: भीतर से, बड़े प्यार के साथ।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Manimekalai's story became one of the five great epics of the Tamil language — a Buddhist epic, made on this coast when the Buddha's way flowered here, remembering a girl who out-gave a city. The faith that made it is told here the way it tells itself: from the inside, with love.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-amudha-surabhi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.amudha-surabhi|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"\"राजा नल सचमुच बहुत अच्छे इंसानों में से एक थे — बहादुर, न्यायप्रिय, समझदार और दृढ़-निश्चयी दमयंती के पति, और तो और, अपने दौर के सभी राजाओं में सबसे बेहतरीन रसोइये। उनकी कहानी इतनी पुरानी है कि वह खुद महाभारत के भीतर सुनाई गई है। और यह एक भले इंसान के बुरे दिनों की कहानी है — जो इसे दुनिया की सबसे काम की कहानियों में से एक बनाती है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"King Nala was one of the genuinely good ones — brave, fair, married to the wise and steadfast Damayanti, and, of all things, the finest cook among the kings of his age. His story is old enough to be told inside the Mahabharata itself. And it is the story of a good man's bad years — which makes it one of the most useful stories there is.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"क्योंकि परंपरा यही कहती है: आकाश की नौ महान ज्योतियों के बीच शनि चलते हैं — धीमे, सांवले और धीरज वाले। डरे हुए लोग चाहे कुछ भी कहें, वे क्रूर नहीं हैं। वे एक सख्त शिक्षक हैं, और उनका विषय है सब्र, और राजाओं सहित हर किसी के जीवन की बारी उनकी कक्षा में आती ही है। फिर नल की बारी आई।","lang":"hi","body":"For the tradition says this: among the nine great lights of the sky walks Shani — the slow, dark, patient one. He is not cruel, whatever frightened people say. He is the strict teacher, and his subject is patience, and every life, kings included, gets a turn in his classroom. Nala's turn came.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"और सब कुछ हाथ से फिसल गया। वे चौपड़ का ऐसा खेल खेलने बैठ गए, जिसमें उन्हें कभी नहीं बैठना चाहिए था, और वह सब हार गए जो अकेले उनका खोने के लिए नहीं था — अपना राजपाट, अपना सुख-चैन और अपने परिवार के सिर की छत। वह भला राजा रास्तों पर भटकने वाला एक मुसाफ़िर बन गया, तन पर बस एक फटा-पुराना कपड़ा और अपना ही नाम ऐसा लगने लगा जैसे किसी और का हो।","lang":"hi","body":"And everything slid. He sat down to a game of dice he should never have sat down to, and lost what was never his alone to lose — his kingdom, his comforts, the roof over his family. The good king became a wanderer on the roads, in one worn cloth, with his own name feeling like someone else's.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"आखिरकार उन्होंने एक दूसरे राजा की रसोई में काम संभाल लिया — क्योंकि राजा भले ही सब कुछ खो दे, पर एक अच्छा रसोइया कभी भूखा नहीं रहता। और बहुत दूर दमयंती ने, जो अपने सामने आए हर राजदरबार से कहीं अधिक समझदार थीं, उन्हें भूल जाने की हर सलाह ठुकरा दी। पुरानी कथाएँ कहती हैं कि उन्होंने स्वाद के सहारे अपने पति को ढूँढ निकाला: दूर की किसी रसोई से एक पकवान आया, और वे पहचान गईं कि यह किन हाथों का बनाया हुआ है। नल जैसा खाना कोई और बना ही नहीं सकता था।","lang":"hi","body":"He took work, in the end, in another king's kitchen — for a king can lose everything and a good cook still eats. And far away Damayanti, who was cleverer than any court she ever stood in, refused every suggestion that she forget him. The tellings say she tracked her husband down by taste: one dish arrived from a distant kitchen, and she knew the hand that had made it. No one else cooked like Nala.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"लेकिन वे कहाँ हैं, यह जान लेने भर से कठिन दिन खत्म नहीं हो सकते थे — कठिन दिन सिर्फ हमारे चाहने से खत्म नहीं होते। तो यही वह सवाल है, जिस पर यह पूरी कहानी घूमती है।","lang":"hi","body":"But knowing where he was could not end the hard years — hard years do not end because you want them to. So here is the question the whole story turns on.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"और भटकते-भटकते, जैसा कि दक्षिण के मंदिरों की परंपरा बताती है, वे आखिरकार कोरोमंडल तट के पास हरे-भरे धान के खेतों वाले एक छोटे से स्थान पर पहुँचे — तिरुनल्लार — जहाँ शिव जी का एक प्राचीन मंदिर है, और उसी के भीतर स्वयं शनि का एक पावन देवालय भी। नल अंदर गए, अपने कठिन बरसों का सारा बोझ उसके आगे उतार कर रख दिया, और मंदिर के सरोवर में स्नान किया।","lang":"hi","body":"And in his wanderings, the temple tradition of the south tells, he came at last to a small place in the green paddy country near the Coromandel coast — Thirunallar — where there is an old temple of Shiva, and within it a shrine of Shani himself. Nala went in, and laid the whole weight of his years down in front of it, and bathed in the temple tank.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"और वहीं, परंपरा कहती है, शनि ने उन्हें मुक्त कर दिया। हारे हुए नहीं — बल्कि पाठ पूरा करके। \"इन्होंने वह सब कुछ सीख लिया है जो कठिन बरस इन्हें सिखा सकते थे। पाठ पूरा हुआ।\" नल के ऊपर से बोझ ऐसे उतर गया जैसे कंधों से कोई भीगा लबादा उतार दिया गया हो, और कहानी का रुख पलट गया: उनका राज्य उनके पास लौट आया, और दमयंती भी, और वह उजियारा भी।","lang":"hi","body":"And there, the tradition says, Shani let him go. Not beaten — finished. \"He has learned everything the hard years had to teach him. The lesson is over.\" The weight lifted off Nala like a wet cloak taken from his shoulders, and the story turns: his kingdom found its way back to him, and Damayanti, and the light.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.thirunallar-nala-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.thirunallar-nala:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The King Who Waited Out the Storm","text":"\"तिरुनल्लार के कुंड को आज भी नल तीर्थम कहा जाता है — यानी नल का जल — और अपने कठिन वर्षों का बोझ उठाए तीर्थयात्री आज भी यहाँ आते हैं, स्नान करते हैं, और राहत की सांस लेते हैं। और तिरुनल्लार कराईकल ज़िले में पड़ता है, जो पुदुच्चेरी का एक टुकड़ा है — जैसे किसी एक बटुए का सिक्का तमिल देश की जेब में गहराई में रखा हो।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The tank at Thirunallar is called Nala theertham to this day — Nala's waters — and pilgrims carrying their own hard years still come, and bathe, and breathe out. And Thirunallar sits in Karaikal district, which is a piece of Puducherry — a coin from one purse sitting deep in Tamil country's pocket.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-thirunallar-nala.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.thirunallar-nala|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"\"ऊँचे हिमालय में जब शिवजी ने पार्वतीजी से विवाह किया, तो सचमुच हर कोई वहाँ गया। देवता, ऋषि-मुनि, गण, और अपने चलने-फिरते रूपों में नदियाँ — युग के इस सबसे बड़े विवाह के लिए पूरी की पूरी सृष्टि उत्तर के पहाड़ों में सिमट आई। जिससे एक ऐसी समस्या खड़ी हो गई जिसके बारे में किसी ने सोचा ही नहीं था। वज़न।\"","lang":"hi","body":"When Shiva married Parvati in the high Himalaya, absolutely everyone went. Gods, sages, spirits, rivers in their travelling shapes — all of creation packed itself into the mountains of the north for the wedding of the age. Which created a problem nobody had thought of. Weight.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"\"कथा कहती है कि दुनिया एक तरफ झुक गई। हर ख़ास चीज़ के उत्तरी छोर पर जमा हो जाने से, धरती ज़रूरत से ज़्यादा भरी नाव की तरह डोलने लगी — उत्तर भारी होकर नीचे धंसने लगा, और दक्षिण हल्का होकर ऊपर उठने लगा। किसी बहुत भारी चीज़ को, या किसी बहुत भारी व्यक्ति को, दूसरी तरफ जाकर बैठना था। विवाह के ही दौरान। फ़ौरन।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The world, the story says, tilted. With everything of importance crowded onto its northern end, the earth began to lean like an overloaded boat — the north groaning downward, the south rising light and empty. Something, or someone, of very great weight had to go and sit on the other side. During the wedding. Immediately.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"\"शिवजी ने सभा पर नज़र दौड़ाई कि किसे भेजा जाए। और उन्होंने न तो सबसे बलवान देवता को चुना, न सबसे लंबे को। उन्होंने अगस्त्य को बुलाया — जो सभी ऋषियों में सबसे छोटे कद के थे, इतने छोटे कि बच्चों की भीड़ में भी खो जाएँ। क्योंकि शिवजी जानते थे कि दुनिया का तराज़ू ताक़त नहीं तौलता। वह तो ज्ञान तौलता है। और इस छोटे से कद वाले ऋषि का ज्ञान, उस पूरी बारात के कुल वज़न जितना भारी था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Shiva looked out over the gathering for the one to send. And he did not choose the strongest god or the tallest. He called Agastya — the smallest of all the sages, a man you could lose in a crowd of children. Because the scales of the world, Shiva knew, do not weigh muscle. They weigh wisdom. And this small man's learning weighed as much as the whole wedding party put together.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"\"अगस्त्य का चेहरा उतर गया, और कथा भी इसे छिपाती नहीं — उनसे युग के उस सबसे बड़े विवाह को छोड़ने के लिए कहा जा रहा था, जिसकी चर्चा दुनिया हमेशा-हमेशा करने वाली थी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Agastya's face fell, and the story lets it fall — he was being asked to miss the wedding of the age, the one everyone would talk about for the rest of time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"और शिव ने, उन्हें जाते हुए देखकर, एक ऐसा वचन दिया जो पहली पंक्ति में बैठने से भी कहीं बढ़कर था: \"आप जहाँ भी खड़े होंगे, जब भी अपने मन को इस ओर मोड़ेंगे — आपको यह विवाह बिल्कुल वैसे ही साफ दिखाई देगा, जैसे आप मेरे पास ही खड़े हों।\" इसलिए वे नन्हे ऋषि दक्षिण की ओर चल पड़े, और पूरे रास्ते उनके मन की आँखों के सामने यह उत्सव चलता रहा, और आखिरकार उनका कुछ भी नहीं छूटा।","lang":"hi","body":"And Shiva, watching him go, gave him a promise worth more than a front-row seat: \"Wherever you stand, whenever you turn your heart this way — you will see the wedding, clear as if you stood beside me.\" So the small sage walked south with the celebration playing before his inner eye the whole way, missing nothing after all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"विंध्य पर्वत पर, जो हाल ही में और ऊँचा और अभिमानी होता जा रहा था और आसमान को छूने लगा था, उस पर्वतमाला ने महान ऋषि को रास्ता देने के लिए झुककर सिर नवाया। अगस्त्य ने मुस्कुराते हुए कहा, \"जब तक मैं लौटकर न आऊँ, तुम ऐसे ही रहना।\" वे कभी उत्तर वापस नहीं लौटे। विंध्य आज भी झुका हुआ है — और बुज़ुर्ग आँख मटकाते हुए कहते हैं कि यही वजह है कि आज कोई भी दक्षिण की ओर जा पाता है।","lang":"hi","body":"At the Vindhya mountains, which had lately been growing taller and prouder and were shouldering into the very sky, the range bowed low to let the great sage pass. \"Stay just so until I return,\" said Agastya pleasantly. He never returned north. The Vindhyas are bowing still — which, the old ones say with a wink, is why anyone can cross into the south at all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"और जैसे ही उन्होंने दक्षिणी धरती पर कदम रखा, दुनिया बिल्कुल सीधी-सपाट हो गई — और तब से वैसी ही बनी हुई है। अगस्त्य हमेशा के लिए दक्षिण में बस गए और सबके दादाजी जैसे बन गए: परंपरा उन्हें तमिल व्याकरण का पहला शिक्षक मानती है, और आप इन कहानियों में उनसे पहले ही मिल चुके हैं, जब वे अपने छोटे से कमंडल में एक खास नदी को लिए घूम रहे थे, जब तक कि बीच में एक कौआ नहीं आ गया।","lang":"hi","body":"And the moment he crossed into the southern lands, the world came level — and level it has stayed. Agastya settled in the south for good and became its grandfather: tradition makes him the first teacher of Tamil grammar, and you have already met him in these stories, carrying a certain river in his little pot until a crow got involved.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.agastya-south-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.agastya-south:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Small Sage Who Balanced the World","text":"कोरोमंडल के तट पर, पुरानी परंपरा कहती है कि ऋषि का आश्रम वहाँ था जहाँ आज पांडिचेरी बसा है — उस शहर को कभी वेदपुरी कहा जाता था, और पुडुचेरी का वेदपुरीश्वरर मंदिर आज भी उस पुराने नाम को जीवित रखे हुए है। बाद में उन्हीं गलियों को अपने तमिल नामों के साथ-साथ फ्रांसीसी नाम भी मिल गए। यह हमेशा से एक ऐसा शहर रहा है जो एक साथ एक से ज़्यादा नाम सँभाल सकता है।","lang":"hi","body":"On the Coromandel shore, old tradition says the sage kept a hermitage where Pondicherry stands today — the town was once called Vedapuri, and the Vedapureeswarar temple in Puducherry keeps that old name alive right now. Later the same streets grew French names alongside their Tamil ones. It has always been a town that can hold more than one name at a time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-agastya-south.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.agastya-south|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"बहुत समय पहले, अकाल के उन सालों में, जब बारिश बार-बार दगा दे जाती थी, उस सूखी-झुलसी धरती पर बस एक ही हरा-भरा खेत बचा था — पश्चिमी पहाड़ों में ब्रह्मगिरि की पहाड़ी पर, ऋषि गौतम के आश्रम में। कहानी कहती है कि उनकी प्रार्थना के नियम और शक्ति से, वह अकेला खेत हर एक दिन चावल देता था। और हर एक दिन, गौतम आने वाले हर व्यक्ति को भोजन कराते थे। हर किसी को। उन लोगों को भी, जो उन्हें पसंद नहीं करते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"In famine years, long ago, when the rains failed and failed again, there was exactly one green field left in the burnt country — on Brahmagiri hill in the western mountains, at the hermitage of the sage Gautama. By the steadiness of his prayer, the story says, that one field gave rice every single day. And every single day, Gautama fed everyone who came. Everyone. Including people who did not like him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"और यहीं से सारी मुसीबत शुरू हुई। जिन लोगों की जान कभी उनके दिए चावल से बची थी, अकाल खत्म होते ही उनमें से कुछ लोग इस बात को पचा नहीं पाए। कुछ दिलों में अहसान का भाव खट्टा होकर जलन बन जाता है। वे सब मिलकर उस भले ऋषि को नीचा दिखाने की तरकीबें सोचने लगे।","lang":"hi","body":"And that last part is where the trouble grew. Some of the very people his rice had kept alive found, as the famine ended, that they could not forgive him for it. Gratitude curdles in some hearts, and turns to jealousy. They put their heads together to make the good sage small again.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"उनकी चाल बड़ी बेरहम और शातिर थी। उन्होंने एक माया रची — परछाई जैसी दुबली-पतली, एक कमज़ोर बूढ़ी गाय — और बुआई के समय उसे गौतम के प्यारे खेत में छोड़ दिया। गौतम, जो हमेशा बहुत दयालु थे, उन्होंने घास का एक मुलायम तिनका उठाया — हाथ से छूने लायक सबसे कोमल चीज़ — और उसे हिलाकर गाय को वहां से हटाने लगे।","lang":"hi","body":"Their trick was cruel and clever. They shaped an illusion — a frail old cow, thin as a shadow — and sent her wandering into Gautama's precious field at seed time. Gautama, ever gentle, picked a soft blade of grass, the mildest thing a hand can hold, and waved her away with it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"और गाय गिरी, फिर उठी ही नहीं — और फिर धुंध बनकर गायब हो गई, क्योंकि वह कभी असली गाय थी ही नहीं। लेकिन गौतम को यह बात पता नहीं थी। उन्हें तो बस वही मालूम था जो उनकी आँखों ने देखा था: उनका हाथ, और नीचे गिरी हुई गाय। एक ऐसे ऋषि के लिए, जो कभी किसी पत्ते को भी चोट न पहुँचाते, इससे भारी दुख का बोझ उन पर और कोई नहीं हो सकता था। और वे ईर्ष्यालु लोग बाड़ के पास खड़े होकर ज़ोर-ज़ोर से कहने लगे कि यह सब उन्हीं की गलती है।","lang":"hi","body":"And the cow fell, and did not get up — and then thinned into mist and was gone, for she had never been a real cow at all. But Gautama did not know that. All he knew was what his eyes had shown him: his hand, and a fallen cow. For a sage who would not bruise a leaf, there was no heavier stone the world could have laid on him. And the jealous ones stood at the fence, loudly agreeing that it was all his fault.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"तो वे वहीं खड़े रह गए, यही मानते हुए कि उनसे अनजाने में ऐसा अनर्थ हो गया है जो वे कभी करना नहीं चाहते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"So there he stood, believing he had done a harm he never meant.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"वे ब्रह्मगिरि की चोटी पर चढ़े और उन्होंने शिव से प्रार्थना की — जो हो चुका था उसे मिटाने के लिए नहीं, बल्कि उसे धोने के लिए: “मुझे यहाँ, दक्षिण में, गंगा का जल दीजिए, ताकि यह दुख और इसके बाद का हर दुख धुलकर साफ़ हो सके।” और शिव — उनके सच्चे दुख से द्रवित होकर, और यह अच्छी तरह जानते हुए कि यह किसकी चाल थी — उन्होंने अपनी जटाओं से एक चमकती हुई जलधारा उस पहाड़ी की चोटी पर बहा दी।","lang":"hi","body":"He climbed to the top of Brahmagiri and prayed to Shiva — not to undo what could not be undone, but to wash it: \"Send me Ganga's water, here, in the south, so that this sorrow and every sorrow after it can be washed clean.\" And Shiva — moved by the honest grief, and knowing perfectly well whose trick it had been — released from his own bound-up hair a shining stream onto the hilltop.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"वह धारा उमड़ी, फैली, और पूरे भूभाग को पार करती हुई पूरब की ओर बह निकली — गोदावरी, दक्षिण की महान नदी, जिसे परंपरा में गौतमी भी कहा जाता है: यानी ऋषि की नदी। और वह छल सामने आ ही गया, जैसे हर छल खुल जाता है; और वे चालबाज़ उथले पानी में शर्मिंदा खड़े रह गए; और नदी ने बिना माँगे उन्हें भी धो दिया। नदियाँ पहले नाम नहीं पूछतीं।","lang":"hi","body":"The stream rose, and gathered, and set off east across the whole width of the land — the Godavari, the great river of the south, whom the tradition also calls Gautami: the sage's river. And the trick came out, as tricks do; and the tricksters stood ashamed in the shallows; and the river washed them too, without being asked. Rivers do not check names first.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.gautami-yanam-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.gautami-yanam:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The River That Washed a Sorrow","text":"नक्शे पर उसकी राह देखो, तो वह पूरे प्रायद्वीप से होकर बहती है, आगे बढ़ते-बढ़ते और चौड़ी होती जाती है, यहाँ तक कि नारियल के पेड़ों, नहरों और धान के हरे-भरे डेल्टा में बँटकर कई मुहानों से समंदर में जा मिलती है। और उन्हीं मुहानों में से एक पर छुपा है एक अचंभा: यानम, एक तेलुगु नदी पर बसा पुदुच्चेरी का एक छोटा-सा टुकड़ा, जहाँ कभी घाटों पर फ्रेंच बोली जाती थी — खुद पॉन्डिचेरी से दो घंटे की दूरी और पूरे एक तट के पार।","lang":"hi","body":"Follow her on a map and she runs right across the peninsula, widening as she goes, until she splits into a great green delta of coconut and canal and rice, and meets the sea through many mouths. And at one of those mouths sits a surprise: Yanam, a little piece of Puducherry on a Telugu river, where French was once spoken on the ghats — a two-hour drive and a whole coast away from Pondicherry itself.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-gautami-yanam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.gautami-yanam|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kadalamma-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kadalamma:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Sea Mother's Storeroom","text":"माहे पुदुच्चेरी का सबसे छोटा और सबसे हरा-भरा हिस्सा है — मालाबार तट पर नदी के मुहाने पर बसा एक कस्बा, जहाँ मलयालम बोली जाती है, जहाँ कभी फ्रांसीसी झंडा लहराता था, और अगर आप इसे इसके पुराने नाम, मय्यषी से पुकारेंगे, तो पूरा कस्बा समझ जाएगा कि आपका मतलब अपने घर से है। यहाँ के लोग हमेशा समंदर में पाँव उतारे जीए हैं। यह उनके तट की सुनाई एक कहानी है।","lang":"hi","body":"Mahe is the smallest and greenest piece of Puducherry — one river-mouth town tucked into the Malabar coast, speaking Malayalam, once flying a French flag, and if you ask for it by its older name, Mayyazhi, the whole town will know you mean home. Its people have always lived with their feet in the sea. This is a story their coast tells.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kadalamma.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kadalamma|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kadalamma-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kadalamma:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Sea Mother's Storeroom","text":"मालाबार तट के मछुआरा परिवार समंदर को बेजान चीज़ नहीं समझते। वे उसे 'कदलम्मा' कहते हैं — कदल यानी समंदर, अम्मा यानी माँ। अम्मा से आप सिर्फ माँगते ही नहीं रहते। नाव समंदर में उतारने से पहले आप उन्हें प्रणाम करते हैं। उनके मिज़ाज का ध्यान रखते हैं। और तंगी के दिनों में भी यह भरोसा रखते हैं कि वे आपको भूली नहीं हैं।","lang":"hi","body":"The fisher families of the Malabar shore do not call the sea \"it.\" They call her Kadalamma — kadal, the sea; amma, mother. You do not simply take from an amma. You greet her before you push the boat out. You mind her moods. And you trust, even in the thin times, that she has not forgotten you.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kadalamma.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kadalamma|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kadalamma-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kadalamma:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Sea Mother's Storeroom","text":"और तंगी के दिन आते हैं, हर एक साल। जब मानसून आता है, तो हफ़्तों तक समंदर इतना उफनता है कि नावें बिल्कुल बाहर नहीं जा सकतीं। ये वो महीने होते हैं जब रात का खाना कम होने लगता है, माँएँ एक ही मछली से तरह-तरह के जतन करने लगती हैं, और मछुआरों के घरों के बच्चे बचपन में ही समझ जाते हैं कि सूनी रसोई की खामोशी कैसी होती है।","lang":"hi","body":"And there are thin times, every single year. When the monsoon arrives, the sea turns wild for weeks on end and the boats cannot go out at all. Those are the months when dinners grow small, and mothers get very clever with one fish, and the children of fisher houses learn early what a quiet kitchen sounds like.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kadalamma.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kadalamma|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kadalamma-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kadalamma:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Sea Mother's Storeroom","text":"और फिर — कुछ सालों में, जैसे ही बारिश थमती है — तट एक चमत्कार के साथ जाग उठता है। समंदर का एक हिस्सा मंदिर के कुंड जैसा चपटा और शांत हो जाता है, उथली हुई मिट्टी से मटमैला, जबकि दोनों तरफ लहरें हमेशा की तरह सफ़ेद फेन बनकर टकराती रहती हैं। और उस थमे हुए मटमैले पानी में ज़िंदगी छलकने लगती है: झींगे, सारडीन और इतनी मछलियाँ जितनी पिछली बार के बाद किसी ने नहीं देखीं — किनारे के इतने पास झुंड बनाए हुए कि रेत पर खड़ा बच्चा भी उन्हें चमकते हुए देख सकता है। इस तट के पास इसके लिए एक शब्द है: चाकरा।","lang":"hi","body":"And then — some years, as the rains ease — the coast wakes up to a miracle. A stretch of sea gone flat as a temple tank, calm and brown with stirred-up mud, while the waves crash on white as ever to either side. And that still brown water is boiling with life: prawns, sardines, more fish than anyone has seen since last time — swarming so close in that a child on the sand can watch them flicker. The coast has a word for it: chakara.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kadalamma.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kadalamma|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kadalamma-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kadalamma:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Sea Mother's Storeroom","text":"और समुद्र तट की एक पुरानी बात यह भी है, वही जो दादियाँ-नानियां सुनाती हैं। हर अम्मा की तरह कदलम्मा का भी एक भंडार-घर है। बारिश के उन भूखे हफ़्तों में, वे किनारे पर बसे अपने बच्चों की शांत रसोइयों की आहट सुनती हैं। और जब आख़िरकार तूफ़ान थम जाता है, तो उन्हें अपने बच्चों की याद आती है — और वे अपने भंडार-घर के दरवाज़े खोल देती हैं, ठीक उनकी चौखट के पास, जहाँ सबसे छोटी नाव और सबसे बूढ़े काका भी पहुँच सकें।","lang":"hi","body":"And here is the coast's old explanation, the one the grandmothers give. Kadalamma keeps a storeroom, the way every amma does. All through the hungry weeks of the rains she hears the quiet kitchens of her children on the shore. And when the storm finally spends itself, she remembers them — and opens the storeroom doors wide, right at their doorstep, where even the smallest boat and the oldest uncle can reach.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kadalamma.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kadalamma|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kadalamma-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kadalamma:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Sea Mother's Storeroom","text":"इसलिए जब चाकारा आता है, तो बड़े-बुज़ुर्ग पुराने नियमों का पालन करते हैं। समंदर में हर परिवार की नाव की बारी आती है। बाज़ार में एक भी टोकरी जाने से पहले, उन घरों को मछलियाँ दी जाती हैं जिनके पास कोई नाव नहीं है। और कोई भी, कभी भी इस तोहफ़े को बर्बाद करके इसका मज़ाक नहीं उड़ाता — क्योंकि अपनी अम्मा के हाथ के खाने का कोई अपमान नहीं करता, और उनके भंडार-घर का तो बिल्कुल भी नहीं।","lang":"hi","body":"So the elders keep the old rules when the chakara comes. Every family's boat gets its turn at the water. The houses with no boat at all are given fish before one basket goes to market. And nobody, ever, mocks the gift by wasting it — because you do not insult your amma's cooking, and you certainly do not insult her storeroom.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kadalamma.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kadalamma|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.kadalamma-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.kadalamma:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Sea Mother's Storeroom","text":"अब, समंदर की पढ़ाई करने वाले बड़ों का अपना ही कहना है: मानसून की बाढ़ किनारे पर कीचड़ की एक नरम परत बहा लाती है, और वह कीचड़ तेल की तरह लहरों को शांत कर देता है, फिर उस शांत और समृद्ध पानी में ढेर सारी मछलियाँ जमा हो जाती हैं। मछुआरों के परिवार बड़े आदर से उनकी बात सुनते हैं और सिर हिलाते हैं। दोनों ही बातें एक साथ सच हो सकती हैं। आख़िर एक अच्छी कहानी का काम ही यही होता है।","lang":"hi","body":"Now, the grown-ups who study the sea have their own telling: the floods of the monsoon wash a soft bank of mud down along the shore, and the mud calms the waves like oil, and the fish gather in that calm, rich water. The fisher families listen politely and nod. Both tellings can be true at once. That is what a good story is for.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kadalamma.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kadalamma|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"पॉन्डिचेरी में शाम के समय पूरा शहर समंदर किनारे टहलता है, काले पत्थरों के ऊपर बनी सड़क पर, और नीचे समंदर किसी ऐसे बड़े नगाड़े की तरह गूँजता रहता है जो कभी थकता ही नहीं। और उस किनारे पर वे आज भी उस समंदर के बारे में एक ऐसी कहानी सुनाते हैं जो पंचतंत्र जितनी ही पुरानी है — क्योंकि वह सचमुच पंचतंत्र से ही है। वह कहानी कुछ ऐसी है।","lang":"hi","body":"In Pondicherry the whole town walks by the sea in the evening, along the promenade above the black rocks, and the sea booms away below like a big drum that never gets tired. And on that shore they still tell a story about that sea which is as old as the Panchatantra — because it IS from the Panchatantra. It goes like this.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"टिट्टिभ पक्षियों के एक जोड़े ने — छोटे, फुर्तीले समुद्री पक्षी जिनकी टाँगें घास के तिनकों जैसी पतली थीं — लहरों की पहुँच से ज़रा ऊपर, ठीक रेत पर घोंसला बनाने की सोची। समंदर उन्हें घोंसला बनाते देखता रहा, और उस वक़्त समंदर बड़े घमंड में था। \"नन्हे पक्षियो,\" वह फुसफुसाया, \"तुम इतने विशाल समंदर के चरणों में घोंसला बना रहे हो।\" \"हमें पता है,\" अपने काम में जुटे पक्षियों ने कहा। \"ज़रा कायदे से रहना!\"","lang":"hi","body":"A pair of tittibha birds — small, quick shore birds with legs like grass stalks — decided to nest right on the sand, above the tide line. The sea watched them build, and the sea was in one of its proud moods. \"Little birds,\" it murmured, \"you are nesting at the feet of the enormous sea.\" \"We know,\" said the birds, busy. \"Mind you behave yourself.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"समुद्र को अपनी पूरी ज़िंदगी में किसी ने तमीज़ से रहना नहीं सिखाया था। इसलिए एक रात, बस यह दिखाने के लिए कि 'विशाल' होना क्या होता है, वह चुपचाप उमड़ा, अपनी एक लंबी लहर रेत पर आगे बढ़ाई, और घोंसले में से दोनों अंडे उठा ले गया — नीचे अपने हरे अंधेरे में, यह देखने के लिए कि वे नन्हे पक्षी अब क्या कर लेंगे।","lang":"hi","body":"The sea had never in its life been told to behave. So one night, just to show what enormous meant, it swelled up quietly, reached one long wave up the sand, and took the two eggs out of the nest — down into its green dark, to see what the little birds would do about it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"दुख से भरी एक भयानक सुबह के बाद उन्होंने जो किया, वह था गुस्सा होना — ऐसा गुस्सा जो काम आए। उन दोनों ने पूरे क्षितिज की ओर देखा, दूर-दूर तक, और पूरे महासागर से ऐलान कर दिया: \"हमारे अंडे वापस कर दो। वरना हम तुम्हें खाली कर देंगे। एक-एक चोंच भर-भरकर, तुम्हारे आखिरी भीगे पत्थर तक।\"","lang":"hi","body":"What they did, after one terrible morning of grief, was get angry — the useful kind. The two of them looked out at the horizon, all of it, and made an announcement to the entire ocean: \"Give back our eggs. Or we will empty you. Beakful by beakful, down to your last wet stone.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"और वे लग गए। चोंच डुबोई, उड़कर चट्टानों तक गए, बूंद गिरा दी। फिर दूसरी बूंद के लिए वापस। पूरी सुबह। पूरा दिन। बूंद, दर बूंद, दर बूंद, उस पूरे हिंद महासागर के सामने — जो पूरे पंचतंत्र का सबसे मजेदार मज़ाक लगता है, जब तक कि आपको यह एहसास न हो कि आपकी हंसी रुक चुकी है।","lang":"hi","body":"And they began. Dip the beak, fly to the rocks, spit out the drop. Back for another. All morning. All day. Drop, by drop, by drop, against the entire Indian Ocean, which is the best joke in the whole Panchatantra until you notice you have stopped laughing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-5":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:5","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"बाकी पक्षी पहले तो हँसे, ज़ाहिर है। फिर उन्होंने थोड़ी देर देखा। फिर, एक-एक करके, उनकी हँसी बंद हो गई और वे भी चोंच डुबोने लगे — गौरैया, बगुले, गंगा-चिल्ली, कौवे, चोंचों से भरा पूरा तट, बूंद-दर-बूंद-दर-बूंद। जब कोई छोटा सा जीव रुकने से इनकार कर देता है, तो उसमें कोई ऐसी बात होती है जिसे देखकर बाकी लोगों के लिए आगे बढ़ जाना नामुमकिन हो जाता है।","lang":"hi","body":"The other birds laughed first, of course. Then they watched a while. Then, one by one, they stopped laughing and started dipping — sparrows, herons, gulls, crows, a whole coastline of beaks, drop by drop by drop. There is something about somebody small who will not stop that other somebodies find impossible to walk past.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h5","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-6":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:6","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"और यह खबर एक-एक पक्षी से होते हुए, खुद गरुड़ तक जा पहुँची — उड़ने वाले हर पक्षी के राजा, जिनके परिवार की वह नन्ही टिटहरी भी एक सदस्य थी। वे नीचे उतरे, अपने ऐसे पंखों के साथ जिन्होंने सूरज को भी धुंधला कर दिया — और ध्यान रहे, वे नन्हे पक्षियों की तरफ़ थे, वह सारी विशालता उन दो तटवर्ती पक्षियों के साथ थी — और उन्होंने समुद्र की तरफ़ देखा, और उससे बस एक बार कहा कि जो उसने लिया है, वह लौटा दे।","lang":"hi","body":"And the news of it rose, bird by bird, all the way up to Garuda himself — the king of every bird that flies, whose family the smallest sandpiper belongs to. Down he came, with wings that dimmed the sun — on their side, mind, all that hugeness on the side of two shore birds — and he looked at the sea, and asked it, once, to give back what it had taken.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h6","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-fk.tittibha-sea-7":{"src":"passage:hi:story:fk.tittibha-sea:7","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Birds Who Argued With the Sea","text":"और समुद्र ने — जिसमें सिर्फ़ घमंड था, जो दिल से बुरा नहीं था — उन दो नन्हे पक्षियों को देखा, उनके पीछे चोंचों से भरे पूरे तट को देखा, और ऊपर गरुड़ से घिरे पूरे आसमान को देखा, और समझ गया कि अकेले घमंड का बोझ उठाना बहुत भारी होता है। वह एक कोमल लहर पर अंडों को वापस ऊपर ले आया और उन्हें सही-सलामत घोंसले में रख दिया, उनके चारों ओर सफ़ेद झाग की एक माफ़ी के साथ।","lang":"hi","body":"And the sea — which had only ever been proud, not truly wicked — looked at the two little birds, and the coastline of beaks behind them, and the sky full of Garuda above, and understood that pride is a very heavy thing to carry alone. It brought the eggs back up on a soft wave and laid them in the nest, unbroken, with an apology of white foam around them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tittibha-sea.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tittibha-sea|h7","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-aryabhata-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-aryabhata:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Made Nothing Count","text":"\"ईसवी सन् 499 में, बिहार में आज के पटना के पास कुसुमपुर में, एक विद्वान ने गणित और खगोलशास्त्र की एक ऐसी किताब पूरी की जो पूरी तरह से पद्यों में लिखी गई थी, ताकि उसे याद रखकर अपने दिमाग में संजोया जा सके। हमें ठीक-ठीक पता है कि यह कब हुआ, और हम यह भी जानते हैं कि उनकी उम्र कितनी थी, क्योंकि उन्होंने खुद किताब के अंदर हमें बताया है: तेईस साल। इस किताब का नाम 'आर्यभटीय' है, और लोग तब से आज तक इसे पढ़ते आ रहे हैं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"In the year 499 CE, in Kusumapura — near today's Patna, in Bihar — a scholar finished a book of mathematics and astronomy written entirely in verse, so that it could be memorised and carried in people's heads. We know exactly when, and we know how old he was, because he tells us himself, inside the book: twenty-three. The book is called the Aryabhatiya, and people have been studying it ever since.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-aryabhata.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-aryabhata|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-aryabhata-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-aryabhata:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Made Nothing Count","text":"\"उनकी एक समस्या बहुत पुरानी थी: अनगिनत संकेतों में डूबे बिना आप बहुत बड़ी संख्याओं को कैसे लिख सकते हैं? उनकी किताब जिस उत्तर पर काम करती है, वह वही है जिसे आप बिना ध्यान दिए हर रोज़ इस्तेमाल करते हैं — कोई अंक कहाँ बैठता है, यही तय करता है कि उसका मान क्या है। 25 में 2 का मतलब बीस होता है। 205 में 2 का मतलब दो सौ होता है। जिससे एक अजीब सा सवाल सामने आता है: 205 में, बीच में काम कौन कर रहा है?\"","lang":"hi","body":"One of his problems was very old: how do you write enormous numbers without drowning in symbols? The answer his book works with is the one you use every day without noticing — WHERE a digit sits decides what it is worth. The 2 in 25 means twenty. The 2 in 205 means two hundred. Which forces a strange question: in 205, what is doing the work in the middle?","art":"art/story/sm/sci-aryabhata.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-aryabhata|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-aryabhata-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-aryabhata:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Made Nothing Count","text":"\"भारत में उस चिह्नित खालीपन को 'शून्य' कहा जाने लगा। गणित के इतिहासकार आर्यभट के संख्याओं के साथ काम करने के तरीके को एक ऐसी जगह बताते हैं जहाँ यह विचार पहले से ही अपनी पूरी ताकत दिखा रहा था — और यही वह विचार है, वह खाली जगह जो मायने रखती है, जिसका ज़िक्र लोग तब करते हैं जब वे कहते हैं कि भारत ने दुनिया को शून्य दिया। इसके लिए सबसे पहले वह छोटा गोल चिह्न किसने बनाया? कोई भी इसे साबित नहीं कर सकता। बड़े लोग आज भी शोध-पत्रिकाओं में इस बात पर खुशी-खुशी बहस करते हैं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"In India that marked emptiness came to be called shunya. Historians of mathematics point to Aryabhata's way of working with numbers as a place where the idea is already pulling its weight — and it is this idea, the empty place that counts, that people mean when they say India gave the world zero. Who first drew the little round symbol for it? Nobody can prove it. Grown-ups still argue about that one, happily, in journals.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-aryabhata.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-aryabhata|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-aryabhata-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-aryabhata:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Made Nothing Count","text":"\"और फिर यह किताब एक ऐसी बात कहती है जिस पर उस समय धरती पर शायद ही किसी ने विश्वास किया हो। आर्यभट लिखते हैं कि तारे हर रात हमारे चारों ओर चक्कर नहीं लगाते — बल्कि धरती खुद घूम रही है। जिस तरह चलती नाव में बैठा व्यक्ति नदी किनारे के पेड़ों को पीछे छूटते हुए देखता है, वे अपने पद्य में कहते हैं, ठीक उसी तरह हम, घूमती हुई धरती पर सवार होकर, तारों को पश्चिम की ओर बढ़ते देखते हैं। आप आज भी उस श्लोक को पढ़ सकते हैं। उन्होंने एक नाव से धरती के घूमने की पहेली सुलझा ली थी।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And then the book says something that almost nobody on Earth believed at the time. The stars, Aryabhata writes, do not wheel around us each night — the Earth itself is turning. Just as a person in a moving boat sees the trees on the riverbank drift backwards, he says in his verse, so we, riding the turning Earth, see the stars drift west. You can still read that verse today. He worked out the turning from a boat.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-aryabhata.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-aryabhata|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-aryabhata-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-aryabhata:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Man Who Made Nothing Count","text":"\"उनके बाद आने वाले ज़्यादातर खगोलशास्त्रियों ने इसे नकार दिया। घूमती हुई धरती! हमें तो ज़रूर इसका अहसास होता। कइयों ने उनकी किताब की नकल तो की लेकिन उस हिस्से को \"सुधार\" दिया, और लगभग एक हज़ार साल और पाठ्यपुस्तकों में थमी हुई धरती का ही राज रहा, जब तक कि दूसरे देशों के दूरबीनों ने आखिरकार उनकी ही बात साबित नहीं कर दी। समय से पहले सही होना एक अकेलापन भरा काम हो सकता है — जब तक बाकी सब वहाँ नहीं पहुँच जाते, तब तक सबूत ही आपका साथ निभाते हैं।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The astronomers who came after him mostly said no. A spinning Earth! Surely we would feel it. Many copied his book but \"corrected\" that part, and the still Earth stayed in charge of the textbooks for about a thousand years more, until telescopes in other countries settled it his way. Being right early can be a lonely business — the evidence keeps you company until everyone else arrives.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-aryabhata.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-aryabhata|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-jcbose-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-jcbose:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","text":"\"1890 के दशक में जगदीश चंद्र बोस कोलकाता के प्रेसीडेंसी कॉलेज में फ़िज़िक्स पढ़ाते थे। उनकी लैब नाम भर की लैब थी — एक छोटा-सा कमरा, और साज़ो-सामान के लिए न के बराबर पैसे। इसलिए उन्होंने वहीं के कारीगरों के साथ मिलकर अपने उपकरण खुद बनाए, और वे इतने शानदार निकले कि दुनिया में सबसे बेहतरीन साबित हुए। 1895 में उन्होंने उन्हीं में से एक यंत्र से कोलकाता के एक हॉल में दीवारों के पार अदृश्य तरंगें भेजीं और दूर रखी एक घंटी बजा दी — यानी रेडियो तरंगें, तब जब दुनिया के ज़्यादातर लोगों ने ऐसी किसी चीज़ का नाम भी नहीं सुना था।\"","lang":"hi","body":"Jagadish Chandra Bose taught physics at Presidency College in Kolkata in the 1890s, in a laboratory that was barely a laboratory — a small room, almost no money for equipment. So he built his own instruments, working with local metalworkers, and they turned out to be some of the finest instruments anywhere. In 1895 he used one set of them to send invisible waves through walls across a Kolkata hall, ringing a bell at a distance — radio waves, years before most of the world had heard of such a thing.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-jcbose-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-jcbose:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","text":"\"लेकिन एक सवाल उनका पीछा नहीं छोड़ रहा था, और वह पौधों के बारे में था। कुत्ता दौड़ता है, इंसान चिल्लाता है — पर पौधा, जो इन दोनों में से कुछ नहीं कर सकता, वो आपसे कुछ कैसे कहेगा? बोस का जवाब था: ऐसा तरीका बनाओ जिससे उसकी आवाज़ सुनी जा सके। उनके बनाए क्रेस्कोग्राफ़ ने पौधे के बढ़ने को हज़ारों गुना बड़ा करके दिखाया, ताकि जो बढ़त आँखों से कभी न दिखती, वह एक चलती हुई लकीर बन गई जिसे आप देख सकते थे — एक पौधा, आपकी आँखों के सामने बढ़ता हुआ।\"","lang":"hi","body":"But the question that would not let go of him was about plants. A dog runs, a person cries out — how would a plant, which can do neither, ever tell you anything? Bose's answer: build it a way to be heard. His crescograph magnified a plant's growth many thousands of times, so that growth too slow for any eye became a moving line you could watch — a plant, growing, live.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-jcbose-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-jcbose:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","text":"\"और उस लकीर में एक ख़बर छिपी थी। पौधे को ठंडा करो, तो लकीर धीमी पड़ जाती। उसे ज़ोर से छुओ या कोई ख़राब चीज़ पीने को दो, तो लकीर लड़खड़ाती और सहम जाती। पौधा सचमुच जवाब दे रहा था — वह यूँ ही चुपचाप नहीं बैठा था, बल्कि अपने शांत और धीमे अंदाज़ में अपनी दुनिया पर असर दिखा रहा था। बोस यूरोप की बड़ी-बड़ी विज्ञान सभाओं में गए और कोलकाता में बनाए अपने उपकरणों से यह सब करके दिखाया।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And the line had news in it. Chill the plant, and the line slowed. Touch it roughly, give it something nasty to drink, and the line stumbled and flinched. The plant was responding — not sitting there at all, but reacting to its world, in its own silent, slow-motion way. Bose travelled to the great scientific societies of Europe and showed them, with the instruments he had built in Kolkata.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-jcbose-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-jcbose:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","text":"\"और यहीं से यह कहानी एक बड़े फ़ैसले का मोड़ लेती है। उनके इस यंत्र — और उससे पहले उनके रेडियो तरंगों वाले उपकरणों — जैसी चीज़ों से ही उस ज़माने में लोग भारी दौलत बना रहे थे। कंपनियाँ जो भी सामने आता, उसी का पेटेंट करा रही थीं। पर बोस कोई अमीर आदमी नहीं थे।\"","lang":"hi","body":"And here is where the story turns into a decision. Instruments like his — and his radio-wave devices before them — were exactly the kind of thing fortunes were being built on. Companies were patenting everything in sight. He was not a rich man.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-jcbose-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-jcbose:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","text":"\"1917 में उन्होंने कोलकाता में बोस इंस्टीट्यूट खोला — जो एशिया के सबसे पहले रिसर्च संस्थानों में से एक था — और अपने सारे उपकरण, अपने तरीके और अपनी ज़िंदगी भर की कमाई आने वाले लोगों के लिए वहीं सौंप दी। वह संस्थान आज भी वहीं है, और आज भी काम कर रहा है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"In 1917 he opened the Bose Institute in Kolkata — one of the first research institutes in Asia — and gave his instruments, his methods and his life's work to it, for whoever came next. It is still there, still working.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-raman-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-raman:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · Why Is the Sea Blue?","text":"तमिलनाडु के तिरुचिरापल्ली में जन्मे चंद्रशेखर वेंकट रामन, सन 1921 में पानी के जहाज़ से लंदन से घर लौट रहे थे, और भूमध्य सागर गज़ब का नीला दिख रहा था। किताबों में इसका जवाब तैयार था: महान लॉर्ड रेले ने कहा था कि समुद्र में आसमान का अक्स दिखता है, और सब बस वही दोहराते रहे। रामन रेलिंग के पास खड़े रहे और देर तक पानी को देखते रहे। दोहराते रहने का मतलब यह तो नहीं कि बात की जाँच भी की गई हो।","lang":"hi","body":"Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman, born in Tiruchirappalli in Tamil Nadu, was sailing home from London in 1921, and the Mediterranean was outrageously blue. The books had an answer ready: the sea reflects the sky, said the great Lord Rayleigh, and everyone repeated it. Raman stood at the rail and looked at the water for a long time. Repeating is not checking.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-raman-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-raman:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · Why Is the Sea Blue?","text":"उनके सामान में एक छोटा-सा प्रकाश-उपकरण था — वे थे ही ऐसे मुसाफ़िर जिनके पास ऐसी चीज़ें होती हैं — और वह यंत्र झलकी हुई रोशनी को छानकर अलग कर सकता था। उन्होंने उसे समुद्र की तरफ़ घुमाया, ताकि आसमान का अक्स कटकर अलग हो जाए। अगर किताबें सही होतीं, तो वह नीला रंग भी उसके साथ ग़ायब हो जाना चाहिए था। पर नीला रंग तो वहीं रहा। समुद्र अपना रंग आसमान से उधार नहीं ले रहा था। पानी खुद सूरज की रोशनी को बिखेरकर अपना नीला रंग बना रहा था। जहाज़ के भारत पहुँचने तक, उन्होंने इस पर पूरा लेख लिख लिया था और उसे 'नेचर' पत्रिका को डाक से भेज भी दिया था।","lang":"hi","body":"He had a small optical device in his luggage — he was the kind of traveller who would — and it could filter out reflected light. He pointed it at the sea, cutting away the sky's reflection. If the books were right, the blue should vanish with it. The blue stayed. The sea was not borrowing its colour from the sky. The water itself was scattering sunlight and making its own blue. By the time the ship reached India he had already written this up and posted it to the journal Nature.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-raman-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-raman:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · Why Is the Sea Blue?","text":"रंग महज़ एक सच्चाई नहीं होता — वह तो पानी के भीतर से आया हुआ एक संदेश होता है। कोलकाता में अपनी प्रयोगशाला, 'इंडियन एसोसिएशन फ़ॉर द कल्टीवेशन ऑफ़ साइंस' में लौटकर, रामन और उनके साथियों ने, ख़ास तौर पर के. एस. कृष्णन ने, सात साल अलग-अलग तरल पदार्थों से रोशनी गुज़ारने और उससे निकलने वाले नतीजों को समझने में बिता दिए। बुनियादी सवाल यह था: जब रोशनी किसी चीज़ के आर-पार गुज़रती है, तो क्या वह चीज़ रोशनी पर अपनी उंगलियों के निशान छोड़ जाती है?","lang":"hi","body":"A colour is not just a fact — it is a message from inside the water. Back at his laboratory in Kolkata, the Indian Association for the Cultivation of Science, Raman and his colleagues, above all K. S. Krishnan, spent seven years shining light through liquids and reading what came out. The question underneath: when light passes through a substance, does the substance leave its fingerprints on the light?","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-raman-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-raman:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · Why Is the Sea Blue?","text":"28 फ़रवरी 1928 को उन्हें साफ़-साफ़ जवाब मिल गया: बिखरी हुई रोशनी का एक नन्हा-सा हिस्सा अपना रंग बदलकर बाहर निकलता है — और यह बदलाव इस बात पर निर्भर करता है कि रोशनी किन अणुओं से टकराई थी। इसे 'रामन प्रभाव' कहा जाता है, और इसका मतलब है कि चीज़ों को छुए बिना ही, सिर्फ़ रोशनी के ज़रिए यह पहचाना जा सकता है कि वे किस चीज़ से बनी हैं। आज दुनिया भर की प्रयोगशालाएँ इसका इस्तेमाल दवाइयों से लेकर पुरानी तस्वीरों और यहाँ तक कि मंगल ग्रह की सतह की जाँच के लिए करती हैं।","lang":"hi","body":"On 28 February 1928 they had it, unmistakably: a tiny fraction of the scattered light comes out with its colour shifted — and the shift depends on the molecules it met. It is called the Raman effect, and it means light can be used to identify what things are made of without touching them. Laboratories all over the world use it today, on everything from medicines to paintings to the surface of Mars.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-raman-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-raman:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · Why Is the Sea Blue?","text":"सन 1930 में रामन को भौतिकी का नोबेल पुरस्कार मिला — विज्ञान में नोबेल जीतने वाले वे एशिया के पहले व्यक्ति बने। उनकी जीवनी लिखने वाले बताते हैं कि उन्हें यह पुरस्कार मिलने का इतना पक्का भरोसा था कि एलान होने से महीनों पहले ही उन्होंने स्टॉकहोम के टिकट बुक करवा लिए थे। इसे या तो ज़बरदस्त आत्मविश्वास कह सकते हैं या फिर गज़ब की बेबाकी, और रामन के मामले में तो आम तौर पर ये दोनों ही बातें थीं।","lang":"hi","body":"In 1930 Raman won the Nobel Prize in Physics — the first person from Asia to win a science Nobel. His biographers tell that he was so sure it was coming, he booked his tickets to Stockholm months before the announcement. That is either tremendous confidence or tremendous cheek, and with Raman it was generally both.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-ramanujan-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-ramanujan:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","text":"तमिलनाडु के इरोड और कुंभकोणम के रहने वाले श्रीनिवास रामानुजन, मद्रास पोर्ट ट्रस्ट में एक क्लर्क के रूप में काम करते थे, जहाँ वे कम तनख्वाह पर दूसरों के माल की गिनती करते थे। वे कॉलेज में फेल हो गए थे — एक बार नहीं, दो बार — क्योंकि वे गणित के अलावा कुछ भी, बिल्कुल कुछ भी नहीं पढ़ते थे। उन्होंने अपने अनोखे तरीक़े से लिखे गणितीय नतीजों से एक के बाद एक कई कॉपियाँ भर दी थीं, ऐसा गणित जिसे उनके आस-पास कोई नहीं समझ सकता था, और दीये की रोशनी में वे स्लेट पर काम करते थे क्योंकि कागज़ ख़रीदने में पैसे लगते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"Srinivasa Ramanujan, from Erode and Kumbakonam in Tamil Nadu, worked as a clerk at the Madras Port Trust, counting other people's cargo for a small salary. He had failed out of college — twice — because he would study nothing, absolutely nothing, except mathematics. He filled notebook after notebook with results in his own strange notation, mathematics nobody around him could follow, and worked by lamplight on a slate because paper cost money.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-ramanujan-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-ramanujan:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","text":"जनवरी 1913 में उन्होंने एक बड़ी हिम्मत और थोड़ी बेबसी भरा काम किया: उन्होंने इंग्लैंड के सबसे मशहूर गणितज्ञों में से एक, जी. एच. हार्डी को ख़त लिखा — एक ऐसे इंसान को जिनसे वे कभी मिले नहीं थे, और एक ऐसे देश में जिसे उन्होंने कभी देखा नहीं था। उन्होंने बड़ी ईमानदारी से अपना परिचय एक ऐसे क्लर्क के रूप में दिया जिसके पास यूनिवर्सिटी की कोई डिग्री नहीं थी, और साथ में सौ से भी ज़्यादा प्रमेयों के पन्ने-के-पन्ने भेज दिए, बिना किसी सबूत के। कैम्ब्रिज के दो अन्य प्रोफ़ेसरों को भी उनसे ऐसे ख़त मिल चुके थे, और उन्होंने कोई जवाब नहीं दिया था।","lang":"hi","body":"In January 1913 he did a brave and slightly desperate thing: he wrote to G. H. Hardy, one of the most famous mathematicians in England — a man he had never met, in a country he had never seen. He introduced himself honestly as a clerk with no university degree, and enclosed page after page of theorems, more than a hundred of them, with no proofs. Two other Cambridge professors had already received such letters from him, and had not replied.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-ramanujan-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-ramanujan:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","text":"कुछ नतीजों को हार्डी पहचान गए। कुछ में बारीक ग़लतियाँ थीं। और कुछ ऐसे थे जैसा उन्होंने पहले कभी देखा ही नहीं था — हार्डी ने बाद में लिखा कि ऐसे प्रमेयों का सच होना तय था, क्योंकि किसी के पास भी इन्हें मन से गढ़ने की कल्पना नहीं हो सकती थी। उन्होंने इनके रचने वाले को कैम्ब्रिज बुलाने का इंतज़ाम किया, और पाँच साल तक उस क्लर्क और प्रोफ़ेसर ने मिलकर ऐसा गणित रचा जिसे लोग आज भी खंगाल रहे हैं।","lang":"hi","body":"Some results Hardy recognised. Some were subtly wrong. And some were like nothing he had ever seen — Hardy wrote afterwards that such theorems had to be true, because no one would have had the imagination to invent them. He arranged to bring their author to Cambridge, and for five years the clerk and the professor did mathematics together that people are still mining today.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-ramanujan-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-ramanujan:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","text":"उनके बारे में सबसे मशहूर क़िस्सा खुद हार्डी का है, और वे इसे सुनाना बहुत पसंद करते थे। रामानुजन बीमार पड़े थे; हार्डी उनसे मिलने पहुँचे, और कहा कि उनकी टैक्सी का नंबर, 1729, काफ़ी उबाऊ सा लगता है। \"नहीं,\" रामानुजन तुरंत बोले — \"यह तो बहुत ही दिलचस्प नंबर है: यह वह सबसे छोटी संख्या है जिसे दो अलग-अलग तरीक़ों से दो घनों के जोड़ के रूप में लिखा जा सकता है।\" अपनी बीमारी के बिस्तर से। बिना एक पल रुके। हार्डी ने कहा कि ऐसा लगता था मानो हर संख्या रामानुजन की अपनी ख़ास दोस्त हो।","lang":"hi","body":"The most famous story about them is Hardy's own, and he loved telling it. Ramanujan lay ill; Hardy visited, and remarked that his taxi's number, 1729, seemed rather a dull one. No, Ramanujan said at once — it is a very interesting number: it is the smallest number you can write as the sum of two cubes in two different ways. From his sickbed. Without a pause. Hardy said every number seemed to be Ramanujan's personal friend.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-ramanujan-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-ramanujan:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","text":"इंग्लैंड की ठंड और युद्ध के साल उन पर बहुत भारी पड़े, और वे अक्सर बीमार रहने लगे। 1919 में वे जहाज़ से भारत लौट आए, और अगले ही साल, महज़ बत्तीस साल की उम्र में उनका देहांत हो गया। लेकिन उन्होंने आख़िर तक काम किया, और जो कॉपियाँ वे पीछे छोड़ गए — जिनमें से एक दशकों बाद मिली — उनसे आज भी नया गणित निकल रहा है। गणितज्ञ उनका नाम उसी तरह लेते हैं जैसे संगीतकार मोज़ार्ट का नाम लेते हैं।","lang":"hi","body":"England's cold and the war years were hard on him, and he was often ill. He sailed home to India in 1919, and died the next year, at just thirty-two. But he worked to the very end, and the notebooks he left — including one found decades later — are still yielding new mathematics today. Mathematicians speak of him the way musicians speak of Mozart.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-janaki-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-janaki:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","text":"एडवलेथ कक्कट जानकी अम्माल का जन्म 1897 में केरल के तट पर बसे थालाश्शेरी में, बच्चों से भरे एक बड़े से घर में हुआ था, जहाँ उनके पिता एक बगीचा सँभालते थे और पक्षियों के बारे में लिखते थे। उन्हें बचपन में ही पेड़-पौधों से प्यार हो गया, और यह प्यार कभी छूटा नहीं — वह भी उस दौर में, जब किसी प्रयोगशाला में किसी भारतीय महिला को देखना ज़्यादातर प्रोफ़ेसरों के लिए बिल्कुल अनसुनी बात थी। फिर भी वे आगे बढ़ती रहीं: पहले मद्रास में कॉलेज, और फिर दुनिया के उस पार, अमेरिका के मिशिगन के लिए एक छात्रवृत्ति।","lang":"hi","body":"Edavaleth Kakkat Janaki Ammal was born in 1897 in Thalassery, on the Kerala coast, into a big house full of children where her father kept a garden and wrote about birds. She fell in love with plants early, and refused to fall out of it — at a time when an Indian woman in a laboratory was a sight most professors had simply never seen. She kept going anyway: college in Madras, then a scholarship across the world to Michigan, in America.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-janaki-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-janaki:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","text":"वे डॉक्टर जानकी अम्माल बनकर घर लौटीं — वनस्पति विज्ञान में डॉक्टरेट पाने वाली पहली भारतीय महिला के रूप में याद की जाने वाली। उनका विज्ञान था साइटोजेनेटिक्स: यानी हर जीवित कोशिका के भीतर धागों जैसी नन्हीं किताबों, क्रोमोसोम को गिनना और समझना, जो तय करते हैं कि कोई पौधा क्या बन सकता है। क्रोमोसोम गिनना सुनने में बड़ा मामूली काम लगता है। मगर असल में यह एक मास्टर चाबी है — यह बताती है कि किन पौधों का आपस में मेल कराया जा सकता है, और उनसे पैदा होने वाले नए पौधे कैसे बनेंगे।","lang":"hi","body":"She came home Dr Janaki Ammal — remembered as the first Indian woman to earn a doctorate in botany. Her science was cytogenetics: counting and reading chromosomes, the tiny thread-libraries inside every living cell that decide what a plant can be. Counting chromosomes sounds humble. It is actually a master key — it tells you which plants can cross with which, and what their children might become.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-janaki-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-janaki:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","text":"और भारत के पास एक ऐसी समस्या थी, जिसके लिए इस मास्टर चाबी की ज़रूरत थी। कोयंबटूर के गन्ना प्रजनन संस्थान में उनका सामना इसी से हुआ: भारत के अपने गन्ने मज़बूत थे — वे भारत के सूखे और मिट्टी को झेल सकते थे — मगर उनमें मिठास कम थी, इसलिए रोपाई के लिए सबसे मीठा गन्ना विदेश से, जावा से आता था। मज़बूत लेकिन मीठा नहीं; मीठा लेकिन मज़बूत नहीं।","lang":"hi","body":"And India had a problem worth a master key. At the Sugarcane Breeding Institute in Coimbatore she faced it: India's own canes were tough — they could take India's droughts and soils — but thin in sweetness, so the sweetest planting cane came from Java, abroad. Tough but not sweet; sweet but not tough.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-janaki-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-janaki:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","text":"इस काम से भारत को ऐसे गन्ने मिले जिनमें दोनों खूबियाँ थीं: भारतीय खेतों के लिए मीठे गन्ने, ताकि मिठास को बाहर से मँगवाना न पड़े। फिर वे अपनी यह मास्टर चाबी लेकर विदेश गईं: इंग्लैंड के एक बड़े जेनेटिक्स संस्थान में उन्होंने और वैज्ञानिक सी. डी. डार्लिंगटन ने 'क्रोमोसोम एटलस ऑफ़ कल्टीवेटेड प्लांट्स' लिखी — दुनिया की फ़सलों के क्रोमोसोम की ऐसी जनगणना, जिसे शोधकर्ताओं ने दशकों तक इस्तेमाल किया। विज़ली में रॉयल हॉर्टिकल्चरल सोसाइटी के बगीचे में, जहाँ उन्होंने मैग्नोलिया पर काम किया था, उनके हाथों उगाया एक मैग्नोलिया आज भी खिलता है — और उसका नाम मैग्नोलिया कोबस 'जानकी अम्माल' है।","lang":"hi","body":"The work helped give India canes that were both: sweet canes for Indian fields, so the sweetness no longer had to be imported. Then she took her master key abroad: at a great genetics institute in England she and the scientist C. D. Darlington wrote the Chromosome Atlas of Cultivated Plants — a census of the chromosomes of the world's crops that researchers used for decades. At the Royal Horticultural Society's garden at Wisley, where she worked on magnolias, a magnolia she raised still flowers — and it is named Magnolia kobus 'Janaki Ammal'.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-janaki-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-janaki:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","text":"भारत ने उन्हें बॉटनिकल सर्वे ऑफ़ इंडिया को नए सिरे से संगठित करने के लिए वापस बुलाया — जो इस धरती के हर पौधे का देश-स्तरीय बड़ा हिसाब-किताब था। और अस्सी की उम्र में उन्होंने एक और लड़ाई लड़ी: वैज्ञानिक केरल के एक प्राचीन वर्षावन, साइलेंट वैली को बाँध बनाकर डूबने से बचाने की मुहिम चला रहे थे। वे भी उनके साथ जुड़ गईं, उस पर अध्ययन किया, और उसके पक्ष में आवाज़ उठाई। वह जंगल आज भी खड़ा है, पक्षियों के गीतों से गूँजता एक राष्ट्रीय उद्यान।","lang":"hi","body":"India asked her back, to reorganise the Botanical Survey of India — the great national accounting of every plant the land holds. And in her eighties she took up one more fight: scientists were campaigning to save Silent Valley, an ancient rainforest in Kerala, from being dammed and drowned. She joined them, studied it, spoke for it. The forest stands today, a national park, full of birdsong.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-bhabha-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-bhabha:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · A Home for Science","text":"1909 में बंबई में जन्मे होमी जहाँगीर भाभा बीस-बाईस साल की उम्र में ही कैम्ब्रिज के एक जाने-माने भौतिक वैज्ञानिक बन चुके थे, जो कॉस्मिक किरणों पर काम कर रहे थे — यानी वे कण जो अंतरिक्ष से हम पर बरसते हैं। 1939 में वे छुट्टियों में भारत अपने घर आए। तभी दूसरा विश्व युद्ध छिड़ गया, समुद्री रास्ते बंद हो गए, और वह छोटी सी छुट्टी चुपके से उनकी बाकी ज़िंदगी बन गई।","lang":"hi","body":"Homi Jehangir Bhabha, born in Bombay in 1909, was by his twenties a physicist with a growing name at Cambridge, working on cosmic rays — particles that fall on us from space. In 1939 he came home to India on holiday. Then the Second World War broke out, the sea routes closed, and the holiday quietly became the rest of his life.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-bhabha-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-bhabha:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · A Home for Science","text":"उन्होंने बैंगलोर के 'इंडियन इंस्टीट्यूट ऑफ साइंस' में सी. वी. रमन के साथ काम संभाला, और उस सच्चाई को गहराई से समझा जिसे वे हमेशा से जानते थे: कोई भी युवा भारतीय जो दुनिया की सबसे बेहतरीन भौतिकी पढ़ना या करना चाहता था, उसे भारत छोड़ना ही पड़ता था। इसलिए नहीं कि यहाँ प्रतिभा की कमी थी — बल्कि इसलिए कि उनके पास कोई ठिकाना नहीं था। न इमारतें, न उपकरण, न तनख्वाह, और न ही गलियारे में साथ काम करने वाले साथी। वे समझ गए थे कि विज्ञान किसी शेड में बैठा अकेला प्रतिभाशाली व्यक्ति नहीं होता। विज्ञान तो एक पूरा भरा-पूरा घर होता है, और भारत को कभी ऐसा घर मिला ही नहीं था।","lang":"hi","body":"He took a post at the Indian Institute of Science in Bangalore, in C. V. Raman's orbit, and looked hard at a fact he had always known: a young Indian who wanted to do the world's best physics had to leave India to do it. Not for lack of minds — for lack of a home. No buildings, no instruments, no salaries, no colleagues down the corridor. Science, he understood, is not a lone genius in a shed. It is a household, and India had never been given one.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-bhabha-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-bhabha:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · A Home for Science","text":"इसलिए 1944 में — भारत के आज़ाद होने से भी पहले — उन्होंने बैठकर 'सर दोराबजी टाटा ट्रस्ट' को एक चिट्ठी लिखी, जिसमें बुनियादी शोध के लिए एक संस्थान बनाने का प्रस्ताव रखा। वह चिट्ठी आज भी मौजूद है, और उसका एक वाक्य बहुत मशहूर हो गया: उन्होंने लिखा था कि जब बिजली बनाने के लिए परमाणु ऊर्जा का इस्तेमाल होने लगेगा, तब भारत को विशेषज्ञों के लिए विदेशों की तरफ़ नहीं देखना पड़ेगा, बल्कि वे यहीं तैयार मिलेंगे। जब उन्होंने यह लिखा था, तब पूरी दुनिया में कहीं भी कोई परमाणु ऊर्जा केंद्र नहीं था। वे एक ऐसे भविष्य की तैयारी कर रहे थे जो सिर्फ़ उन्हें दिखाई दे रहा था और जिसे अभी तक किसी ने बनाया नहीं था।","lang":"hi","body":"So in 1944 — before India was even independent — he sat down and wrote a letter to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, proposing an institute for fundamental research. The letter survives, and one sentence in it has become famous: when nuclear energy was successfully used for power, he wrote, India would not have to look abroad for its experts, but would find them ready at hand. Nuclear power stations existed nowhere on Earth when he wrote that. He was budgeting for a future he could see and nobody had built.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-bhabha-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-bhabha:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · A Home for Science","text":"ट्रस्ट ने हाँ कह दी। 1945 में 'टाटा इंस्टीट्यूट ऑफ फंडामेंटल रिसर्च' खुला और बंबई में समंदर के किनारे अपने नए घर में फला-फूला — और भाभा, जो खुद एक बेहद उम्दा चित्रकार थे, उन्होंने इसकी एक-एक बारीकी पर पूरा ध्यान दिया: बगीचे, इमारत की बनावट, और दीवारों पर लगाई गई आधुनिक पेंटिंग्स, यहाँ तक कि उस संस्थान में भारत का एक अनूठा कला-संग्रह तैयार हो गया। लोगों ने पूछा कि भौतिकी के संस्थान को खूबसूरती की क्या ज़रूरत है? फिर भी उन्होंने उसे खूबसूरत बनाया; उनका मानना था कि बेहतरीन दिमाग बदसूरत कमरों में नहीं पनपते।","lang":"hi","body":"The Trust said yes. The Tata Institute of Fundamental Research opened in 1945 and grew into its home by the sea in Bombay — and Bhabha, who was a genuinely fine painter himself, fussed over every detail of it: the gardens, the architecture, the modern paintings he hung on its walls, until the institute held one of India's great art collections. People asked why a physics institute needed beauty. He built it beautiful anyway; he did not believe fine minds grow in ugly rooms.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-bhabha-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-bhabha:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · A Home for Science","text":"और वह मशहूर वाक्य बिलकुल सही वक्त पर सच साबित हुआ। जब भारत ने अपना परमाणु कार्यक्रम बनाना शुरू किया, तो उसे विदेशों की तरफ़ नहीं देखना पड़ा — वैज्ञानिक यहीं तैयार खड़े थे, जिन्हें भाभा के बनाए उसी घर में तराशा गया था। टीआईएफआर आज भी समंदर के किनारे शान से खड़ा है, अपने सुंदर बगीचों और पेंटिंग्स के साथ, दुनिया के सबसे ऊँचे स्तर पर गणित और भौतिकी के नए रास्ते तलाश रहा है।","lang":"hi","body":"And the famous sentence came true on schedule. When India came to build its atomic programme, it did not have to look abroad — the scientists were ready at hand, trained in the house that Bhabha built. TIFR stands by the sea today, still doing mathematics and physics at the world's edge, gardens and paintings and all.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-sarabhai-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-sarabhai:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Rocket in the Church","text":"विक्रम साराभाई का जन्म 1919 में गुजरात के अहमदाबाद के एक बड़े मिल-मालिक परिवार में हुआ था, और वे चाहते तो आराम से ज़िंदगी भर कारखाने चला सकते थे। मगर उन्होंने कॉस्मिक किरणों की पढ़ाई की, और 1947 में — भारत की आज़ादी के ठीक ग्यारह दिन बाद, सिर्फ़ अट्ठाईस साल की उम्र में — उन्होंने अहमदाबाद में एक भौतिकी प्रयोगशाला शुरू की। वे नए-नए संस्थान ऐसे बनाते थे जैसे कुछ लोग डाक टिकट जमा करते हैं: वे बस रुक ही नहीं पाते थे।","lang":"hi","body":"Vikram Sarabhai was born in 1919 into a great mill-owning family of Ahmedabad, in Gujarat, and could have spent his life comfortably running factories. Instead he studied cosmic rays, and in 1947 — eleven days into India's independence, aged twenty-eight — he founded a physics laboratory in Ahmedabad. He was a builder of institutions the way some people are collectors of stamps: he simply could not stop.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-sarabhai-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-sarabhai:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Rocket in the Church","text":"1962 में भारत ने उनसे एक ऐसी चीज़ शुरू करने को कहा जो सुनने में सचमुच बेतुकी लगती थी: एक अंतरिक्ष कार्यक्रम, वह भी एक ऐसे देश में जहाँ ज़्यादातर गाँवों में बिजली तक नहीं थी। कई समझदार लोगों ने यह बात साफ़-साफ़ कही भी।","lang":"hi","body":"In 1962 India asked him to start something that sounded, frankly, absurd: a space programme, in a country where most villages had no electricity. Plenty of sensible people said so out loud.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-sarabhai-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-sarabhai:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Rocket in the Church","text":"पहले रॉकेट की उड़ान के लिए उनके वैज्ञानिकों को चुंबकीय भूमध्य रेखा के पास एक ऐसी जगह चाहिए थी, जहाँ आसमान की ऊपरी हवा का अध्ययन सबसे दिलचस्प होता है। नक्शे में थुंबा का नाम सामने आया — केरल में तिरुवनंतपुरम के पास मछुआरों का एक शांत गाँव। और चुनी गई ज़मीन के बिल्कुल बीच में गाँव का एक चर्च खड़ा था, सेंट मैरी मैग्डलीन। बिशप और वहाँ के लोगों ने आपस में बातचीत की — और देश के विज्ञान के लिए अपना चर्च दे दिया, और अपनी प्रार्थनाओं के लिए पास ही एक नया चर्च बना लिया।","lang":"hi","body":"For the first launch site his scientists needed a spot close to the magnetic equator, where the upper air is at its most interesting. The map pointed at Thumba — a quiet fishing village near Thiruvananthapuram in Kerala. And in the middle of the chosen ground stood the village's church, St Mary Magdalene's. The bishop and the parish talked it over — and gave their church to the nation's science, moving their prayers to a new church built nearby.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-sarabhai-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-sarabhai:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Rocket in the Church","text":"तो इस तरह भारत का अंतरिक्ष कार्यक्रम एक चर्च में आ गया। जहाँ वेदी थी, वहाँ प्रयोगशाला बन गई; बिशप का घर दफ़्तर बन गया; और चूँकि आने-जाने के साधन न के बराबर थे, इसलिए रॉकेट के पुर्जे आख़िरी रास्ते तक साइकिल और बैलगाड़ी से पहुँचाए गए — और उनकी तस्वीरें आज भी इसरो के संग्रह में मौजूद हैं, जो भारतीय विज्ञान की सबसे प्यारी तस्वीरों में गिनी जाती हैं। 21 नवंबर 1963 को, पहला साउंडिंग रॉकेट थुंबा से शाम के आसमान में उड़ चला। सच तो यह है: वह पहला रॉकेट विदेश के दोस्तों की मदद से अमेरिका से उधार लिया गया था — शुरुआत में कुछ उधार लेने की पूरी छूट होती है।","lang":"hi","body":"So India's space programme moved into a church. The altar end became a laboratory; the bishop's house became the office; and because there was hardly any transport, rocket parts travelled the last stretch by bicycle and by bullock cart — and the photographs of that survive in ISRO's archives, some of the best-loved pictures in Indian science. On 21 November 1963, the first sounding rocket rose from Thumba into the evening sky. Honesty note: that first rocket was borrowed from America, with help from friends abroad — a beginning is allowed to borrow.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-sarabhai-4":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-sarabhai:4","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Rocket in the Church","text":"उसी चर्च से निकला इसरो, यानी भारतीय अंतरिक्ष अनुसंधान संगठन — वही संगठन जिसने आगे चलकर हर मानसून पर नज़र रखने वाले उपग्रह छोड़े, वादे के मुताबिक गाँव-गाँव के स्कूलों तक टेलीविज़न पहुँचाया, और आगे चलकर चाँद और मंगल तक अपने अंतरिक्ष यान भेजे। साराभाई यह सब देखने के लिए जीवित नहीं रहे; 1971 में, सिर्फ़ बावन साल की उम्र में उनका देहांत हो गया। मगर जिस कार्यक्रम की नींव उन्होंने रखी थी, उसने उनका वादा पूरा कर दिखाया।","lang":"hi","body":"From that church grew ISRO, the Indian Space Research Organisation — the organisation that went on to put satellites over every monsoon, television into village schools exactly as promised, and, in time, spacecraft around the Moon and Mars. Sarabhai did not live to see most of it; he died in 1971, at just fifty-two. The programme he planted kept his promise for him.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-sarabhai.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-sarabhai|h4","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-swaminathan-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-swaminathan:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","text":"साल 1925 में तमिलनाडु के कुंभकोणम में जन्मे मॉन्कोम्बु सांबशिवन स्वामीनाथन 1943 में एक छात्र थे, जब बंगाल में भयानक अकाल पड़ा और सड़कों पर इतने लोग मरे कि उनकी गिनती लिखना भी मुश्किल है। उन्होंने बाद में हमेशा यही कहा कि इसी बात ने उनका फैसला तय कर दिया: वे डॉक्टर बनने जा रहे थे, लेकिन उसकी जगह उन्होंने अनाज उगाने के विज्ञान को चुन लिया। वे कहते थे कि वे एक ऐसा भारत चाहते थे जहाँ कोई भूखा न रहे।","lang":"hi","body":"Monkombu Sambasivan Swaminathan, born in Kumbakonam in Tamil Nadu in 1925, was a student in 1943 when famine struck Bengal and people died in the streets in numbers that are hard to write down. He said ever afterwards that it was this that decided him: he had been headed for medicine, and he turned instead to the science of growing food. He wanted, he said, a hungry-proof India.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-swaminathan-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-swaminathan:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","text":"1960 के दशक तक भारत जहाज़ भर-भरकर गेहूँ मँगवा रहा था और गोदियों से उतरते ही उसे खाया जा रहा था — लोग इसे 'जहाज़ से मुँह तक की ज़िंदगी' कहते थे। पर एक उलझन भरी मुश्किल थी: भारत के गेहूँ को खूब पानी और खाद दो तो वह लंबा और ऊपर से भारी हो जाता था — और फिर हवा चलते ही गिर पड़ता था और कीचड़ में सड़ जाता था। उसे अच्छी खुराक दो, और वह ज़मीन पर लोट जाता था।","lang":"hi","body":"By the 1960s India was importing wheat by the shipload and eating it almost off the docks — people called it living ship-to-mouth. Here was the maddening trap: give Indian wheat plenty of water and fertiliser and it grew tall, top-heavy — and then fell flat in the wind and rotted in the mud. Feed it well and you flattened it.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-swaminathan-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-swaminathan:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","text":"स्वामीनाथन और उनके साथियों ने मैक्सिको में नॉर्मन बोरलॉग द्वारा तैयार किए गए छोटे कद के गेहूँ की किस्में मँगवाईं — उन्होंने 1963 में बोरलॉग को भारत आमंत्रित किया — और भारतीय खेतों और भारतीय रोटियों के हिसाब से उनका संकरण और परीक्षण किया। किसानों ने, खास तौर पर पंजाब में, इस नए बीज को हाथों-हाथ लिया। 1968 में गेहूँ की ऐसी बंपर फसल हुई जो पहले कभी नहीं देखी गई थी, यहाँ तक कि कई जगह अनाज रखने की जगह ही नहीं बची — उस साल के इतिहास में दर्ज है कि स्कूल बंद कर दिए गए ताकि कमरों में गेहूँ भरा जा सके। लोगों ने इसे हरित क्रांति कहा, और बाहर से आने वाले जहाज़ बंद हो गए।","lang":"hi","body":"Swaminathan and his colleagues brought in the dwarf wheats bred by Norman Borlaug in Mexico — he invited Borlaug to India in 1963 — and crossed and trialled them for Indian fields and Indian rotis. Farmers, above all in Punjab, took the new seed and ran with it. In 1968 the wheat harvest came in so far beyond anything before it that in places there was nowhere to put the grain — the histories of that year record schools being closed so the classrooms could be used to store wheat. They called it the Green Revolution, and the ships stopped.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-swaminathan-3":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-swaminathan:3","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","text":"और एक सच्ची बात, क्योंकि इस कहानी में यह बताना ज़रूरी है: इस नई खेती ने बहुत कुछ सोखा — खूब सारा पानी, खूब सारी खाद — और वक्त के साथ ज़मीन ने अपना हिसाब माँगना शुरू कर दिया, थकी हुई मिट्टी और गिरते हुए कुओं के रूप में। स्वामीनाथन जी ने खुद यह बात मानी, और अपने बाद के दशक उस काम में लगाए जिसे वे सदाबहार क्रांति कहते थे — यह उनका अपना शब्द था — जिसका मतलब था ऐसी भरपूर फसलें जो धरती को थकाए बिना मिलती रहें। लोगों का पेट भरना कोई एक बार की समस्या नहीं निकली, बल्कि एक ऐसा सवाल निकली जिसे लगातार सुलझाते रहना पड़ता है।","lang":"hi","body":"And one honest sentence, because this story has earned it: the new farming drank deep — heavy water, heavy fertiliser — and in time the land began presenting its bills, in tired soils and falling wells. Swaminathan said so himself, and spent his later decades on what he called an evergreen revolution — his own phrase — meaning harvests that stay high without wearing out the earth that gives them. Feeding people turned out to be not one problem but a problem you must keep solving.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan|h3","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-salimali-0":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-salimali:0","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fall of a Sparrow","text":"सालिम अली 1900 के आसपास बंबई में पले-बढ़े, वे अनाथ थे और मामा-चाचाओं और भाई-बहनों से भरे एक बड़े, प्यार भरे घर में बड़े हुए। उस ज़माने के बहुत से लड़कों की तरह उनके पास भी एक एयरगन थी जिससे वे गौरैयों का शिकार करते थे। एक दिन उन्होंने अपनी गिराई हुई एक गौरैया उठाई और ठिठक गए। उसके गले पर पीले रंग का एक निशान था। हर लड़का जानता था कि गौरैया का गला पीला नहीं होता। तो फिर उनके हाथ में यह कौन-सा परिंदा था?","lang":"hi","body":"Salim Ali grew up in Bombay around 1900, an orphan raised in a big, warm houseful of uncles and cousins, and like a lot of boys then he had an airgun and used it on sparrows. One day he picked up a sparrow he had shot and stopped. On its throat was a patch of yellow. Sparrows, every boy knew, do not have yellow throats. So what exactly was lying in his hand?","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-salimali|h0","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-salimali-1":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-salimali:1","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fall of a Sparrow","text":"\"वह चाहता तो कंधे उचकाकर बात टाल देता। इसके बजाय वह उस चिड़िया को अपने चाचा के पास ले गया, और चाचा ने भी एक व्यस्त इंसान होने के बावजूद बड़ी समझदारी दिखाई: उन्होंने एक चिट्ठी लिखकर उस लड़के को 'बॉम्बे नेचुरल हिस्ट्री सोसाइटी' भेज दिया। वहाँ डब्ल्यू. एस. मिलार्ड नाम के एक भले अंग्रेज़ ने हाथ में मरी हुई गौरैया लिए उस छोटे से बच्चे का मज़ाक नहीं उड़ाया। उन्होंने जतन से रखी परिंदों की खालों वाली एक के बाद एक कई दराज़ें खोलीं, जब तक कि बिल्कुल वैसी ही चिड़िया नहीं मिल गई: एक पीली गर्दन वाली गौरैया। फिर वे और दराज़ें खोलते गए — और उस लड़के को ऐसे-ऐसे भरे हुए पक्षी दिखाए जिनकी उसने कभी कल्पना भी नहीं की थी, और उसे पढ़ने को किताबें भी दीं। सालिम अली ज़िंदगी भर यह किस्सा सुनाते रहे; उनकी अपनी आत्मकथा की शुरुआत भी यहीं से होती है।\"","lang":"hi","body":"He could have shrugged. Instead he carried the bird to his uncle, who did an unusually wise thing for a busy uncle: he sent the boy with a note to the Bombay Natural History Society. There, a courteous Englishman named W. S. Millard did not laugh at a small boy with a dead sparrow. He opened drawer after drawer of carefully kept bird skins until they found the match: a yellow-throated sparrow. Then he kept opening drawers — and showed the boy stuffed birds beyond anything he had imagined, and lent him books. Salim Ali told this story all his life; it is the opening of his own memoir.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-salimali|h1","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]},"hp-sci-salimali-2":{"src":"passage:hi:story:sci-salimali:2","title":"Read it in Hindi · The Fall of a Sparrow","text":"\"जिस लड़के ने कभी परिंदों पर निशाना साधा था, वही बड़ा होकर उनकी गिनती करने वाला इंसान बन गया। दशकों तक सालिम अली पक्षियों का सर्वेक्षण करते हुए पूरे भारत में घूमे — एक-एक रियासत, एक-एक जंगल, दलदल और पहाड़ नापते हुए — अपनी कॉपियाँ, धैर्य और बेहद कम पैसों के साथ। उन्होंने पहली बार यह पता लगाया कि भारत में कौन-सी चिड़िया ठीक कहाँ रहती है। फिर उन्होंने यह सब एक ऐसी किताब में समेट दिया जिसे आम लोग भी खरीद सकें, 'द बुक ऑफ़ इंडियन बर्ड्स', और पूरे देश को उनके अपने पड़ोसियों के नाम सिखा दिए।\"","lang":"hi","body":"The boy who had shot birds became the man who counted them. For decade after decade Salim Ali walked India doing bird surveys — princely state by princely state, forest by marsh by mountain — with notebooks, patience and famously little money, finding out for the first time exactly which birds lived where in India. Then he put it in a book ordinary people could afford, The Book of Indian Birds, and taught a whole country the names of its own neighbours.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-salimali|h2","cta":"Read it in Hindi","why":"A story you can read in Hindi","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["On the path","Padhna — Reading"]]}});
